        <p>Notes by " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warringto a's cup-tie career this sea,n may rsibly to be abruptly terminated next week. The k of the draw hos a lot to do with these matters, and as it is Warrington's fate to visit Wigan in the second round their prospects cannot be considered bright. But there are always buts ! Wigan are not great as cup tie exponenta. and in the Lancashire Cup were beaten at horns by Widnes. What Wein, have I lone it is open to Warrington to emulate. If they only succeed in making a draw with the Winaners they will have done enough to give unbounded delight to their supporters.</p>
        <p>The draw for the second round was made in Manchester on Tuesday, and remitted as follows: 01.INIAM v, WAKEFIELD TRINITY. Referee, F Renton (lionslet); touch-judges, J Winstanley (Warrington) and 0 Richardson iswinton).</p>
        <p>BARROW V. SALFORD. F. II Smirk (Mew; F Hays. (Leigh) mild W Cosa (Preston).</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS V. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>.1 H Whiteley (Wakefield) ; II Lowe (Broughton) and H Cralreharr (Salford .</p>
        <p>HALIFAX v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>A Hestford (Brocghton); H Schaeld (Leeds) end H E Roebuck (Huddersfield).</p>
        <p>ROCHDALE HORNETS v. WIDNES. fl Ennion (Wigan); T Longden (Swinton) and A Lao (Leigh).</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON R. V. DEWSBURY.</p>
        <p>o Dickenson (Halifax); D Graham tOldham) and E Ilan.ford (St.. Helens).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BATLEY. W W Taylor (Hum let) ; E Benuell (Livespool) andJ McGowan Broughton). WIGAN N. WARRINGTON. W MoCutheon )01dbarn); W Johnson (Rochdale) and V Ratcliffe (Swinton). Gameo to he played on the ground of the first:named club on March 4th.</p>
        <p>What about Coventry ! Well, Warrington won there right enough and qualified for the second round by the substantial score of 18 points to 10 points. They had not quite such an easy journey as the margin of points would indicate, however. Coventry are a smart side, and with luck during the next season ! they should prove the masters of most visitoro to their aneient city. There is plenty of pace and cleverness in the team, but the forwards lack weight, and the defensive measures are not quite equal to the demands of Northern Fniou football. For a new team, how- ever, the Coventry men have made a verygood impres- sion, and I hope they will continue to get ample support from the rapidly growing population. There are already rumours that the Leicester anti other Midland clubs may follow suit is the near future.</p>
        <p>The two train-loads of Warrington entliusiasta who made the long journey on Naturday had a very enjoy- able noting. The weather continued fine and dry, and the L. N.W. Railway Company made amends for a dkastrous trip to Leeds a few years back, by running Coventry right on time. The specials were two of the beat organized trains which have ever been at the se. vice of IVarrington football excur- sionists. There was over an hour to spare in the City before the match, and some of the boys made a evreful seal ch for Lady tiodiva, but only found Peep- ing Toni.</p>
        <p>On &amp;nivel at the Butts ground one found that it was the troth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth, to say that the Coventry Club has one of the finest enclosures in the Union. The turf is green enough and level enough for cricket surrounihng it ins large cycle track, and on three sides there ere covered mends. I should ,think 25,1.100 people could be accommodated there with ease. On Saturday, unfortunately, owing to two other football engage- ments in the city, there wait barely a fifth of that number and the Warrington section mad. enough raw for the whole lot.</p>
        <p>A ivmerfill wind prevailed—blowing nearly straight from goal to goal and Coventry had the good fortune to win the toss. Warrington settled down therefore to dour defence. It was little use punting against the gale, and no joke running against it, so that the bulk of the play was at oue end. Coventry made little impression for some time, Warrington kept the hall close in the scrums, and far behind was Tilley who fielded every lofty punt like no artist, and ran till lie was tackled. Then the .me performance would be repeated. Eventlially the honie team gained confidence, and some sman passing gave ViToodhurn a chance, which this promising young player made full use of. He dished past Fish. punted over Tilley's head, and scored after running from his own half. How did the Rangers come to part with such a player? He is streets ahead of any of their present backs !</p>
        <p>With a margin against them, the Warrington men changed their tactics. Defence and defiance were not enough, am! a move was made to Coventry's quarters. Here the ball was handled to some effect, and both wings came very near to penetrating the home defence. Eventually Creevey did the trick in clever style. He received from Lloyd and then set off for Fish's wing. Near the line he gave Fish the dummy and tricked the Coventry men at the same time. It was a smart try and a tribute to the St Helena man's resourcefulness. Coventry made great efforts to ;pin the lead again, but the defence of the Warrington backs WAN impregnable.</p>
        <p>On resuming, after the change of ends, we con- cluded it was only a .mestion of how many ? Skelhorn strengthened the impression by scoring smartly under the posts in the first tso minutes awl Thomas kicked • goal. Poised)ly the Warrington men thought the match was as good as over. Anyhow the forwards died away, and Coventry began to press. Woodburn kicked a fine goal from a penalty, awl then the fun commenced. The homesters played like a great team for a quarter et an hour. end the Warrington defence looked very cheap when O'Malley completed a dashing dribble under the bar, awl Woodburn gave his vide the lead by placing a goal. This was alirmt threequarter time, and the locals were play- ing so well that there were serious doubts as to Warrington's ability m pull off the match. For- tunately we were soon reassured. Creevey flashed through the home hacks and established a footing in the Coventry quarter. Here Lloyd tricked his way through and the ball going to Fish, the burly one was over the line in a trice, and Thomas kicked a great goal from the touch line. Coventry collapsed like a punctured tyre after this, and Skelhorn proved the value of fast following up by getting another try under the posts, from which Thomas did the necessary conversion. There was only one team in it to the close, and Creevey would have retired again but for a damaged ankle which prevented him from getting up speed.</p>
        <p>On the whole Warrington's display was not a gseat one. The forwards scrummaged poorly until the last quarter, when they showed their power unmistakably. It a..eins obvious that they were only working at half pressure op to this point. ('money was the best back neither team, and showed great cleverness in break- ing away front the acnun and handling the ball. Lloyd also did well, though neither he nor Creevey tackled with any resolution, and Brooks's vigour was much missed. Dickenson was the best of the three, imarters and played a genuine cup-tie game. Thomas and Skelborn took the bonours for forward play the youngster following up at a tine pave. O'Malley, looking even thinner than when at Warrington. played a fine game and was easily the speediest and most dangerous forward in the Coventry ranks. Ile gave Tilley some obvious momenta. The borne halves were both good, and Dakin and Wooodburn made a very smart right wing. Burdett. at full back, was weak.</p>
        <p>I bare heard Fish criticised a gocrt lea! since Saturday. In my opinion he did all that was required of him Ile got the deciding try at a critical moment in the game, and that is what he was nut on the team for. If he jeers Op his training there should be no question about playing him at Wigan. Ile is still nur incrst dangerous wing three-quarter. and another fortnight's practice will get him into even bonier trim than lie was at Coventry.</p>
        <p>LINSPED COMPOUND for Coughs sad Colds. of pro.. .flicaci. 9d.,.!‘ Is. I Id. Of CUlsmists ouly.—Ady</p>
        <p>The following is a list of to-day's League matches, with the results of the corresponding games last</p>
        <p>.1 ford</p>
        <p>13 Broughton Ranger. '... 8 11 Widow,</p>
        <p>Swinton Oldham l6 Rochdale Hornets</p>
        <p>Wigan Hull Kingston &amp; Ilunslet ll Ebbw Vele . 10</p>
        <p>Batley Brantley 8 Dewsbury 5 Iludderafield Bradford York .</p>
        <p>arrington ought to repeat their victory of last season at Swinton. They have a strong aide in the field, whereas the Lions will be without H. Davies, Fairhurst, and Bailey. In the Warrington ranks, Unaworth takes the place of Fish, who has been ' relegated to the A" team. and Johnston replaces Poison in the forwards. Brooks comes back in place of Lloyd. Teams Warrington Tilley, fall-back; l'neworth, Dicken- son, Taylor and Waywell, three-quarters; Brooks and Creevey, half-backs ; Thomas, Shugars, Johnston, Morgan. Cheatere, and Skelhorn, forwards. Swinton—Griffiths ; Oartrell, Valentine. Flynn, Parker; Davies (Dail, Davies (Dan) ; Blears, Davies (D. B.), M'Veigh, Preston,Pollitt, and Smith. Reserve, Barnett.</p>
        <p>Swinton "A" (• Warrington "A .")—Bramford;John- son, Dawson, Howard ; Potts, Hackett ; Simister, Markland, Randall, Price, Jonas, and Evans. Warrington "A" —Mclntyre, full back ; Fish, Kenny, Jordan, and Bradshaw, threc.marters ; Lloyd sod Daintith, half backs ; Naylor, P. Boanitnau. A. Boardman, Hesketh, Wcrrall, and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Warrington journey to Halifax for a game in which neither tearn will he inclined to take risks in view of the Cup-ties on the Saturday. Thio Shrove Tuesday match is a popular fixture at Hanson Lane, and usually V, arrington give • good account of themselves. The team will not be chosen until after the Swinton match.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting games to-day should be seen at Weaste. where Saliord soil Widnos meet. The Chemicals will he at full strentrth, and need to win or draw the match to retain the fourth position. If they then Hanalei will move up as the result of • pro'3able win at the expense ~f Ebbw Vale.</p>
      </div>
