        <head>CUP TIES.</head>
        <p>THE CUP TIES.</p>
        <p>Warrington v Coventry. A Keen Game and a Fine Finish.</p>
        <p>The Warrirston men journeyed to Coventry full of confidence in their ability to enter the second round , of theCupeouipetition at the expense of the Midlanders ' but they found the talk anything but an easy one. Coventry had a full aide out and gave Warringtln a very keen game until the last quarter when they were overwhelmed by superior force. Luckily for the Wire- puller. both Fish and Creevey were able to play and Brooks won the only absentee. Capital weather pre- vailed and shout 1,000 people made the journey by the two special trains run by the London and North- Western Hallway, which reached Coventry more than an hour befor, the time for the kick-off. There wme • crowd of about 5,000 and the line ground of the Coventry Club wan .n excellent order. The only thing to militate against goo.l play was a half-gale of wind which blew from goal to goal. The teams and officials w•re : _ . . .</p>
        <p>atCoventry T Burdett, hack; Woodburn, R Dakin, II 011ViS, and J Houghton, threegnarters; I) Beyeon (captain) and M Vesrlett, halves ; J Tomes, J P Oldham, I. O'Malley, C I. Pigott, IV Hutt, and E Hatt, forssarda._... . • .</p>
        <p>Warrington : Tilley, hack ; Fish, Dickinson, Taylor and ‘Vaywell,threaquartere ; I,lorrl and Crease!, balf-backs ; Cheerers, lhonsaa, Shugara, Stelhorn, Poison and Morgan, forwards. Referee Mr A Hestford Broughton . . .</p>
        <p>Coventry won the toss and Thomas set the ball in motion ageinet the wind. The home nide were early pennlieed arid Thomas made a fine attempted. goal in the teeth of the gale, but the hall fell short. Fore short spell Werrington attacked, the three-quarbers pawing 'cross nicely, but being well checked by the other side. Burdett made a good return from Dickinson, and drectly after Warrington touched down. Woodburn secured and raced On. but was tackled by Tilley. Houghton snapped up the ball in track succession, and after beating his man he got in his kick. A free kick fell to the borneaters for obstructing Honghton, and Woodburn missed in hie attempt at goal. Keenly contested serum ended at half-way, from one of which Reynon got away nicely, but was collared, and then Warrington tried to break through, only for Derck) to get in a smart return. A free kick fell to Warrington, which enabled them to gain ground, bet Coventry made a -trod reeponed until Dicki son made his mark. More play in neutral territory followed. but at length Dakirs came away in promising style. Ile tricked past the opposition very cleverly before giving to Wo ti born, who made further headway. than parted with the ball to Davis, who made a dash for the line, and w e only collared just in time. Fish wes laid out in the struggledat after attention from the tre i ner he resumed. Dickinson was prominent on the defensive, and broke ciran away with. Rood dribble, which was carried well into the Coventry before he weer stopped. A deter- mined attack by Coventry brought about some exciting play, which ended to the hall being kicked dead. Yewlett followed up his kick nicely but Tilley ray ght the ball safely and worked his way round the Coventry man before making his return. More poseur. by Coventry caused the visitors anxiety in close quarterr, and a capital chance was Inbreed. Burdett had his kick charged down, but Coventry quickly recovered, only for Warrington to renew the effort, and this time lie Coventry foil back Was only just in time to send into touch. Warrington pressed hotly And Taylor got away on the right, but sires tackled in turner to donble in before parting to Waywell. Bryn., Larkin, end Woodburn came away with omen , laming. and the wing roan getting clear of Fish eprintrd grandly over half the length of the field. finally kicking ! past Tilley and scoring a grand try at the corner amid great cheers from the home crowd. Yevelett took the place kick, brd mode a goon attempt to supply the extras. Resuming, Warrington Lemnos rsther dangerous in an attack, which was renewed in still more vigorous faelsionand Fialt all hot brought about a score with one of his characterietic dashes. Coventry's tackling war remind, and on the opposite wing the visitors had a mplendid combined attack by Taylor and Weywell. which was only prevented from being crowned with sucrose throught be admirable tackling of Davis. A f ree kick went to Warrington. from sirhico they four d touch grandly esthe result ofTilleyaexcellentpr.dgment. , Warrington got well into the Coventry half and made !deeperats efforts to equalise. At last Creevey got going from f.loyd's pass, and drawing the defence on to Fish he swerved over the line in clever style, nod made tbs scores equal. Thomas failed at goal. Coventry et once made tracks fur the other end and Tilley defended in grand style. Play settled in the Warring- ton quarters, and twice Waywell relieved to centre with telling sprint.. Bryn.i gut away well, and Davies hail a greet chance hut got in front of the pan and Warriugton escaped. Half-time : Warrington I rev. Coventry I try. Warrington were on good terms with therneelven on resuming with the wind behind. They coon took the lead. A sterling dash hy th• forwatris took play to the Coventry quarter. Here Trylor got hold an.i raced down to the full hack. Burdett tackled, hot Skelhorn way well upend taking Taylor's pa... smartly honoured under the bar for Thorne. to kick &amp;newsy goal. The match seemed as good as over, at thin stage, and poseibly Warrington eased op. Anyhow Coventry reeponded in dashing style, O'Malley leading the way to the other end with a line dribble. Fish came in for a few words from the referee, and from a free kick which followed, Woodburn from wide our landed a splendid gad, This invested the gems with fresh intereet. Warrington resomed with some herd present's on the Coventry line, and the bell being %worked over, Burdett just managed to roach down, Waywell making a fne effort to get there first. Dakin got away on his own, but O'Malley mined a low pees, and then the Coventry forwards calla away in the loose. Play estroeg to the Coventry right wing. Tewlett and Dakin helping Woodburn along, and there wss a spell of exciting play as the home hacks raided the visitors' querter., hilt the movement was bottled up just in time, through the pass going on the wrong aide. The Reyeon kicked Comm. and Houghton getting d dratted away finely, and wee only stopped Putt in he nick of time a yard from the line. Amendn me directly after in bather a remarkable way. B fielded the ball end kicked. Het followed up a kick over the heed of Tilley. O'Meilley rushed scored amidst tremendrms excitement. Woodbu verted, and no pot Coventry shoed by a. coople of Tim is was about three-quarter time, and the local with such confidence and dash that Warringto time seemed beaten. Eventually they rose to t lion, and the forwards putting their weight work forced Coventry beck. Taylor tried • goal, but failed badly. Then Lloyd came picture, end tricking Revered men he pealed to eon. The latter ran well and then parted to Fi burst over at the corner despite the attention o burn and Dakin. Thomas took the kick. and _ . .</p>
        <p>great goal from touch. Warrington slapporrerl themselves hoarse under the excitement, and t more life in the game on resuming. Cron a beautiful swerving rllll oilier] clean beet th threequarters but he was not backed op. Ho followed in the Coventry quarter, and Iturdet rushed off the ball, Skelhorn picked up and</p>
        <p>under the itoete niteloi for Thom, to kick anothi and mat.. the inane aectire.Warrington kept up the and Creevey would have dodged over again t lame ankle which compelled bi al to pets ~, , P. ,Lloyd, Taylor and aywell passed cleanly. j</p>
