        <p>wing man was stopped a rod out, E. Hutt relieved with strong burst, and then the flnal cense with War- rington winners by 3 gosle, i tries, IS points ; to Coventry 2 guala, 2 trim, To point.. WIDNES HAVE AN EASY JOI-RNEY. _ _</p>
        <p>• Leeds Lane End United—a junior team—were the visitors to tVidnes on Saturday. Two thousand people assembled. The visitore lost the toes and had to face a strong wind. The " Chemicals at once attacked. Parker kicked when pressed, and the wind carried the ball over the line, JOl.Oll scoring. Disinter h improved. Aapey toned Touch at the corner flag, nod from the acrummage O'Gara gave to Swinton, who scored. Dainteth failed at the goal-kick, Lally broke away finely, nod then banded to O'Gara, who ran in under the poets, for Daintett to improve. At time. the United forwards by good footwork got into their opponenta' half, and Townson and Merriott runde • good but ineffectual et tempt to cross the line. Jun; before the interval O'Gara picked op in the loose, and passed to Laws, who had a clear run in. !Minted, improved. Halt-Use :—Wldnea, 3, goals and 4 tries ill points), Lane End United nil. Aided by the high wind, the Caked did. good deal of attacking, hut the hacks were too slow, and made per use of their opportunities. (Ally was penalised tinder the poste for not playing the ball, but Lockett found to phice a goal from an easy pokition. Just before the finish Widnes forward, broke away, and Era°, scored, Ltaititeth placing • goal. Result :—W Wines, I goals and 5 lei 44 C 2•; 140tht0 ; Lone End Coiled, oil. Teems and officials</p>
        <p>--Winn-etrliones, hack ; Swit.ton, Pennington, Dein- tith, and Laws, throwlinsrter backs ; O'Gara and Lally. half backs ; S. Aspey, ittgby, Johnson, Evans, Cosgrove, and. McCarthy, forwards. . .</p>
        <p>—l'efrard-±./Vr;;;;;IZilen; 'Marriott, Townson, Lockett, and Andereon, three-quarter becks; Parsons end Whit., half backs ; Dlattheive, Ford, Ryan, Hall, Hanson and Shaw, torevarde.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. Tillison, Salford. Touch judges, Megrim H. Heat, Runcorn, and J. Dutton. Warrington. RUNCORN OUTCLASS BROUCHI FON MOOR. The vilest of weather marred the Cup-tie At Broughton Moor, a gale and rainstorm sweeping the high, desolate upland on which the colliery village is perched. The attendance was probably the smallest that ever witnemed a Northern Union Cup-tip, only • hundrodl or .0 bracing the drenching rain. The game opeheil briskly, the Broughton Moor forwards attack- ing with rare dash end resolution, and playing their favourite kick-and-roan game bet once the Cheshire rear division got goring tt was not a aillicult matter for them to (nitwit the Moor defenders. It tincorn opened the scoring, Ws, after A cloy., /miming movement, Walton crooned at the corner. Shaw failed to improire. Toe local ream soon equalised. Being awarded a free kick, Pell kicked over, sill Moore following up well, scored.but Bell failed at goal. Then 04.1 got a pane, feinted to transfer to Walton, and darted over—a clever ry.Shaw made the extra points. In the seconil bait Runcorn over-ran their opponents. N lannagan Accom- plished the " bat trick " by scbring throe dashing tries in quick succession, all of which Shaw iniproved Broughton Moor came again with a forward each which 'ambito Ackerley to more, but Monsop failed St goal. Theo the 100.1 Wain appeared to realise the hopeless- nen of standing tip to the Cheshire combination, and tilo teuut withdrew twenty minutes before time warn up, Beata :—lttincorn 4 goods and 5 trier (23 points', Broughton Moor 2 trier Di points,. Teems and officials</p>
        <p>Runcorn—Shaw, back ; Cawley, Okell, Richardson, and Elsonagau, three-quarter hacks White and Watton half hark,; J. Walker, S. Walker, Doggett. Williams, Kennedy, and Parlbury, forwards. Broughton Moor.— Young, bark ; Moore, Moasop, R. Metcalfe, nnil Hutton, three-quarter hacks : Lynch and Moarrop, half hacks ; Bell, Iteatithwaire, Ackerley, Richard Metcalf, Atkinson and Watson, forwards. Referee, Mr. A. Smi:l3, Halifax. Touch-judge., Messrs. T. Tin° 101 l end T. C. Thomas. ST. HELENS v. BRAMLEY.</p>
        <p>This Cup-tie attracted a fair "gate" to the St. Helens ground, where the Ilminley thirteen, although beaten, gave a sterling accoont of themselves. The gate receipts amounted to .C 92 the attendance being 3,200. Playing with a hurricane at their backs, the visitors were oil over Sr. Helena for lime considerable time, not eventually Ward opened the score for them with a cleverly dropped goal. St. Helens asserted themselves, and Belshow, picking up at the centre, dodged .4/vend opponents, and ran in under the posts, for Turtill to improve. Bromley pressed desperately after this, but St. Helens increased their score through clever work by Kelly. Turtill again negotiated the goal. Ilelf-tinie —St. Helens, 2 goals 2 tries 'lO points? ; Bramley, I goal (2 points,. Brantley resumed,and early on made progress, Turtill amounting for Land and Ward in quick succession. Flanagan took a Hying ball at the centre, and though slightly impeded ran over for Turtill Sc do the needful. St. Helens continued to be aggressive, sod from pass- ing between Chavasse and Flanagan the latter scored far out. Play was keenly contested, and Behrhaw receiving, scared after. swerving TOIL Pope again crosee.l for Turtil to kick bin founo goal, and Bromley added to their score through Morgan, who wu assisted ton great extent by Ward making the opening. :11organ added the extra points. Iterodt. i—St Helens 4 goals 6 tries (26 pointej, Bramley 2 goal. I try (7 points .</p>
