      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR 1111S11 READERS.</head>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR 1111S11 READERS.</p>
        <p>PENSIONS AND PoOR RELIEF.</p>
        <p>An announcement of the greatest interest to Ireland was made by the Secretary to the Treasury in the House of Commons this week with respect to the removal of the pauper disqualification for the old age pension. Mr. Hobhouse mated that the i;overnment lisd decided that no contribution should be demanded ' from noor rates to meet the extra charge on the linperiul Exchequer caused by the removal of the disqualification. The Poor Law Unions throughout Ireland will learn of this substantial concession with the greatest gra•ification. The Treasury, Mr. Hobhouse added, cwild not afford ti meet ihie charge without a nme counterbalancing receipts. [oiler the Budget half of the land incremt nt duly was to he allocsteo to the relief of local eaten. A removal of the pauper disqualification means a relief to the Irish poor rates of close upon £2OOOO a year, and in England of course the relief reaches a much bigger figure. The Government now propose to appropriate the half of the land revenee tax in return for the increased charge involved on the exchequer lv the removal of the I.auper ilisqualifieati.m and row; laiut we think cannot fairly be tnwle with the proposal. though it may not be agreed that the only alternatives were those mentioned by Mr. Hohhouse. That gentleman, by the way, tell into a curious error when he said that the statements of the Premier and Mr. Lloyd George as to the probability of the rates being called upon were received without comment in the House of Commons. That wan not ao. Mr. Dillon raised an . objection on the part of Irish ratepayer!, immediately the proposal was mentioned, and his protest strangely enough was not tumb noticed in the English Press. The Irish objection has told, and the ratepayers of Great Britain as well as of Ireland owe the relief that has been granted to the keen, vigilant watchful. near of the Irish Party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>STARVING SEcoNDARY EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>STARVING SEcoNDARY EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>The hope of a bettor era for Irish Secondary Education is, we regret to nay, being frustrated, at least for a time. A surprising solution of the ditli• ruby created by the dependence of Secondary Education in these Wands upon what is known as whisky moray was put forward by Mr. Hobhouse in the debate referred to ab Ye. Government</p>
        <p>intend, it appears, to make the year 19t18-9 a standard year in respect of its *leaky money yield and to allot in the futute not a fluctuating amount dependent on the odd of this money hut to assign to local authorities a auto of El ,:let which he said was considerably ahead of previous grants. Now in the year in which the historic Budget wee introduced the whisky money fell by .Y.17,0t5), but it is proposed nevertheless to take this year ant standard and stereotype en yield with the result that Ireland'n grant will be f:20,0110 less than it was eleven years ago. This loan we have remarked a surprising a lotion of the matter and an anwing response to the Irish demand, whotte justice was admitted by the Chancellor of the Exchequer when he was asked to consular the disquieting Irate of the Secondary Educetion in Ireland. England receives .1:6511,000, Ireland:s proportionate share would be A:74,000, but she receives nothing. In Slip country where rich endowments and private munificence ore so numerous as t•, have made the mystem neatly independent of state aid the Treasury in most generous; Ireland where confisca- tions have deprived the people of the benefit of such help from t rivate benefactore, its to receive an eustenance from the Exchequer. The position is clearly untenable in equity and we sincerely hope the position may be reconsidered by the Treasury authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MIL GINNELL AND PRIVATE MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>MIL GINNELL AND PRIVATE MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>We said hist week in this column that Mr. Ginnell when referring to the somewhat exclusive lists of speakers for important debates would strike a sympithefc cord in many breasts in the ranks of private members, and the highly interesting debate in the House of Commons more than confirms this. Acconling to one report of the debate— that given in the " Manchester (Juardiati "—not a few members, although silting in solemn judgment on Mr Ginnell, rce.e to the height of changing places, no to ape .k, with him and openly taking his views as their own. On their own show- • ing if Mr. t.innell merited a week's suspension so did a good many others and for our part we are inclined to think that the I.etter course would have bee., to have adopted Mr. John Redmond's view of the affair. Whilst regretting Mr. Ginnell's breach of constitu- tional 'wore.' ure,M r. Redmond submitted that since the debate hod drawn an apology from Mr. Wedgwood and an open avowal from Mr. Oinnell that there was nothing personal in his attack on the Speaker the dignity of the House and the impartiality of the Speaker had been vindicated and that, therefore, the incident should close. And the large number whc voted against Mr. tainnell's enspension shows the . . .</p>
        <p>extent to—,vhich this view was shared by the HMls!' .which on the whole had enjoyed the interlude which thrust the nervous Mr. Ginuell into the Parliamentary limelight.</p>
        <p>TRANS \ AALERS AND HOME RULE</p>
        <p>An arresting feature of the many-sided question of Self-Government for Ireland is theunhroken chain of, testimony which in met with in the whole round el the British Colonito in favour of that demand being generously conceded. Occasimmlly we have encountered scepticism on this point and the assertion , Fa, been made further that the Irish in the Colonies I ,Intse to feel any active interest in the 11110800 m . The letter which appears below towhee this point and needs, we fancy, no comment Johnoneshurr, 30th January, 1911. John Redmond, Esq., M.P., Dublin. Dear Sir,—On behalf of my Comrnittaw and subscribers, I heea pleasure in bending you here- with draft value £2CO3 to assist you and your party in furtherance of your great sod glorious cat., the political liberty of Ireland, and which the I,94hr:ten of the Trannatal, in sympathy with their fellow-countrymen all tha world over, earnestly pray nary be early consummated. Meetings for the collection of finds were held at Johannesburg, Boksburg, Germiston, and Krugersdorp, and I hope to la able to forward you another dealt at an early data. fist of subecribers is now being prepared, and will reach you as soon as completed.—Believe me, dear sir, to Fe, you's very sincerely, CiEO. H. O'MEARA, lion. Treasurer, Irish Parliamentary Party Fund. A DEBATE AT NIA'CCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The Reform Club. Manchester, opened its doors the ! other evening to a largely attended gathering of its I members nod the members of the 'f.f.l Club for the ! purpose of conductirg a debate on the question of Home Rule, Mr. J. .1. Whitworth,the Liberal ! date in tbe recent ehction for the Knotsford took the chair and the leading representative Liberals of Manchester, Salford and district were present. Mr. Alderman Boyle, M.P., opened the debate with a general survey of the entire question and his excellent statement was appreciated all round. Mr. J. W. ludanty was called upon to deal with the negative position taken on by Mr. W. P. Crozier, a loader writer on the staff of the '' Manchester Guardian:. and based his rejoinder largely on the economic aspect of the question. roneidetable.intereSt was evinced in some figures and stativtics .ulitnitted by Mr. Polaritv. and the Manchester Liberal Federation are in correspondence with hill: with a view to embodying these figures in a leaflet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchfcrd made a draw of 3-3 against. Winnington Park at Winninston last week and were unlucky to ha. to share the spoils. for three parts of the game they bud Winnington on the d•fentive hut the shooting a the forward. waa not very good. Dale opened the trisitors' account in the first half asid Wilmington rqualized before half time and then took • strong lend by scoring two more goals. Latchford, however, for the remainder of the game put on great pressure and Hackney and ClifTe got through. Tole, Knight, •nd Allanson were in good form in defence for Latchford. The genie WM fast and open all through and furnished many exciting incidents. The second elevens Met, at Latchford and in a moder- ate same the home eleven wore soccertful by 2 goshs to OIL Many opportuniti., of scoring were mimed by the forwards, but one of the chief reasons of their failure etas the sound display of the Winnington left back who intervened time after time when • score seen,. certain. A goal wes scored in each half, Pearson netting before the interval and Starkey afterwards.</p>
        <p>The Second Eleven at Brooklands is :—Three full. hacks, W. T. Eccles, J. B. Wilkinson, It. W•lsh A. Bradshaw, F. Isherwood. It. Parkinson, halves ; T. Bank., D. Travis. A. Starkey, W. H. %nerd, H. Fletcher. forwards. Referee :—Mr. W. Fletcher. Train from Ailsley Station 1.14 p.m. Sate completely overplayed Stockton Heath in i• first half, baton the reetimption Stockton !teeth p, themselves together. nod atithoogh they were onaltli• - score thentselvea they prevented Sale from cdlii• their lead. Thornber and Ilattersby were the r. •• noteworthy players for the winner, whilst fi • losers Richardson and Pierpoint eurked ve.ry</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>I.III6RAL CLUIL</head>
        <p>I.III6RAL CLUIL</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING. By Redcap.</p>
        <p>11l NEN All MENI,</p>
        <p>isst week we tslked of natural incubation and , feeding chickens. This week a chapter on the ail- ments of chickens will doubtlesa be useful, because even now, some of my readers have got them running about I bad the pleasare of seeing a very fine brood last week-end. Well ! Let us get to our subject. -., . . .</p>
        <p>One of the chief and moat CZ111113071 ailment's- in</p>
        <p>chickens is cramp. Especially in tho. artificially reared.</p>
        <p>I•1:X111•. Chickens no reared often one the disease to the brooder having too much bottom beat, and sometimes by keeping the machine at too high a temperature generally. If you have chicks so affected you should at once that gradually) lower the tempe.ature. It is a I good plan, and often a safe one to put about 2in. cf soil on the floor of the brooder ; and on the top of this ;dace a layer of peat moss or chaff. The soil should be renewed at leant twice a week. Another, rause of cramp is by keeping the chicks on hard atone or even boarded floors. They should always have access to an earthen run. As I pointed out last week chickens can stand a lot of cold, but not damp. there- fore, if you ewe to it that the brooder is arranged as I have already pointed out, even if they do get into the open air, they ,will take little harm. Quite instinc- tively they return to their foster-mother when the weather conditiona are Imlay turable. Chickens that are affected should be induced to spend :us much time in the open air as possible, and fed nutritiously. If, as ita soinetimes the cause, it owing to touch damp- ness then they should be provided with a covered ran well tinkled with peat-moss and chaff. Keep them always at exercise by burying a few handful of "dry leech" in the litter.</p>
        <p>Taking these thing. into considerstioo,yort will he n difficulty in disposing of young ducks at good price,</p>
        <p>1111.1:11,</p>
        <p>If von have no special accommodation to rearm! !and (evening early chickens this might be done now Let me say at once that this paragraph is intende purely for the small man. Phone who adopt the nior scientific system of cramming need no advice from ml —Very well then 'Mr. Amateur !' While you at not as yet bony, you may occupy your spare time i building a fattening shed for early chickens. Th structure need not be expensive. What you want in long shed three feet !ugh and about four feet widi should le no erected that the open front face. th south, and with one end entirely boarded down to th ground ; at this end you may either place a few I°. perches or 'imply raise the floor. The rest of tit ground space should not be boarded, simply an eart door sell covered with litter. Accommodation Cu' as this is very useful for the epeedy fattening o boring cockerels not intended for stock purpose, You call determine the length of the fattening she according to the number you intend rearing. Sti further such a llace when not in use for th purpose named. makes an ideal scratching gross for laying stock in winter. :10‘1.:I. 110E1.</p>
        <p>The latest use for old and worn door mats is band. A friend of mine who rears a good number chickens is the inventor. To give it • name let it call it a green food producer. He takes •n old do mat, works into it sand and soil, then soaks it wi water and 11 Mil over the surface mustard seed. It then placed on shot bed, and in the course of a ft day. • good crop of green food for the chicks is hand. It in a aplendid ides and one worth copyin because of the difficulty in obtaining a necessity early in the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>OIPZ,</head>
        <p>OIPZ,</p>
        <p>This much dreaded disease is caused by a worm in the trachea. Sometimes the whole windpipe is filled with these deadly psrssites. It is often that some 111..11</p>
        <p>ground is partictaarly liable in giving this disease to ' chi,kens. When such is frond to be the case, the run should be well treated with lime. There are many and •arious cures suggested for the disease. Person- ally I do not think you can do better than add a little !loop powder to the drinking water. Naturally at first they resent the mixture, but if they are given none other to drink they soon come to. Further, I strongly recommend you to isolate affected ones and in very had cases give them a gargle of Condy's Fluid. This remedy can be applied in the following manner. Take a teatupful of warm water and add a teaspoonful of strong Condy's Fluid. Bold the chick partially head downwaida and iinmerse the • head butliciently far enough into the liquid so onto !get it into its throat. Do this several times and repeat until improvement is noticeable. Chickens dying of this disease should be destroyed by burning.</p>
        <p>These pests generally inhabit chickens in the head. Periodically you should examine the young stock and if any are affected a good ilasting with Keating's Powder will effect a spvedv cure. Last year I pur- chased a trio of Silkies and upon examination I found them literally infested, especially in the crest. Straightaway I mounted my bicycle and purchased a box of Keatingnin less than five minutes alter the lion dressing the fowl were completely rid of these pests, much to their general comfort and well being. From practical experience I have found no equal as a verniin di stroyer to Keating's. I hope these few hints will be beneficial to tot hose rearing chickens. It in often thought that when you see a large brood of chicks that a few have to die, it is impossible to rear them all and when by chance one .1. two of the mites do till ill, they have to take " pot luak." But if care and attention are given and • little trouble spent on them it pap, in the long run. Therefore be guided always by remembering the old adage, " A stitch in time saves nine."</p>
        <p>%RAT 1,1 Al 1,.</p>
        <p>. ....... . seed I remind ~;r1 this profitable branch of poult,y farming. It is high time to start hatching young ducks if you xvoulol he in time for the best market prices. But you say you cannot get broody hens ! Well get an incubator. ' They are always broody.' Bestde. it will soon pay for itself in the , out-put of 3o ung stock ; but get a gcod one. Cheap machims (as well as being a source of annoyance) are Ilear at any price. I have information to band saying that ducks are now laying well ; so you should have no difficulty in getting eggs. If y u keep stock birds then keep the machines mailing; adding to in batches of say twelve egg+. A good incubator and s reiiable brooder are the only equipment necessary to start tight off for early duck raising.</p>
        <p>F-ISKIPING TIIE</p>
        <p>Egg food should be given the first two days ; after that you should feed them on stale blend softened with scalded skimonilk and skim-milk and oatmeal mined to a stiff paste. This should form the diet for a fortnight, then commence with coarser food. The following will be found to he a quick growing diet. Biscuit meal aud ground oats ( best quality( with • little barley meal scalded and made sloppy ; then stiffened with middlings. At noon you should give them a meal of boiled vegetables and bullocks's liver (chopped fine, and Ifried off with bran. Follcwing this method of feeding they should In ready for killing in eight or ten weeks time. The very best varieties for table purposes are the Aylesbury's nod Pekins. and for a quick growing first cross, none can beat the two breeds named. Don't give the youngsters too much freedom or they will run the tiesh off their bones. Keep moving the coupe on to fresh ground, it is not much trounle to stake down a wire-netting Fun—say about twenty square yards for every dozen ducklings. Feed them often, hut see thst they clear up every meal. fin ord leave the food lying about ; it soon gets sour and is therefore detrimental to good feeding ; not only no but harmful to the stock.</p>
        <p>It nearly always depends on the quality of the bird and the way it is poi down" as to whether you obtain the best prices for it. Providing you are an expert dresser I should always advise you to go in for a private retail trade; middlemen's profits are thus eaved,as well as helping you to secure orlers for eggs and chickens. In fact, tile whole business of poultry farming is more protiotble when catering for a private trade. Of course, if you intend makirg a Mg !mimeos of duck raising, you could not pretend to do thie Therefore the next essential would be securing a good market. It is out always a wise plan to send to the ,1 next local tcwnarailable. I believe in supplying the biggest markets because you always get the best pi ives. Furthermore, once the people are satisfied with the quality. the price is secondary to them, l'eople who purchase a luxury never consider the cost. Si you will nee that the two chief pi.ints to observe air high quality and a good finish in dreesitig.</p>
