      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FIRST READING OF THE VETO BILL WAS CARRIED ON WEDNESDAY BY THE GREAT MAJORITY OF 124</head>
        <p>THE FIRST READING OF THE VETO BILL WAS CARRIED ON WEDNESDAY BY THE GREAT MAJORITY OF 124</p>
        <p>lacqiiire the land in Onknood Avenue for an open apace which on ninch needed in that neighboorhood, _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. UPON said 'hat as there was a Nemo of 60 ysrds between the proposed lary well at3,l the railway, 00 noire WOlllll 110 t be or ry w hen the school windows were closed. Ile c,iiitenderl it he committee wanted to negotiate for is site they *tenth' oppreach all the people who Itrol laud to dispose I atm ruche the best luirgnin possible. Cu antswer to Mr. Kin‘ilein, the CHAIRMAN Mid the conicuittve elm not couniiitted to purchase the king , hdwitrd-street site.</p>
        <p>• , Eventually Alderman Bolton agreed to withdraw the I minute for lurther consideration. ilti SILVER-STREEP SITE. • COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S INDIGNATION. At a meeting of the Sites nnd Buildings Committee on the 17th inst.. it having been reported that the General Pill p filt• Committee of the Town Cotincil bad arranged for she porch.° of a plot of land in Oliver. Street, adjoining the Sit ger-street Wesleyan school, coutroning mount 2,107 square yarda, ions,, altar some discusoion, renolV.l, (a) That the loon Council be a.ked to transfer, at the price paid by them for it (Ettl), the land in queltion to the Education Com- mittee with a view to the emotion theieon, subject to the approval of the mite by the lloard of Education, of no Infants' school to supply the needs of the district (b) that, subject to Lho approval of the site by the Board of Education and to thu Town COlll,Ci 1 agreeing to this proposal, authority be given for the necessary application to be inado to the Loos! Government Board for their sanction to the raining of the necessary loan for the purchase of the site : and (01 that the Law Clerk be instructed to iaatte the requisite public: notice of the Committee's intention to provide on the site a public elementary school to accommodate 110 scholars. Alderman Bot-roN said the Goatee. of Silver-street schools had long had the Idea of act-paring the land for extenaion purposes, bat when it 041110 into the market they did not feel they had siClicinot money to boy it. Rocently the site sans offered for rode by auction end the General Purposes Committee of the l'osto Council decided to appoint a o.eplitution to see if they could purchase it non suitable price. The land was bought and as the Sit. and Buildings Committeti were satia- ted tha: the price Wll9 reasonahle they recommended that it be transferred to the di:location Committee. It was the only suitable place for an intuit school in that district.</p>
        <p>( ouncillor I EMIIEILTON mid Fe objected to the pro- posed transfer an principle. 'the land was purchased ithout the consent of the Edueation Committee having been obtained by the Sites and Ibilibilings Com- mittee—in fact it had not been before the latter com- mittee before the purchase was made. It was • highly improper way of mud:ming 111.11 d. The Cuobost is (Councillor Rob: Ma) : I have con- aidered the possibility of your raising this question and 1 mint say at once that I cannot permit any ebiminsion or any amendment which simply deals with the quemion of . ow this property has been purchased. Cooncilbor PF.NIII/.1,0N moved an amendment that the minute be referred back.</p>
        <p>The CH A 1101 AN said he cOulil not accept any amend- ment on the question of procedure but only on the conditions specified in the committee's recommenda- tion. Councillor I'IMBRPTC'N : I claim I am entitled to point out why the committee should not agree to this. Councillor Runners If you care to point out that the land is unauitable or that the price le an improper one, you are perfectly within gent rights, but you cannot move an amen besot merely for the purpose of raising a question of prof t-dure. Councillor l'amitnicroN complained that the Sites and Buildings Committee had been overlooked. rio C 0 onIN ax : Von can raise a discussion on that by special motion but out under cover of an amend- ment. Councillor I'r.vit,ttutox : It is irregular and illegal</p>
