        <head>NEW SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>NEW SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED SITES CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion at the Education Committee</p>
        <p>There was a great deal if dtscussion at the moral V 1 meeting of the Werringion Education etommittee . Monday evening with regard to the purchase of sites 1 for two new athools. At a meeting of the Sites old Buildings Committee a report wan stthmitted by tho o Surveyor upon the sites bra public elementary school in King Edward-street and Oakwood Av... After considerable discussion, it was decided to re-shine the decision arrived at by the Coninthree at their meeting on the 18th November last, and it was accordingly resolved (a) that anthority be given for the purchase of a plot of lend in King Edward-street, containing about scree, eta cost of £72:3 per ecru, for the purpose of the erection thereon of a (Bolin elementary school providing acconimodetion fo-: 420 boys, 420 girls and 430 infants, together with a cookery centre, bendicraft centre, douche hathe and caretakers house ; (b) that the necensary application heeled° to the Lookki (iovarte merit Board for their sanction to the raining of the : neressery loan in respect of the same ; end he) that the Lew Clerk be instructed to issue the requittite public notice of the Committee's intenti MI to provide, on the : site, a nubile elementary school to acoommodate 1260 scholars. Aldermen BOLTON, in moving the adoption of the e.t.a, mid the committee cousitiend the King k.;dwardetreet eite wan the bent that could he olotained In that dint rict end the price agreed upon was eutiefas- tory. 'rho vole on the purchase was equal—three being he lather nod three fig/sleet—end considering ell the circunietences he gees his costing vote for the King Edward-street rite. lie would, however, like to trot the feeling of the Education Committee tie to whether the recommendation should be referred hack with a view to placing the owners of eligible sites in competi- tion. lie contendid that the committee wonld not fiancee touch to spend on Street making in connection with the king Edwardetroteteith as they would for the other. Nir. 11. T. Full Lot- Kfi seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor WILKINSON InortAl na amendment that the recornmeuriation be referretl back, lie maintained trot one site was (mint as good 88 the other but the price of the King Edwarrbetreet tile (including poivingt was £192 lOs, per acre more than that of tlie 0 tknotod- /Melee site. On the acreage which the committee would require the difference was £386. The Oak stood- aressue site was putt as convenient as the other,.. Patigate-lene vthold be the main approach to either tate for roost of tire children attending the new school. lie admitted that the Oakwood-avence land was slightly more water-logged than the other, hut the water aila at such a distance from the surf.tne that it would not he any aeriott.. drawback. Councillor Pesti:MOON thcontled. sett! there WAS no reason why the committee should give the prefer- ence to the Klng Edward-street site. The difference in the pri.s, according to his computetion, won. be .C 1,500. The burden of the education rate was heavy enough and it behoved the committee to do everything as economically es possible. It would he p.sittle to build a school on the Oakwood-avenue nit* without reaching the water. Canon SLEvESSON liain lino objection to the Oakwood- venue site was that it men on the extreme edge of the borough and if the new nchool were built there, it would he very largely used by children for whom the Lancashire Counts, Council ought to be responsible. The school would also he too far away froni the area where it would he most woeful. ills second objection was the proximity of the site to the railway. It use the noisieet place that could nave neon chosen. The Rung Edward-street rite wart much more preferable, rind although the difference in cost was rather Winn!, there was some reationable ho of its n re-considered end lessened. fig Just Co DAVILA said thet unless there was a footpsth higher than the c.d.!, some of the children would have to wade through several inches ttf water on I their grey to the Oakwoorimvenue school. Mr. (t. WALK Kit Reid he could not see that one site would Ise catering for outside children more than the 00. r. much were property was likely to he erected in the neighbourhood of Oak woodevenue,and the children attending the school would not have to cross Padget.: lane where there wee constant motor traffic. Aidertnne BoLTOK Said that if tooth tithe v-ere cart in the 81.10 way the difference in cost 34.1.1 be</p>
        <p>only k..i23. Mr. PhINSTON Wee in favour of the amendment on the ground of oust mid euitsbility. Alderman BeNsitry .aid it would not be fair for the committee to go back on a bargain and allow under- cutting. Either site was fairly goo.l hut on the whole the King Edward-street Cite wav the better one. No .elf-realiecting borough ought to be satisfied with 'mond heat. In considered the ideal solution would IJO to build the 101001 in King Edward•ntreet and</p>
