        <p>sod it ire job. That is my opinion, and if I cannot express it hero I shall express it In another place. Here property is bought and the General PurposesContmittee hart only two hours notice of it. The special business was not even specified on the agenda. I say it is highly improper. I don't care what the TIMIL may he —whether it is good or had—if hand is going to be pur- chased in this manner any sort of job CI II go through. The Co or NIAN : Your remarks are out of order and I wont listen to them.</p>
        <p>. ..... Mr. WALKIrIe aaid that although he might have ay tot patby with Cotincillor Pemberton's objection he though, It ought to be 'reined in the Town Council and not that meeting., The CB xlnNt ow:flue not going to he raised here. CruncillorPlias:son said it wan wrong for the purchase to hnve been married out Lover the heads of the Sites and Building, Committee. It bad been a job !rom beginning to end. Alderman Ituccon Fish! thin was the lint time the site ever came before the Si ten and Building. Committee :Ind there was only one dissentient from the proposed transfer.</p>
        <p>I:ERTON : After it was bought. Alderman lio,rox said the question could not have :erne before the Sites and Buildings Committee in tangible form at en earlier date. Councillor Wii.k mien said the General Purposes Committee bought the land for the Corporation. They wanted to know if the Sites and Buildings Com- mittee wanted it; if they did not, they could leave it. As a member of the Corporation he would not listen to Councillor Penoberton's allegation.. Jobbery would never exist where he (Councillor Wilkinson) was. Ile looked upon the purchase al one of the finest the Cor- poration had made for many years. The minutee were epprwed.</p>
        <p>ARCHITECTURAL WORK. NEW Al' POINTNIENT SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Conaidoration of a suggestion to appoint an officer to undertake the architectural work, etc., of thin Com- mittee wee deferred to the next meeting of the Sites and Building,.Committee, tho intirveyor being roqueeted to report upon the eubject in the meantime. Alderman BOLTON seid the Committee had got into conflict with people as to the best way of conducting their busineer. When they tried to save money some one stepped in on trade union grounds, and mid they were not doing right. They were committed to an eavonse of £220 a year for thirty years for the schools already built, and so more schools wool(' beim to be provided thocommittee wesconsidering the advisability of appointing an officer to undertake the architectural work. 7 he minutes were parsed.</p>
