      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LADY GREENALL AND UNEMPLOYMENT. A CONTRADICTION FROM AMERICA. Death from Starvation FOLLOWS DEAR FOOD AND LITTLE WORK.</head>
        <p>LADY GREENALL AND UNEMPLOYMENT. A CONTRADICTION FROM AMERICA. Death from Starvation FOLLOWS DEAR FOOD AND LITTLE WORK.</p>
        <p>To TUE EDIT.: lir TIIE " EN 11 lEEE."</p>
        <p>Sir,—You will perhaps wonder why this epistle, hut after reading in your paper lAdy Greenall's remarks with regarti to Tariff Reform and work for all, I thought a few facts which have mine under my notice the last two months might Iwo! interest to your goodself with reference to unemployment in this part of the 1..5.A. In the first place I enclose two cuttings taken from the " Rochester [llion and Advertiser " of February ,qh, En I, which speak for themselves.</p>
        <p>STRUGGLE FOR EXISTENCE PROVES TOO HARD, WHOLE FAMILY DEAD. By I /nivel Preen. Now roan, Fob. H.—Their ravings gone, with no work available and with starvation in sight lornnk Bernard, 32, and his wife, Mamie, :10, decided that the struggle for exintence wits too great. While their two noon, Georgie, four, and Ibtoiel two' were aleepine in their little apartment on the third floor of 750 Fulton•ntreet, Brooklyn, the parents turned on the gas and went to nloep. To-day Joseph Barratt, a boo driver, who lives on the top floor of the tenement, while going through the balk, smelled gns. Ile traced it to the Bernard apsttment, weot down the lire escape and broke in a window. All four of thit family were dead in their beds. Neighboors said that Mrs. Bernard told them on Monday that they were down to thhir last fifty cents. She said they had had nix hundred dollars hod 'Ammer, bob that her ho•band had loot his job and all the money had been spenr, for food. WIFE DF,AId ANL/ HUSBAND MAY DIE. Now YORK, Feb. H.—Mithin a few blocks of the wealthiest section of Brooklyn Mrs. Mary Sidel ntar•ed to death toolny. Me husband, Peter Sidelotho had aacrilleed himself to try to keep his wife alive, is in St. Catherine's Ilospilni suffer- ing fromlbunger and expostire. Ile, too, may die. The couple's bnliy llied a few weeks ago nhortly after it was born, becatise its mother coolil not give it sufficient nourinhmont. The police were called to 125 Bushwickeivenne to-dar. In a garret room, whose broken windows permitted the snow to sift in until it almont , covered the ilnor, they friond Mrs. Side] lying on it i twit deed. Over her wan piled the clothing sod her loisband's overcoat. Beside the bed-side, on the floor onconscioun from esponore, lay Peter Sidel. At the hospital to which he was hurried he told his story. tt I had plenty of work until lent fall. Then I lost my job, and since then hove atruggled, oh, no hard to get soother but have felled. We were dispronessed and sold our fornitore, all but the bed and the stove. Then we got this gorret and were let live here without paying rent. Two month. ago our baby wns born. It didn't live long nod the doctors told as it etnrved and froze to death. Monday I got a jolt as a night porter sail earned 1.25 dollars which I brought home. h/y wife cent out to boy !cod and fainted on the Street from weekness. They carried her tip here and I got her come food which she ate. Maybe we ate too mech for coercing people." Againjo yesterday's "New York Ilerald " (Cebritary 7th, 11111,1 roe ow informed I hat Nome 51)00 Men of Illinois Steel I'lant Co. have received notice that the works will commence running again, the first week in April, after nix months rest. Also 4,1151 men employed in the plants of the American Bridge Co., The American Sheet and Tin l'lrtte Co., and The Ili-Products Coke Co. of Gary, Intl., who were laid off some time ago, on account of poor busineas, will he taken back between Starch 25th and April Ist, 1911. Again, Pittsburg and dietrict iron and idoel workers to the tone of 2111,0111 men have been idle for over 1 months, and .111 per cent of the remainder aro only o.t half time.</p>
        <p>..... Again, a short time ago it wan reported (middle of January) that in and an'und Boston, Mans, there were over 50,015 t textile workers ont of na rk, and the American Woollen Co., which control :VI large factories in New England and usually employ 35,000 hande, has stopped over lit per cent until further notice, and other factories which do not belong to the trust are employing only one half of their usual staff and similar conditions prevail in the factories of Pennsylvania, New York and New Jersey. Again, we expect every day to hear of a great railway strike, owing to the failure of the directors and the men's representatives to come to term% with reference to the wages dispute of the 35,0 i MI firemen employed by the ill western railroads. Again the" New York Herald " of to-day informs on that Senator Sullivan of New York, gave yestenlayl to 6,15/0 destitute and homeless men of all ages and ' nationalities, hoots and sock/band some of these poor beggars had waited for over 12 hours in the snow with the thermometer almost at zero.</p>
        <p>.. Again, you cannot pick up any daily paper without seeing a paragraph similar to the following. The identical appeared in laitt Saturday's "Rochester Times." "Six Unions on strike in Gary. Ind., at one and the same time, vie., cart enters, roofers, lathers, steamfitters, sheettnetril workers, and electrical workers, and it in feared that other Unioes will noon be called out. More than 3,000,000 dollars worth of new work in checked and hundreds of men are out of work." Why ? The masters will not pay union wages. Again, the meat wagon drivers and meat handlers. have just declared general strike against the large packing hooves, and have gone out for higher wages and less hours. At present the men get 12 dollars per week, and have to work from 12 to 15 hours per day. 'rho following houses are involved. Messrs. Armoure, Bannuond, Cudahy. Nelson Morris, Swift and Co., Schwartaschild and Co., and Sulzberger, , seven of the leading lights in the canning business and who are making millions every year in dividends. Again 11,00 Cigar Makers, haio only just returned ,1 to work, from a strike which started as far back as I last July. The immufactorers refused to recognize the Union, and pay proper wages, which has resulted in enormotet financial loan to the masufactarer (and misery to the people/ but who at last were compelled ! to give in. Again, the etrike of the Garment Workers of Chicago which cetrimeneed in Docemlmr han just been , brought to a finish and the workmen have got their jurit dues. ! This strike through the cancellation of orders cost the manufacturer. over 1,000,1010) dela. In addition to thin loss the masters have seen thousands a doil."! worth of materials ruined by the incompetent blacklegs ' employed by them. In one house alone over 2,100) waistcoats were " botched " owing in bail work. ! This strike cost the manufacturers in all some 11,00IGSKI dollars. What for ? "To keep down the wages." But if you liaten to thin Tariff Reform honk and pact of lies, you would almost believe that this was the land flowing with milk and honey, and at least there were two jobs for every man. DON'T BELIEVE IT!</p>
        <p>Finally,even in thin fair city of Itocheater,a miniature Birmingham, unemployment is rife, thou Panda walk- ing the street. from Monday morning until Saturday night. Fancy if you can, a large store which in tlie ordinary may employs some 1,60,1 hand., advertieed for and got without any trouble, acme 700 extra band% for their Christmas trade, and only ten days ago the mine firm held their annual linen pale, and asked for 75 extra hands. With what result? SUMO 8,10 people applied for the position.. These are facto. If everybody had been in work how could such numbers have been found in no short a time? Again the poorhouse, workhoune, onion. or whatever you care to call it, is not large enough to hold the destitute (to the tune of Kane 100) of this city, with a population of 220,0041. And no they have been com- pelled to board this number 1400) out, as hest they could. On Christmas Day the Salvation Army pro- vided 3,000 dinners to adults and 2,1550 children and Ike " Volunteers of America " another society similar to the Army, did likewise. -</p>
        <p>" TEI .6 11.%1E N,, UNE3IIIOIMENT." Hero are a few FOOD PRICES</p>
        <p>- at the Moment. Bacon, Is. Sd. per lb. Bread, 10d. per 416 loaf. Tea, 23.11 d. per lb, and hardly fit to drink. Flour, 21d. per lb. Cheese, anything from Is. :A. to 2x. Gd. per lb. Ens, IS cents. a dozen or Ys. English money and mind you not fresh. You cannot get such things here. These were laid some four or five years ago, and have been in cold storage. Faithfully yours, ARTHUR M. LANDON:. Don't forget, please put in Arthur Croefield at the next election. Warrington must have gone mad in , December. 8.3, Avenue C, Rochester, N.V. February Bth, 1811.</p>
