      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SOAP TRADE 1901-1910.</head>
        <p>THE SOAP TRADE 1901-1910.</p>
        <p>'COMPILED BY C.</p>
        <p>One does not require to be an authority, or to be engaged in the soap trade, to be interested in it. To the savage it is a super- fluity, to the civilised man a necessity. The price we pay for soap is of concern to every- one, because as a necessity it is one of the things we have to provide for before we reach the margin of surplus which we can spend 88 we like. Locally it has a further importance, inasmuch as it is an industry upon which a considerable number of Warrington people are dependent for their livelihood. What then nationally is the soap trade record during the last decade? I 'tone of those " injured industries," 'ng ; are the markets of the wort w out and</p>
        <p>crippling our exports which have adopted</p>
        <p>countries capturing</p>
        <p>our home trade? The accotnpanyi answer to all these</p>
        <p>MR W. H. LEVER'S AUTHORITY INVOKED.</p>
        <p>PARKER.-NO. 6.1</p>
        <p>I States, when handicapped by Protectionist fallacies. British manufacturers have I enjoyed the blessings of Free Trade whilst I their foreign rivals have suffered under the ' height of Tariff Reform. There is nothing else to explain the present situation. Most raw materials, such as cotton and others, are grown at the door of foreign factories. Neither can it lie truthfully said that the foreigner does not under- stand his business as a manufacturer. In many lines he is showing greater scientific knowledge of Ids business than oruelven, 1111(1, in some rime., by superior scientific knowledge, he hen been enabled to make progress in competition with its in spite of his Tariff Reform handicap.' OBJECT LESSON FROM GERMANY.</p>
        <p>" A few years ago a deputation from German soap- Imakers paid a omit on behalf of the soap trade ,d Germany to Englidt soap works to inquire into the I came of Britain's great hold on the export snap trade of the world. After their tour of inspection they returned to Germany and reported that German soap- makers had not even the remotest chance of lateness-</p>
        <p>.1 I k i give, ;(i . 07.1 1901 I 1902 1 1903 I 1904 1905 1906 1907 1900 1709 1910 0.. . /PO 1901-.110 1.4.00 /,70004 dP 00. /,‘,O dxhorli increase 7qtan /Aso /.-rl 0 imiborli increast ,JJ:F7I 111 41-00 /ion" 7-pade Gain 6624216 //foe /Joe) / /0 4:210al pi." 11,,,..v 111 „0, ~,,„ 9,0 11 ~ ~, 0. 7: Iliwoll 7,0 4w I. i.., J. ....111 i4:00 300 WPAIMMMMIMMIMIMMIMIMIMIII Jac) 400 ININEIIIII.IIIII.II MIME lon 1. Am Ell all 111111111111 iov</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Note—Soap Stock is included in the Imports throughout, although classed elsewhere in the Board of Trade Returns since 1906.</head>
        <p>Note—Soap Stock is included in the Imports throughout, although classed elsewhere in the Board of Trade Returns since 1906.</p>
        <p>What is the principahcause of this advance? It is all explained in a sentence. Cheap production, the result of free imports. I cannot better enforce this truth than by borrowing from the writings of Mr. W. H. Lever, of Port Sunlight. IMPREGNABLE AT HOME— IRRESISTIBLE ABROAD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lever says: "The strength of the position of the British manufacturer rests upon the fact that, under Free TradeAhm enjoys access to his raw materials at their lowest possible cost of production. His .Government does not artificially raise the cost to him of his raw materials, nor does it coddle him with tariffs for his home market. Free Trade conditions have enabled the British manufacturer to work himself into a position•that makes him impregnable at home, and irresistible abroad.</p>
        <p>MASTERS•OFITHE MARKET. Take the soap business, for instance •, the prosperity of the British soap-maker dates from the removal of the duties from off soap, and also from off the soap-makers' materials, which was accomplished by Mr. Gladstone in the middle of last century. Prior to that time the British soap-maker enjoyed practi- cally no export trade in soap,whilst he had to compete against a very considerable importa- tion of foreign soap into the United Kingdom. Soap was also at that time very dear, and mostly very poor in quality. Free Trade in both soap. and the soap-makers' raw materials has changed all this, and, to-day, British soap-makers enjoy an enormous export trade in soap—larger,in fact, than the real export trade in actual soap of all the other nations of the world added together, and this is not the only gain, for, simultane- ously, with the growth of this export trade in soap, has gone on the gradual decrease in the importation per head of foreign soap, until, to-day, it is a neglig- ible quantity, confined 'mainly to fancy articles. The British soap-maker to-day suffers from no competition with the foreigner. Every once in a while a foreigner comes over, opens an office in London, and makes an advertising expenditure out of all proportion to the sales he can realise, and then gradually or suddenly retires from the field a sadder and a wiser man." . .</p>
        <p>'1 repeat that the British soap-maker, by reason of our I. roe Trade policy on both soap and raw materials, has no serious competitor to meet in his home market, and at the same time, enjoys a larger real export trade in actual soap, than all the other nations of the world added together. To Free Trade, and Free Trade alone, has the credit to be given for this. The United Kingdom, as a manu- facturing centre, has few advantages to offer her manufacturers. But our immediate forefathers, in their wisdom, gave us Free Trade, and that has proved of greater importance to our manufacturers than all the natural advantages of soil, rivers and climate, enjoyed by manufacturers in the great United</p>
        <p>fully competing with British soap-makers for export trade no long as the cost to German soap-makers of all their raw materials, and all the ingredients and sundry supplies used in the making of stop, down to the very paper for wrappers and the nails for the packing cases, was artificially rained by Tariffs. In short, that no long as German soap-makers produced under Tariff Reform conditions and British soap- makers under Free Trade conditions, the Germans had no prospect of !securing even a portion of this huge export trade in sap." "To the British se well as to the German nianit- faeturer, the raw materials of one manufacturer are the manufactured and finished products of sonie other manufacturer. The German soap-maker has a tariff put on soap by his Government. This certainly acts, as it was intended to act, an a barrier to importation of soap into Germany. At the name time the Hamburg oil-crusher hair a duty placed on oil that bars, as it was intended to do, the free importation of oils by the German soap-makers The manufacturers of salt, alkali, paper, and the thousand and one other manu- facturers of sundry supplies used by tho German soap-maker, have each also seen to it that they each had Tariffs on their articles to bar their free importa- tion by the German soapmaker."</p>
        <p>FREE IMPORTS MORE EFFECTIVE THAN TARIFFS.</p>
        <p>"The British manufacturer requires no Tariff Reform [barriers to keep the foreigner oat of his home market, Free Trade condition* enable him to hold his own market eqiially as effectively as they enable him to build up his export trade on the bargaining power of price, value and quality. The foresight of our immediate ancestors in enabling British manufacturers to work under the magic, vitalising conditions of Free Trade, has, in one short half century, made us impregnable at home and irresistible abroad. The magic of Free Trade bas brought to the factory door of me liritish manufacturer a world-wide empty of raw materials at natural cost prices, and in doing so has placed the British manufacturer in the strongest pmition possible, and ham at the mime time given him the best and most effective weapon with ivldeli to light anccesshilly the hostile tariffs id foreign nations and to effectivly hold his borne market." In answer to a correspondent Mr. lover says "1 gather from your letter that you do not tee why, if manufacturing is cheaper in England, goods cannot be manufactured in England and shipped to high Protectionist countries such as, let no say, Germany or the United States, as freely as to neutral markets. I thought I had made the reanon clear that it is in consequence of the high protective duties put on by these two countries.</p>
        <p>Take Lever Brother.'" Lifebeoy "soap for instance, In the United Staten, Lirebuoy soap sells for exactly 54. for the name quality and quantity as sells in England for M., that is to say, 2d. per 12oz. bar dearer.</p>
        <p>The duty, however, in the United States is 2() cents a pound on Carbolic soape (item 69 in the Tariff of I Hurd) which would add another 7d. to the coat of a bar of Lifebuoy if tho soap were made in England and shipped to the United States. Thus the 3d. tablet in England would after payment of duty be 101 d. in the United States, whereas by manufacttuing in the United States the 3d. tablet becomes 3d.</p>
        <p>If Tariff Reform is introduced into England, Tariff Reformers say that there should be no increased cost to the consumer, which is exactly contrary to the fact. There is not a single high Protectionist country where Lever Brothers have put works where the coat of soap in that country is so cheap as in England. There is not a single high Protectionist country in which Lever Brothers hare works where soaps eon he made for exportation out of that high Proteciionist country."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" GUILD " PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>With my whole heart would I support the plea in behalf of Warrington's young (Inild of Help. I have experience of eimilar organisations in at least two other towns, and have certainly nothing but com- mendation for the work of all engaged therein. I tel that as to the local Guild not a little misapprehension exists in the public mind. Naturally, the effects are somewhat depressing no far as officials and active supporters are concerned, though the rank, of these are readily recruited from weli-informed sympathisers; but the promoters need not yet be downhearted—more light upon their aims and objects will asenredly rally to their side the forces they are anxious to attract, and HO, fainting not, neither betraying wearinees in their well-doing, they may confidently expect in due season to reap thirty, sixty, nay even a hundred-fold. MISTAKEN CHARITY - OR LAZINESS.</p>
        <p>I would particularly empliaaitte the points in the Warrington Guild'a annual report respecting pi °- tensional beggars and indiscriminate altnegiving. A. intimated, I happen to be familiar with the moths* operand; of one other Lancashire and one Yorkshire organisation of the like character and scope. The latter of thew insues a perirslical entitled "The Helper," awl although I left that district many yearn ago, I am greatly interested in this publication. Time 'after time I fled it repeated therein that the stardy beggar, the door-to-door cadger, is one of the Guild's greatest difficulties. The advice is that if people would cease from giving, the beggar would rearm from troubling. In a recent number which in before me an I write, the following occurs How Mon will the householder realise that an a clime these beggars are impordern and altogether otiworthy of help? The Guild h. had conaiderable effect in the diminntion of the 'mothers of this class here, but unfortunately, some are still with us. Granted that now and again a worthy case of need' conies to your door. In it not infinitely better that thin ease should be adequately treated rather than giving an offhand penny or shilling to salve one's conscience? Thin is where the Guild steps in, and if the moor is reported to the office it will be properly and efficiently dealt with." A circular has just been issued to the public on diene questions. It places before readers Urn real case of the vagrant and begga:—men who, apart tierhaps from I or 2 per cent., do not want regular work, but live a useless life, sustained by foolish gifts given at the door. Take one or two examples. One who asked a certain newspaper correspondent for " something tobarda a night's lodging," first alleged that he had lately been employed an a bottle washer at a brewery, but left voluntarily because (these are hie own words) he "inns afraid he might give way to the temptation of drink." Pressed for a better reason he had the amlacity to declare that he had decided to leave because the other boys "used such dreadful language " and he wan afraid he might pick up that habit. Later on he said that his ambition was to get a job where he could save a little money in order to buy an illuetrated Bible in parts. As the correspondent pertinently added, there must present- ably be people with whom this "lay "is successful ; it is a pity. t 'tear indeed it should be that so lung ae a tender-hearted public, often against its better judg- went, gives money to beggars, beggars there will be. So many people would rather give a few coppers to half-a-dozen wastrels then run' the risk of spurning one unfortunate wayfarer, and thongh they discover to-day that they were shamefully deceived yeeterdr, they will wee the same soft-hearted logic to-morrow. There will be leas of thim indiscriminate giving, how- ever, when the public learn of the many dodges these cadgers adopt—an they are destined to do through that admirable agency, the Warrington Guild of Help. FUNNY Al) VE It FISEMENTS.</p>
        <p>I may here mention as a coincidence that on the very day of the appearance of the diverting lint of blundering advertisementn compiled by " Uncle Caleb " for tho " Examiner " Children's Corner I came across one suitable for addition thereto. Ilere it is :—" For Sale, a Second-hand Family Coach, I.nitable for a country gentleman with a red and yellow striped body and a new inside." Only a few weeks ago, attention was directed to the following example or another kind :—" The South Easurn and , Chatham Railway have just published a very finely illuminated map, entitled,' 'through the Garden of Eden.' Particulars may be had from the Superin- tendent of the Line, London Bridge Station." In that care the presumption was that, inasmuch nun limp of Kent was referred to, it should have read "Garden of England," instead of " Eden" DICKENS.</p>
        <p>The projected visit of Mr. Alfred Tennyson Dietena under the auspicen of that most enterpriming body, the Warrington Philinuathic Society, affords me opportunity to call special attention to the highly inteiesting fixture, and incidentally to remark that, like many belonging to my period, I am more familiar with Dickens' novels than with the works of any other writer of fiction. Throughout the Int forty years, I have generally had one or other of then in hand; I have read the whole of them aloud in the family circle twice; and a postcard album consisting wholly of illustration of Dickene' Land and of the master a creations is among my most cherished poesessions. Mr. Powys, whom I line heard lecture on the subject, ranks Dickens with Rabelais and l'orvanus and Shakespeare, giving hint a place of honour among great creative geniusee. I considered his estimate eminently just. It was to the effect that if one interpreted beauty in its widest arose, and made it mean life, Dickens had done more to make beauty oat of uglinen than anyone. What Dickens really loved was life wherever it appeared. Dickens saw humour in everything. lie broke the bounds of every limit with his imagination, in this there hail never been nine like him. Ile was a born democrat, and had a meulnesa for the people. Ile believed in the inherent goodness of the people. All through hie books one met with that spirit. As I recall the deliverance, Mr. Powys dealt further with the wonderful " lending character, created by Dickens who noticed people's peculiarities to each an cumin that it would be true to maintain that in hie writing* the people were found as though they had been born in their clothes. He dealt with the great rank and file. Ile represented the proletariat. The re.ult wan that they got'a great deal of vulgarity. The NVIi"lo gist say of Pickwick, was vulgar. Pick- wiekian jokes were the kind that appealed to the innocent but not too fastidious members of the English proletariat, an instance of which was when Mr. Weller, senior, !mots of Stiggin eating no many muffins that "he wan a nailing Risibly." Of the pathos, the humorous and the child characters of the master whose immortality ensured by (among other influeneem) the abounding popularity of the Dickens' fellowship, one might, of course write at length ; but my object is eimply to commend to all and eundry the remarkable local event announced for oe4 Week (vlde advertisement on front page), and not in any pay to anticipate what may he said by a worthy eon of a worthy sire on that reersorable oecnion. CONC ERNIDKVMARRIAGE. " Thg Milleriege Law "wee the topic for considers- tiny at hut Sunday evening's service in St.. Panic Church. What a tempting theme is here so eeted ! Of those who long for cheap and any m.t ode for the dissolution of the marriage bond, 'ow many rushed into matrimony utterly regardlesa .f the character or temper or other qualities of the one to whom they were at the moment so eager to be joined? Married in haste, they have, according to the proverb, repented at leisure. With five oat of every half dozen actions for breach of promise one can have little patience. A girl is well rid of a fellow who no longer loves her ; surely, it were better to discover the truth of the matter before the knot has been tied for life-or until goal ground for divorce can be alleged. Bet, however, let me illustrate. In Barrie'e delightful come:ly, "'the Profenor's 1•0111 Story," there is one Pete who wants to hack ont of an engagement to wed, and he resorts to a well-worn trick. Says he to his old sweetheart-" I'm a had one. I'm a drinkin', swearin' rascal ; in fact, I'm a</p>
        <p>' regular deevil." And what reply makes the maid? " Deeril or no deevil, Pete,' says she, " thel man for me." By-the-bye there's every reason to believe that in earlier ages brides were bought, not wooed—just, in fact, as they are among certain semi- , civilised peoples at the present day. As is writer upon the subject recently observed, there are many interesting references to the practice in the Pentateuch, and long before the time of Moses it in certain the aide of women, as wives, took place. Jacob paid for his wife, though he had her on credit, by service of seven years to Lilian. 'lhe Prince of Shalern made suit for Dinah by the offer of any amount of dowry that might be asked. Berodotus, who flourished about 450 years before Christ, gives a graphic account of the male of brides in his day, and as it obtained for long before his time, at ancient Babylon. Well. what of it all ? Dora no such thing ever happen even in oar own country in this twentieth century ? But I eta on dangerous ground, I must leave it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SEA-NYMPH.</head>
        <p>A SEA-NYMPH.</p>
        <p>By JUSTIN McCARTHYJ</p>
        <p>Author of "Dear Ludy Disdain," "Red Diamonds,• "Mold of Athens," "Glenora's Marriage," "Myra," tic.</p>
        <p>Linda Weston was a girl of tremendous energy. So, at least, all her young women-friends said. ' moet of whom displayed an energy far more tremendinis in going the round of the London season, and who could have run her off her feet in a mere fortnight of mall and early dance,. When there was no amusement going on, most of her women-friends had an unconquerable desire to he doing nothing in particular. But Linda could never be at net. She was not, to use the American phrase,'" built that way." She alwaye wanted to be doing good, or trying to do good for somebody or other. She must have had what • great states- man ascribed to another great statesman, "a passion of philanthropy." She made sad mistakes sonnet nine.; she helped the wrong p..ople, she was imposed noon by idioms and I bugs; she missed thereby many • chance of doing good; but her purpose all the same, used and unchangeable, was a resolve nit least to try and do good. She was an heiress, and a very self-willed helm. at that, living with a stately old aunt in • Went-end London rapier°, and she had lately been left mime considerable property in the South Coast village of Roxburgh-on-Sea. Linda was • young woman of twenty.two--tall, supple and handsome. In being tall she was emphatically a girl of today, when the audacious girls are growing to be On an average considerably taller than the linen. Linda innintained that the mere fact in itself sliewed the deliberate intention of Nature that the women should gradually come to be mulch taller than the men, and, in fact, to guide from their uperior height the movement of social life. Yet she never took any reins about the enforcement of woinen's rights, and always pleaded that she had enough to do in trying to help women to get through their duties. She was fond of riding and limiting and swimming, and she took to the bicycle, but chiefly as a means of enabling her to get through her round of visits at the houses of the poor. It would be needless to say that when Linda Weston found herself the owner of property in Roxburgh-on-Sea she hurried to the epot without her aunt, and accompanied only by her faithful amid, and settled herself down in the principal hotel in the place. It was jut at the opening of the wagon, and she fondly believed that this healthful seaside resort would afford her fresh and limiting means of doing good to her poorer brothers and sisten in humanity. Linda, of course, knew all the fuhionable watering-places by heart. She was familiar with Brighton and Folkestone and Eutbourne. She bad talked with painters at Whitby, and explored ruins at Scarborough, and delighted in the Valley of Rocks at Lyntori. Bat in all these places she W. no outsider, a passing visitor, who had no claim on the locality and no Eroprietorial right to try to do any good there. BM. in Roxburgh-on- Sea, she thought, as she sped along in the train towards that inviting spot, she would...be a sort of landowner, • kind of little queen in her own richt, with the privilege to make all her eubjects happy in her own way. She had always thought of a watering-place as a conglomeration of rich visitors, and pr sperous tradesmen who lived on the rich visitors, and bands and piers and nigger minstrels, and hard-working boatmen, and a con- siderable mass of neglected poor. She longed to be ministering to the comfort, the imaruction and the elevation of the poor at Itoxburgh-on-Sea. Only a day was needed to sinew her how brolly she IPtell chosen her ground, or rather how badly fortune—good fortune most persons would have called it—had chosen her ground for her when making her an owner of property in Roxburgh-on- Sea. In that happy watering-place there were absolutely no poor. There were practically no poor-mtes. There were very few boatmen, and those who were there were fishermen by profeseion as well, and" niade a very decent living for their fnmilin. There were no nigger minstrels—no, not one. There were two streets of shops in the place, and the shops were all doing a good buiness, owing to the fact that a large immber of wealthy people lived within eau distance of the town, and that the season always brought sith it • goodly crowd of well-to-do and futidious 'Miters, who risme there for jut the reason which, if she had known it, would have kept Linda out of the place —that there wa• no pauperism to be encountered in their wanderings by the seashore. Linda, in fact, had conic, if we may say PO, to the wrong shop. It was no if mono good Samaritan had by mistaken Nast. of energy made his way to • 'laid where here DMc was a traveller and never was a robber. The first visitor who called on Linda was ...laptain Gerald Cris.. Captain Craven's might no strictly deecribed ail an official visit, for he was the agent of the great landowners whose property covered nenrly the whole of Roxburgh-on-Sea, and as soon rie lie heard of the arrival of Miss Weston he felt bound to call imon her at once, although lie promised himself little personal pleasure by the visit. For some reason or other he had got it into his mind that Miss Weston most be en elderly person—not to say au old person--and lie had also coneinced himself in advance, and without any evidence whatever to go upon, that Miss Weston would burden him with suggestions and advice having for their object the screwing of niore money out of the local tenants. Now, in the first instance, Captain Gerald Craven wee a handsome young man, fond of bright society, and inc did not much care About old women, or even elderly women ; generally speaking, he preferred women to be young. In the next place, he believed that be had a positive genii]v for the management of the Roxhitrgh estate, and lie dot not think there was an old woninn in the world who could give hiin any hints on that subject worthy of his considera- tion. Therefore, when on sending up his card at the hotel he was shown to Min Weston's sitting- room and saw Miss Weston herself. he was greatly inclined to stammer unit something about haying expected tome her mother or her aunt. However, the motual introductions were arranged satisfac- torily, and Captain Craven soon made up his mind that on the whole he would rather discuss even the screwing up rent question with Mi-s, Weston than with her aunt, her mother, or her grand- mother.</p>
        <p>Miss Weston plunged at once into her favourite subject for the present. "0 Captain Craven!" she exclaimed, "I am so disappointed with your Roxburgh-on-Bea! " "Why not say your Roxburgh-on-Sea, Miss Weston? "he answered with *smile. "You own some part of it, at all events, and I don't own •ny of it." "1 don't care who owns it," she said im- periously. "I don't like the way in which it is managed. Where are your poor, I want to know ? " "Well, Miss Wmton, the fact is we haven't any poor." "Then what have you done with them ? Where have you driven them to?" "Well.' he explained, "I have just been saying that there are no poor in Roxburgh.</p>
        <p>"And I have jtlrst been asking 'what has become of them. Yon don't mean to tell me that there never were any poor in • piece like thin? Look at the great overcrowded towns • few miles off on either side. Why don't you encourage some of the poor from those horrid places to come and settle down by this lovely, lonely seashore, and help them to build nice, comfortable houses and to make themselves happy? 1 should call that the chity.of .landowners in a place like this." . . . .</p>
        <p>"'Well," he awaited, "'the principal landowners don't we tteir duty in that liaht. They have thought over the matter long and deeply, and, I ant.eure, in the most benevolent epirit." . . . . .</p>
        <p>"Benevolent!" she interrupted ecornf "Doss benevolence mean the screwing up of rent ? "1 wan clearly quite out in my judgment of thin young woman," mid Craven to himself. "Ifer sole object in coming here is not the ',crewing up of rent," and he did not like her any the less for it. "Some of them," he said aloud, "are known everywhere as the roost genuine philan- thropists, but they are convinced that to flood their place with the olfecourings of Mackerelville or Shrimptown would only destroy this dear little phew for the purposes to which it is deetined, without giving a particle of real relief to the pauperism of any other community." "And what are your benevolent purposes?" she asked, still with • scornful tone in her voice. "We went it," he replied, "to be • haven of refuge for quiet, orderly, educated folk, no matter how poor they may be. We allure them by no</p>
        <p>vulgar.- attraction.; we don't want the Piece swarming in the fine weather with the riff-raft of London. Look what we give the people here! We give them •plendid public gardens to walk in and where t!mir children may inlay. and we bare • nom! o rO sea where. any poor mecnanic arum</p>
        <p>Lcndon may come on the recommendation of one of the governing subscribere, and bit housed and fed nod cared for until he is well, at fly. shillings</p>
        <p>Tweet. Come. Mien Weston, &amp; think you Irk es!, when you here looked into Roxburgh affairs bulb more, that we haven't done hall badly with our trust."</p>
        <p>"I'll think it over," retlied Min Weston gravely I—as gravely as if the fate of half Europe depended on her sanction.</p>
        <p>.1 shall be happy," he said, rising to take his leave. .to show you. all over the whole place and explain to you the workings of all our inaitutions, and I am sure you would be led to work cordially with us."</p>
        <p>"I shall be delighted, Captain Craven, to 'ram all that I can, •nd I am core that I could no! have a better instructor. Ent you mutt find some- thing for me to do." "Oh, we ran easily find plenty for you to do. We have Sunday-80)mi1e, evening ',dumb., teeing schools; we have lectures, we have charitable ...mark we have jumble galee--" "I am afraid I don't quite see myself in any of thoee enterprises jitet at pre.ent,' replied Mout Weeton. "I never gave a lecture, and I should not know how to conduct nit self at a jumble sate; I couldn't tench in a ochool, I don't know any- thing to teach to anybody—oh, ye., by the way, I do. Lee my way clearly. I'll teach the girl* how to swim."</p>
        <p>• Cretan led &amp; bright smile. "I am sure you will do it well, and our girls along this shore are but poor hosed. at swimming. Walking into two feet of water about stomps them," he said, with a pardonable confusion of metaphor. Then they shook hands, and he left with the understanding that he was to begin his object- lessone in the institutions of Ito:burgh on the very neat day. . . . .</p>
        <p>Best 'day Captain Craven came and made dear to Linda the wlmle social structure of Roll.iirgh- on-Sea. Everything no far as it went seemed emooth and eatisfactory, and of course there wure no paupers to lie looked after. But Moss %Velem, was impreseed tnore than she had ever been before with the difference between ordinary poverty and art oat patiperiem. and in Itiablerch it mewed to her that of the brdinary poor, the decent poor— what vrould have been called in Germany "the shame-faced," or modest, "poor "--no account whatever was taken.</p>
        <p>"Look here, Captain Craven," she said in her energetic way; "it mem, to me that you are all dying in the face of Providence here. You are only concerned that the prosperous shall he kept in their prosperity, and that no paupers shall come to worry you with their complaints. Something ought to be done, if only to show that we are all brothers and sisters. I couldn't stand the present condition of thing. if I were living here, and I mean to live here, and I inow'f stand it!" _ . _</p>
        <p>Captain Craven' smiled at her kindling earneet. net., but thoroughly admired her enthusiasm. "Do what you think beet," was all he said. So Linda went to work at the first practical scheme that lied come into her mind--the one for which she felt rare she was best gratified. The more the work went on the better Linde Weston liked it. She found that. the girl, of Itoxburgh-on.Sea knew little of the art of swimming and still lee. of the rather more important net of floating. Linda was great on the importance of floating. "Now look here, girl.,' she would say to her pupil., when they gathered round her of a morning preparatory to their plunge into the sea ; "don't be filly enough to suppom that any of you could save her life by swimming in the open sea. Learn to keep youreelvm afloat—that is the best thing you can do--and wait until some help can conic to you; and if help doesn't come you would have little chance by swimming; for even Jenny Derrick there "—a very tall, strong girl— "wouldn't have much chance of laving hermit by swimming half across the Atlantic, or even half acrou the English Channel.• So Mies Weston devoted herself mainly to teaching the art of float- ing, bra .he certainly did not neglect the peel' •• nary craft of swimming. It has been said that hardly any of the girls whom Linda took in charge were really able to swim. These were, of course, the girls belonging to the place—the native girls, if one may OM that phrese to describe them. Linda did not propose to Imre anything to do with the young Mies who vieited itoraurgh in the season, and who came chiefly from London. Them fair beings vere not in her line. They could have the best teachers they wanted, and mod of them could swim very well, after the conventional fashion of the waside resort. But Linda's poor girls when she took them in hand had very little idea of hail, ing beyond that of running into the water up to their hoer., and giving little screen., ana occasionally plumping down into a sitting ;endure and getting up again and nicking for the bathing machine. Linda offered niany attractions to them to come in and be taught. Of course, she made no charge for her instruction, and the bathing- hence were at her own expense. But mom than that, she provided every girl with a pretty bath- ing costume—not expensive, indeed, but very picturesque, and admirably adapted for the pur- pose of swimming and floating—and each girl was expected to keep the costume neatly mended and in good order. Linda enrolled in her list the daughtern of any of the email tradespeople or workpeople who applied for • place on it, and, indeed, she accepted any girl who was recom- mended by the local ladies. Miss Weston was to be seen every day at the head of her school of sea-nymphs, She went out very early in the morning, to avoid the eye. of idle gazers, and alto to enable the girls to get to their household or other work in time. Most of them soon learnt to take to the water as if they had been amphibiora Linda became very proud ol the little army of Nereids who followed her movement. this way, that way, and the other about the bay, and who now swam, now floated, now dived, now engaged in attempts to rescue each other, now purpneely upeet boats for the sake of practistng how to put them right pin, and to climb into them easilv when they errre put right; and all this at Miss Weeton's word of command. Them girls had never had so happy • time before, and Linda was as happy as the happiest ',mon,/ them.</p>
        <p>Naturally, all thin, however quietly conducted and however prudently reserved for the early morn- ing, began to make considerable talk in Roxburgh- on-Sea, and the language of comment was not always that of approval. "Look here, Ceptein Craven," said the Hon. Mrs. Lightwood at a very select little dinner-party in the house of one of the local smart people; "I no wanted to ask you about all this rubbish they tell me of.</p>
        <p>"What rubbish, Mrs. Lightwood? &amp;eked Craven with a benignant mile. "And who has shot the rubbish hero? " "Well,</p>
        <p>7t,n!r great „..friend Mime We•tan:</p>
        <p>tell me—thin latn't addition to our community." "What in she doing ? " lie had not the slighteet doubt no to Mrs. Lightwood•e meaning, but he 'mold not give her the satisfaction of admitting that he knew. "But don't you know molly? Oh, eurely you most know that she goes out every morning in the bay with • gang of dirty, draggle-tailed girls behind her, whom @he is teaching to swim and to ' dive and to make absurd, indecent exhibitions of themselves—a lot of servants-of-all-work, and the I daughter. of journeymen baker. and tinker, and the like, and that she minis about henelf at tin bead of her ragged little rabble " Captain Craven laughed. He was thinking in his own mind what a very vulgar, narrow-minded old penon Mn. Lightwood wu, although the daughter of a peer. "Really. Mrs. Lightwood, you are r inning away with the story " "Am I? Very well, then, tell me the story yourself; tell it to no all. We shall all be glad to linen and to hare our wrong impression. nor- ' noted," she added, in the tone of • woman who feels eure that her own impreeeions never could be corrected by any authority. "Well, it', a very simple story," Captain Craven replied. "Miss Weston is an enthasieffit about many things—" "Enthusiuts bore me? • said Mrs. Lightwood. "No doubt they do," Captain Craven blandly resented. "Won't the whole story bore you? Hadn't we better lesiva it untold?" "Oh, no " the bootees called out. "We are dying to hear all about it." Thus encouraged, Craven very willingly went on. You see, Miss Weston has a good deal of property here, and she has gut into her head mime sort of idea about pt.:party having its duties a. well as its rights— "l've heard that sort of thing before," inter- rupted Mn. Lightwood scornfully. "And no she hu been thinking what ,he could do to benefit tome of our poorer friend.; and as *be ie a magnificent swimmer, ehe thought she could do nothing better than teach • lot of the poorer girlo of ltoxbuegh how to swim. I don't see any particular hum in that—do you?" Not I. certainly," said the bootee. "I think iii, • capital idea. "But think.• Mrs. Lirhtwood curled. "how it mat tooter the vanity of thou poor createreat Fancy her giving them ehowy and expensive bell- ing-tire...l al for nothing!" "Tile bathing-dresses," said Ceptain Crave°, "are not showy, nor expeneive, and the girls have u wend them and keep them in order themselves."</p>
      </div>
