        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>TARIFF " REFORM" DEBATE. Mr England a "Free Fooder."</p>
        <p>On, local Parliament has once more addressed itself to the consideration of the all-pbeorbing &amp;cal querition. At Tuesday evening's mooting of tne Debating Society, held in Cairo-street Church Parlour, Mr. W. Furnesa (Secretary for Foreign Affairs) introduced the follow- tnt reso`ution, before • fairly large •ttendanoe : This House, being of opinion that our present fiscal system Is no longer adapted to the requiremente of the country In view of the changed condition of interns- 1 tinsel cvmmeroe, resolyse</p>
        <p>( That a system of preferential tariffs between this country and the Colonies should to ndopted In order to promote the commercial and industrial union of the Empire. (2) That duties should be imposed noon the importa- tion of foreign manufacture, into Grunt Britain end Ireland by which means alone it will Impossible to ensure more work end better wage. for our people. Mr. Ft:asses appeal to the members to tear the mil of party colour from their faces and to consider the qtreetiou in the intonate of the country and of the Empire es a whole. For generations we bad been pour- ing out enormous tremors to consolidete our Imperial possessions but the snowily wee that we were in no better poeitiou for it, except in the case of Camds, than uny other nation. We bed sacrificed lives and treature and our Continental neighbours had come in and reaped the benefit of our expenditure. By our indifference to ths efforts which were being made to ivomote • closer commercial union with the Colonies we were neglecting our birthright and doing ourselves an injostice. We had bed striking evidence of that within the lan fortnight, America having seised the opportuoity of making • commercial treaty with Canada. While the ostensible object was to improte the trading conditions between the two countries, America did not disguise the fact shot there was the ulterior motive that (howl&amp; might ultimately become pert of the Celled States. " Question.") While we had discoursged Cansdian overtures end refused to grant • conceasion of 3d. a hu.bel America bed gone a step further and allowed 7p. a bushel. We had thus lost a powerful lever for drawing the Colonise closer to the mother country. We were told that tariff reform would mean dear food. Under Free Trifle, the price of food in this country had increased during the last few years by between 20 and 30 per cent. On the Continent loud price. had advanced by 22) per cent., while wages had gone up 371 per cent. (A VOICE : "About time they did.") In this country wages had not risen a tingle cent. (" Question.") Mr. Lloyd-Oeorge mid that l'rotective tariffs increased the cost of living to such en extent in Germany that the setleries of the &amp;lends had had to t.e. raised ; they hod even had to raise the Were of the Kohler. (Lnitebter.) While it w admitted. con- tinued Mr. Furness, that the cost of living had risen in Germany, the social and economic conditions were such that they conld advance the stages of °Violate. (Laughter and a Voice "So can we in )Varrington"). Having quoted figures to show that the em igration from England was larger than that from Germany, Mr. Furness said it was time we ignored the Free Trade fetish and did something to meet the increased competition to which we were being subjected by foreign countries without any compensating advsntages from the Colonies. The . . • . . _</p>
        <p>_ witi:drawal of Protection from Ireland had mesot the decay of her industriee and the increasing exporta- tion of her rural population. it was not the potato famine alone which was responsible for the emigretti,n. (A voioe : Rotten landlordiam.") Free Trade principle* were largely responsible. If they gave Ireland a Illeaellre Of Protection to-morrow she would increase her wealth and population. Continuing, Nfr. Furors mid we were allowing the foreigner to Come in and reap where welled gown. What would have been the fate of Japan had she been content to go on :n the mom rut %%kb her advancing civilization she had come into line with tariff reform countrim on the Contineet and we in England were beginning to feel the pinch of her tariffs. Wan it not deplomble that ire had almost to go down in our knees to obtain con- cessions from a country which twenty or thirty years ago Wan ?</p>
        <p>°The Vrt M id IN7bT-1.11 (Mr. W. 11. Stansflell) seconded the.reso)</p>
        <p>A N (I, Burton-on-Trent) said the Fareign Secretary looked at matters from a very selfish point of view when he represented that we bed expended blood and treasure on acquiring the Colonies, simply for what what we could get out of them. Our present fiscal aystem had stood the strain and teat of time better than the fiscal systems of other nations, as was proved by the fact that we recovered from cycles of depression more rapidly thaw other countriss. Our population was on the whole better paid and worked shorter hours than tbe workers in highly protected countries. An exception could be made in the case of America where, however, the higher wages were far exceeded by the ertra cost of living. Our foreign trade was continually on tbe increase. It was not either dead or dying. In 1909 we Imported manufactured goods to the extent of £2 Hie. ltd. per head of the population, Germany CI Ss. 2d. per bead, and the United Suttee £l3s. sd. per head. We exported msnufacturee to the extent of £6 14a. 4d. per heed. Germany £3 9,. per heed, and the United State. £1 14s. 2d. Those figured proved that we were more than holding our own. At the last election the Conservatives stood up for one principle— that they did not intend to increase the cost of living. (Hear, hear.) It was, however, ateolutely impossible to give the Colon les pref eren dal tariffs without taxing food and raw materials. A Voir, : "We do it now.") He admitted that toe small extent we did tax food but under tariffs It would be taxed to a much more serious extent. The millers told us in a straightforward way thst A 2e.tax on corn would mean an increase in the price of mead. If a duty of 5 per cent, were placed on each Cwt_ of butter. it would mean an advance of Id. per lb. The tariff reformers also failed t, specify where they drew the distinction between raw materials and manufactured article.. If we were not to import manufactured goods, what would become of our great shipping industry? We were told that our system of /me imports was criminal to our own population because it tended to keep people out of employment. If that were correct—end he denied it—was it not a scandalous Mame tint we should expect to be MAP. facturers for the whole world, that we should, in short, do unto others whet we would not have them do unto on?</p>
        <p>711r7i:. ENt:LAND (1., Warrington), said that they were all prepared to admit that the conditions in thin country were Oct altogether mtlefactory ; bet at the acme time they were much more mtistaotory than the .itate of affairs in any other country in the world. Englend was the greatest °Woo and the British were the lines. race on the face of the earth. He did not token pessimistic view of their future. The throat question was one of the most dricult problems which had been before us for many generation. As Mr. Gladstone said of the drink question, it seemed beyond the wit of man to wive it. There was such a coo- Sliming variety of opinionn not only in this country but abroad. garland was never so prosperous as to-day, but that was not entirely owing to our Free Trade system. On the other hand he was not prepared to say that tariff reform would cure all the evils in .our midst. Though he was not prepared to &amp;meet the iGerman fiscal system in its entirety, we could in some respects learn lemons from that country. Those who were unbiassed on the subject most acknowledge that -there won less unemployment in Germany than in this country. ("Question.") They had statement. of the the leading supporters of Free Trade to the effect that there was lem unemployment in Germany. Mr. Lloyd George had distinctly said so ; Mr. Winston Churchill in reply to a question in the Boum of Compotes, made *similar edanimion. and Mr. Ramsay Macdonald told no the some. No nation appeared to be really satisfied with its Mind system. They heard murmurings in the Coited Slates and Germany, as well as in this country, in connection with the food question. The world was beginning to be at one on that issue. He believed that Ca tbe question of food taxes the tariff reformers were distinctly In error. lie wee an sheolute free fooder. Me did not believe in any food taxes nor did the people of Germany believe in them. If there could be a tree democratic poll throughout the world—in Germany they had not a demoorntio franchise—he believed there would not be a verdict in favour of food taxes. If they were going to build up • virile 'ration there must be no burdens on the necensries of life. Food taxes would appear to he a good thing for farmers, but he esid with all earnestness and sincerity that the profits would not go into the pockets of the limners but to benefit the land owners. Taxa, on food would ben burden upon the workers whether they worked on the land or in other Industries. The sooner the Tariff Reform party got rid of the Idea of taxing food the better it vrould be for their programme He also advised them to discard the project of bundler up an imperial federation on a trade bubo. The oolonum and the mother country could not be naked together on those lines.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. St.:\ V (S., St. Ives) said the resolution was an attempt at 111 resurrection of tariff reform. The Foreign Secretary had just been converted to that policy in time to turn up It the funeral. tLetighter.) The framers of the resolution had shown a wise discretion in evoiding details. They would probably nil agree with a system which would promote the commercial and industrial onion of the Empire, but they wanted to see the system before committing hemselres. The country at the last three elections a! shown that it wee fairly well satisfied with the existing fiscal conditions. They did not went duties on goods, but they wanted taxes taking off tea, • .gar and CoCOI4 If our workpeople were paid the wage. which they earned there would be no necessity to talk eta Protectice system to remedy their conditions. According to Lord Wolmer this cormtry, although it was paying the hues of all the other countries— flaughteri—was still the richest country in the world. The British workman was better off than any of his foreign competitors. That, however. W. not so much to England's credit as to the foreigner's distr.°. Mr. P. MARRON (N., Newry) said it was surprhit,</p>
        <p>that Germany, who, according to the Protectionists, could make the foreigner pay her taxes, had such • greet difficulty in raising sofficieot revenue for her national purposes. Evidently the foreigner was not " stumping op' very well. (Laughter.; The Cm• servatives ought to be straightforward and any that their real object in preening tariff reform wee to try and do away with the land usu. The people of England, however, wanted to return to the old system under which land bore its proper share of taxation. The PRIME at ISIsTER (Mr. W. ll.Stsufleld) said the object of Tariff Reform wee to put our own country first and to do unto others as they did unto on. At present we were nghting with our hands tied. It wan uked hew Cr managed to he the wealthleat country in the world ? Hermosa we were the first in the field and we had got loid by a great store of wealth made in former times. ," Oh.") %Ye were the arra conntry in the world In industrial wOduCtion. We were also the only country that wan able to carry on its manufactures In the time of strewn when all the other countries of Europe were being ruined by the Napoleonic wars. We got a fifty year's start and it took our competitors • long time to catch up to on. He granted that Pros Trade helped us for a time but we had grown now and we had to change our methods, just se tradesmen in Warrington had to chugs their methods In face of new competition. It was constantly asserted that Tnriff Reform was dead. (Henr, hear.) He admitted there were people who were trying to strangle it. A foul attempt at ausesination had been made by the Liberal party, and there would be an ingoiry into it before the jery. (Laughter.) The Liberals preached so often that Tariff Reform wan dead that, like murderers, they were determined to do it to death if then could. Tony had nearly succeeded in one part of the Empire. The conditions, in Cundet to.dny were (rough to cause n feeling of shame to any tree Englishman. Here was a great continent at °tor very doom, which had offered an time after time better conditions of trade end is deapair at the policy of the Liberals, it wee now drawing clout to America. Mr. Taft said that if the Tariff Reform party In England was eucceesful. American reciprocity with Canada would be melee. and Imperial federetion would become a fact. That was why he was hurrying forward the treaty. The Liberal party knew they wore selling this country for their party system ,Liberal cries of "Shame" and "Withdraw.") Deep down in their hearts, be believed the beet of them were ashamed of It. (Laughter). There was also a rumour that South Africa was going to withdraws from any preferential arrangement. The Conservatives believed in sentiment but they believed that the Empire could be more strongly bound together on a trade basis. Why should we tax oureelves to maintain this Empire for the benefit of foreign countries? If the Colonies were not going to federate on a trade bards, why not let them become independent countries and protect themselves ? He did not suppose anybody wished to tax food, hut they must tax something. (A voice "Tat incomea.") They were not going to tax the food from the colonies. They were going to tax the wheat which came from foreign countries, In favour of our Colonies, and he could see no danger in that. Mr. JARMAN : You can't have • preferential tariff if no tariff °vista rhe PRIME MINIsTeR : Preferential tariffs menu— Mr. J ACM S.S4 A small tax for the Colonies and • large one for the foreigner.</p>
        <p>The PRIME MISISTER It means we prefer goods from our Colonies to those from foreign countries. We will tax the foreigner and let the goods from the Colonies come In free or with a email tax. Mr. P. O'BareN (L.' St. Pencras) said no one was more strongly opposed to tariff reform than be, bet he asked those who shirred his views not to bag themaelves with satisfaction the assumption that tariff reform_ was dead. The incresee of wages is Germany walk not doe to it. fiscal system, but to the pressure brought to bear by the Labour Party in cons.- quence of the International Labour conferences. Do,ing the past twenty-ftve years wages in the United SWAN bed fallen bemuse of the unrestricted competition of labour which had emigrated there from other countries. No matter how high they built tariff walls, if there I were three men for one job, that competition was the barometer which regulated weges. There was a very large section of opinion in the United States in favour of a reduction of tariffs, and the democratic party bad fought nearly every election for twenty.flve years on that qurtim.t. . _</p>
        <p>31r:Y. A. JRNRIVS (C., Waltoo D(vision) moved the adjournment of the Fiouse until next Tuesday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>GIFTS TO CONGREGATIONALISM.</head>
        <p>GIFTS TO CONGREGATIONALISM.</p>
        <p>Large Subscriptions Announced at Liverpool.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool on Wednesday Mr. and Mrs. W. If. Lever entertained the leading members in the city and district of the Congres ational denomination to luncheon Many subscription. were made to the central fund of the Union now being raised to increase the minimum salaries of ministers to £l2O per year. Amongst the &amp;mations announced were :—.C10,000 from Mr. W. M. Lever ; £5,000, Colonel W. W. Piilsington ; £2,000, Ano.nyr.n”l., i:inthport ; El.ooo, Mr. A. W. Paton. ... I</p>
        <p>—Mr. W. H. Lever said he did not believe in philan- thropy. He had the strongest repugnance to anything that bad the taint of philanthropy or charity about it. He believed that roost philanthropic and charitable institutions were entirely on wrong line. Perhaps • rich man died who would not give anything in bin lifetime, and, not being able to take bin rooney with him, he left hi. money to charitable institotions, which were carried on by masterlyn who were just as much paupers as the pauper. who benelitted by the charity the rich man'. looney was expected to help. Therefore, if the object they were met to assist had any taint of organised obesity or philanthropy about it he certainly would hare had nothing to do with it. lint let them get rid of the idea that to help thom who required help at any period of life need of necesnity be philmthropy or charity. If, though it was not; he thought it. wan more often a duty they owel to themnelves mors than to those needing help. There was no philanthropy or charity in a corporation paving or lighting the streets of a town or in the Government providing harbours or erecting lighthouses. He thought they would agree with him that in the past they es Christiana—and he believed Nonconformista were the greatest sinner@ in thin respect—bad each considered only his or bee narrowsellish way of getting to heaven, and had quarrelled with and persecuted any. body who disagreed with them, and especially their minister. It was tires that churches ceasel to think of !their own particular isms and had for their aims aa; I objects the true act of worship and the true basis of , religion. They should care for the ministers in the rural districts who were working under such great difffoulties. If ministere were engaged like lawyern,for the plaintiff and defendmt, he thought this question would satin itself ; all would be auxious to secure their eluvial!! for either one aide or the other. and !era, he thought, would roll up to .01110 decent proportion. It was not charity, it was not philanthropy, it was our duty and it wise on the grounds of duty alone that he made his appeal to them. .e. .</p>
        <p>-13.--J-O;es, of Bournemouth, appealing for donations, remarked that in the village manses many. tragedy went on about which very little was known in the outside world.</p>
        <p>I It was- annotine,ed that of tbe 1150,000 naked for £157,000 had already been promised, of which over £90,000 had been paid in. Yesterday's contributions at Liverpool, including those already mentioned, advanced the fund by nearly L 20,000.</p>
      </div>
