      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MR. J. E. NEWNS As GASPARD.</head>
        <p>MR. J. E. NEWNS As GASPARD.</p>
        <p>Photos I</p>
        <p>tion, bas borne good fruit. He has subjected the company to a course of very careful training and the result is a performance which is full of life and spirit, and goes with a merry swing. On its musical side the opera is also eminently pleasing, the chorus being nicely balanced and well drilled. The many ear haunting numbers are interpreted with intelligence and expression, and the attack is crisp and whole-hearted--an evidence of the effective musical coaching which the performers have received from Mr. F. H. Crossley, the gifted conductor of the Warrington Musical Society. The principals have, generally speaking, been well chosen and several who have figured with acceptance in previous operas add to their laurels. Mr. John E. Newns is entrusted with the important role of Gaspard, the miser—one of the most difficult that an amateur can attempt. He is to be congratulated upon a remarkably clever study in which there is not the slightest element of caricature, the realism of his acting gripping the audience during the whole of the time he is on the stage. His "make up" is perfect and, whether as the tottering and apparently poverty stricken old peasant trying to conciliate the officious Bailie, or as the miser gloating o%er his hoard, he is powerful and convincing. His great triumph is won in the second act when, just as he is feasting his eyes upon his riches, he is terror-stricken by the clanging of the old bells and the appearance of a " phantom " host. Mr. Newns rises to the occasion and, although a more frenzied intensity would improve the portrayal, he does brilliantly, especially when it is borne in mind that his previous roles have generally been of a light humorous eharacter. The effect of the scene is somewhat marred by the 'semi-jocular "asides," in tremulous tones, of the spectator in shining armour.whileGaspard is grovelling over his gold. The better con- ception of this episode is that it should the place in a tense and awe-inspiring silence. Miss Carrie Hastings is a most engaging Serpolette—the saucy, vivacious waif who for a brief period is permitted to enjoy the dignity of a countess She takes the part in a fascinating and natural way, and is respon-</p>
        <p>Ironge.</p>
        <p>acts with freedom and confidence and is particularly amusing when conducting the hiring fair. Mr. Harry Griffiths throws himself with enthusiasm into the part of the happy-go-lucky Grenicheux, the fisherman, and achieves a considerable measure of , success, as far as acting is concerned. On I Wednesday he did not do full justice to the , songs allotted to him, but this was probably I due to the fact that he was suffering from a I cold. Miss Amy Rowe is a bright and self- I possessed Christophe,a page. The following I are a gay bevy of village maidens :—Manette, Miss N. Shaw; Jeanne, Miss F. Bennett; Gertrude, Miss A.Warburton :Susanne, Miss I F. Delahay ; Catherine, Miss J. Woodland; Marguerite, Miss E. Hazlehurst. We do not think sufficient is made of the dramatic possibilities of the denouement in the last act, the startling disclosures of identity being received by the principals most interested, with comparative unconcern. Acknowledgement must be made of the, help of the understudies :—Miss E. Wright' (Serpolette), Miss J. Woodland (Germaine), and Miss E. Hough (Christophe). As already indicated, the chorus is entitled to no small share of praise for the success of the opera. Some exceedingly pretty stage pictures are presented, notably the merry bustling scene in the market place, the wierd and mysterious opening of the second act I when torch-bearers patrol the dark rooms of ' the old chateau singing that effective chorus 'Let our torches," and th 3 rustic revelry in the orchard. The last act is introduced :with a simple but very charming dance by a troupe of garland girls, whose training reflects great credit on Mrs. B. S. Townroe. The orchestra plays with capital unity of purpose and does not obtrude itself unnecess- arily, the accompaniments being well sub- ordinated to the voices of the soloists. Owing to ill-health Mr. E. B. Ogden, the conductor, has been unable to lead the society's forces and Mr. A. Trow, who courageously stepped into the breach at the last moment, has discharged the onerous duties with skill and enthusiasm. Captain G. R. Crosfield, the popular president of the society, has taken a keen personal interest in the usdertaking and Mr. B. S. Townroe, hon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>treasurer and director, has willingly afforded invaluable assistance. Mr. G. B. Dean, the stage manager, deserve warm praise for the excellence of the' effects." Mr. J. Taylor is an energetic bu.inees manager, and lastly, but by no means least, Mr.G.Hall, the capable hon. secretary,has worked heart and soul for the enterprise.</p>
        <p>The attendance on Wednesday was not as large as might reasonably have been expected but the audience was highly appreciative. There were much larger audiences on Thurs- day and last eveningand the performance went with even greater smoothness than on the opening night. Friday was a special military night, when the society had the patronage of officers of the local regiments and of Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall,Mr.Harold Smith,M.P. and Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield. A crowded ' 'house" is expected at the concluding per- formance this evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>PEOPLE V. PEERS. VETO BATTLE IN COMMONS. Government's Great Majority.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE V. PEERS. VETO BATTLE IN COMMONS. Government's Great Majority.</p>
        <p>With the introduction of the Parliament Bill by Mr. Asquith in the House of Commons, on Tuesday, the first stage in the final battle between the people and the Peers was entered upon. Apart from its intrinsic importance, the Parliament Bill enjoys the distinction with- out precedent (except for the Budget of 1909-10)in modern legislation of having been considered in the fullest detail as well as in principle at the last General Election. The favourable verdict of the General Election was rendered all the more emphatic by the circumstance that the terms of the scheme are so simple as to be readily under- stood by the least discerning of electors. The terms are these:</p>
        <p>1. The House of Lords to be disabled by law from amending or rejecting a Money Bill certified to be such by the Speaker. 2. A Bill other than a Money Bill passed in three successive sessions (not necessarily in the same Parliament) and three times rejected by the Lords may be then sent by the Commons direct for Royal Assent. 3. The duration of Parliament to be limited to five years. As Mr. Asquith made clear in his intro- , ductory speech, there are ample safeguards: against rash or precipitate legislation. As regards "reform " of the House of Lords, Mr. Asquith was emphatic that the evils of the present system must be swept away before any scheme of the kind is attempted. Moreover, there was not a hint of "compromise " in his speech. It is to be the Bill, the whole Bill, and nothing but the Bill.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour's speech in reply was inter- preted as an indication that the Lords will adopt a policy of "no surrender."</p>
        <p>PLAN OUTLINED.</p>
        <p>Our plan (said the Premier) as embodied in this Bill is a very simple one. It proposes to give statutory definition and protection to the constitutional doctrine that the House of Commons is supreme in finance. At the same time it affords adequate safeguards against possible abuse in the guise of what is known as "tacking." Further, it cuts down the absolute veto ot the Lords to a suspensory veto with the provision for the lapse of at least two years and three sessions, not necessarily in the same Parliament, and a limitation of the term of the life of the House of Commons to five years. (Cheers.) Mr. Asquith derided the suggestion that the Government intended to enable a despotic single Chamber to ride roughshod over the electorate. (Hear, hear/. They had guarded against any danger of that kind by proposing to shorten the duration of Parliament, so that the House of Conrmons would always be either fresh from the polls or looking for- ward to going to the polls, while the introduc- tion of the suspensory veto would preclude the possibility of converting or arbitrarily smuggling into law measures which defied or were condemned by public opinion, and would ensure ample opportunity for the reconsideration and revision of hasty or slovenly legislation. Moreover it was as certain as daylight that in 99 cases out of 100 cases the House 'of Commons could and would reverse legisla- tion which had been shown by a general elec- tion to be opposed to the will of the mass of the electors. (Cheers). WHAT TORIES WILL NOT DO.</p>
        <p>In winding up for the opposition Mr. I Balfour said anxious as they were that all I should go quietly and smoothly, there were some things Unionists could not do and which they would not do and which, if they were done, must be done over their heads. (Opposition cheers.) He would never be responsible by falling in with any agreement which would make him a party to forcing upon the people of this country a change such as this in the Constitution which they did not desire.</p>
        <p>The people desire a change in the exist- ing relations between the two Houses. (Hear, hear.) They did desire a modification of the constitution of the House of Lords.' They did not desire that the House ofl Commons and the Ministry which ruled that House should use those great reforms and changes as an instrument for bringing about a revolution upon which the people had never been consulted and about which the people knew nothing. (Opposition cheers and Ministerial counter-cheers.) Mdch as I desire peace, said Mr. Balfour in conclusion, and anxious as I am to ensure it, and gladly as I would do much in the way of compromise, there are some things, some issues, so great that no compromise is possible. (Loud cheers). If you are going to use the desire of this country to have some change in the relations between the two Houses as an instrument for getting something they do not desire, we on this side will have no part or lot in your ,plan, and we should think ourselves dis- raced for ever if we gave it our support. (4oud Opposition cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>VOTE.</head>
        <p>THE VOTE.</p>
        <p>'ND READING CARRIED BY 124. . first reading of the Bill was agreed to nesday night in the House of 1 s, the House dividing as follows : r the Bill 3M ainst</p>
        <p>People's majority 124 Bill was brought in by the Prime r amid tumultuous Ministerial cheers Icene of great enthusiasm. Liberal, and Irish members leaped to their I waved bats and papers, cheering iiiith again as he left the House. the watchman cried, "Who woes " Mr Swift Mac Neill said : "The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>at an early date to lay on the table a Bill amending the constitution of the House of Lords. The Bill is expected to follow the lines of the Lansdowne resolutions of last December—surrendering the financial veto, providing for a joint session if the two Houses disagree on other Bills, and for a referendum "if the matter is one of great gravity and has not been adequately sub- mitted to the judgment of the people.' In the debate which preceded the division Mr. F. E. Smith. in a brilliant, but superficial speech, declared with rather grotesque vehemence that "nearly half the people in the country will support the Opposition in any resistance, however desperate." He ridiculed the " mandate " andslaimed that the Government had not a majority or an independent policy. Perhaps the cleverest part of the speech was when Mr. Smith dissected the elerrente of the coalition and endeavour'd to play on.° off against the other. It was not new, of course, but it was well done. No doubt, declared the brilliant advocate of Tory demo- cracy, the Prime Minister believed in the preamble of his Bill, but unfortunately the Prime Minister had meant many things with sincerity and honesty that he was unable to carry out.</p>
        <p>There was, however, less of sparkling epigram than usual. In all Mr. Smith's speeches nowadays one can trace a consistent effort to lose the first reputation he made as a biting humorist and to establish himself as a serious debater. He lot himself go once or twice—as, for instance, when he talked of the Government "slobbering over the back- woodsmen when they wanted them to preside at their county meetings in aid of the territorials," and when he spoke of the Irish party as having been in the political market for 25 years -</p>
        <p>But towards the end of his speech he asked eagerly whether the Liberal Government would not be satisfied with the success which it had already obtained—a success greater than was ever achieved " by the vitupera- tive genius of O'Connell or the convincing eloquence of Gladstone." He entreated them to base legislation on the bedrock of national consent, and not upon the shifting qtlick- sands of party requirements. MR. CHURCHILL'S TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Churchill's speech, addressed to a crowded House, between ten and eleven , o'clock, was a great piece of oratory. But perhaps the most significant incident of the 'speech was the demonstration that followed lon his opposition to the idea of another Con- ference. 'lf we entered upon such a 'Conference," he said, "we should not have fifty supporters." That opinion was con- firmed by the whole of the Coalition party in a burst of terrific cheering which lasted a considerable time.</p>
        <p>As for the alleged disunion of the Coalition, what about the Tory Party? "Is the noble lord "—this to Lord Hugh Cecil—"quite sure that the two pillars of the Opposition—the Church and the licensed trade—have a mission identical in all respects ? " As for the Referendum Mr. Churchill thought it might be used for attacks on property—say, nationalisatied of land—a backhanded argument that delighted the House. The Referendum would lead to "disaster, Jacobinism, and anarchy." "That is the depth to which the Tory Party his been led in the eight years since the resigna- tion of Lord Salisbury "—a blow which seemed to touch Mr. Balfour keenly. Another fact of great importance was his closing sentence: "We shall neglect no step that may be necessary to carry this Bill into law." A MEMORABLE SCENE.</p>
        <p>Then came the Division! The scene that followed reminded one of some of the greatest Parliamentary episodes in the Home Rule fight of 1893. When the figures were announced, giving a majority of 124 for the Bill, the Progressives began a demonstration which continued for a long time. The Tories returned to the House in quite small num- bers, looking very depressed. The Prime Minister walked to the Bar with a copy of the Veto Bill, and as be passed up the Houle to give it to the Clerk the Liberals began to rise from their seats. The idea spread through the House like lightning, and in a moment the Liberals and Radicals, the Labour members and the Irish, were on their feet cheering wildly, some of them even waving pocket handkerchiefs and hurrahing. The scene continued, for some minutes, and when the House had gradually quieted down, and the voice of the clerk was heard proceeding to read the next item on the orders of the day, there was a burst of laughter, and the House emptied into the lobbies.</p>
        <p>So ended the first great scene in the great Veto struggle of 1911. May all the others be equally victorious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MILTON IN PARR°</head>
        <p>MILTON IN PARR°</p>
        <p>ie a phrase taught to every scholl child as illustrative of concentration, or "much in little" Now the Multum in Parvo " of the food world is the Current. How little, but bow goal ! Whilst growing it is smiled upon by the glorious S.,uthern sunshines deriving the inestimable benefice of the generous rave and the pure clear air of an incomparably beautiful climate. Then, when thoroughly ripe, the grape is dried by the heat of the sort that mastered it. Nature, and Nature alone, contrives to provide us with the treasured concentrated sunshine wrapped up in the Currant. That is why Currant, are so nutritioue, no pleasing to the taste, and altogether desirable that tbe highest medical audio, ities recommend them for constant use. Not on Occasions should Currants be eaten, but regularly. Countless delicacies can he made with Currants. All are wholesome and cheap.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
