      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SAITRDAY. FEBRUARY 25, 1911.</p>
        <p>One effect of the first</p>
        <p>THE NEW day's debate on the DEVELOPMENT. Parliament Bill was to change the political ' situation. The idea of bringing forward in the House a measure for the reform of that body on the lines of the Rosebery and Lans- downe resolutions had been definitely aban- doned. But after the speech made by the PRIME MINISTER on Tuesday, it was at once recognised that it is necessary for the Oppo- sition to produce something in the nature of an alternative to the proposals of the Government. Mr. BALFOUR seems almost at once to have left the House of Commons to consult with Lord LANSDOWNE, who has now given notice that he intends to lay on the table of the House of Lords a Bill amending the constitution of that assembly. This is the straightforward and the courageous course. There was something really pitiable in the forsaking :of the resolutions which were adopted during the last session of Parliament. Dropped in that manner, they wore the aspect of a temporary and insincere, device. This was no doubt felt by thel Unionist leaders no less than by their political antagonists, and now they have resolved on the only course which they could honourably pursue. The Government and its supporters will, of course, offer no objection to proposals for reforming the constitution of the House of Lords, except so far as such proposals may appear to fall short of their professed object. It was a great thing to obtain from the Upper Chamber, as was done last session, an admis- sion that the Peers do not at present form a body which is fully competent to perform the duties which it considers itself privileged to discharge. It is a still greater thing to have I it admitted, as is done by Lord LANSDOWNE 's notice, that the reform of the Upper Chamber is a matter of urgency. The Peers might possibly themselves do what is promised in the preamble of the Parliament Bill. They might hit upon a scheme of reform which would be accepted by the Government. This is not probable. but it is something that it is not past hoping for. The mistake that might be made by Lord LANSDOWNE and his friends would be in believing that a scheme of reform could now take the place of the pro- posals which the House of Commons has been discussing these two days past. Reform will be welcome enough, but it cannot be an alternative to the limitation of the veto. It would be unlikely to extend far enough to remove the real grievance.</p>
        <p>It Was highly- necessary from the Con- servative point of view that some such policy as that contained in Lord LANSDOWNE's motion should be announced. We are not, we believe, unduly disparaging the ,Opposi- don when we say that there has been only one side to the debate in the House of Commons. So far as argument from the Opposition benches is concerned, the I duel might, with advantage and some saving of time, have been closed at the end of Mr. BALFOUR'S speech on Tuesday. The question, it must be admitted, is not one that lends itself to effective debate on Conservative lines. The wrong which the Government seeks to remedy is as obvious to</p>
        <p>the one side as to the other, and so, on the real matter at issue, it is never possible to come to close grips. Again, as one speaker said concerning the Referendum, if it were 'established the Lords would only be in favour of referring to the country those matters which were objectionable to themselves. That is a description of the old situation as well as the one that is still in nubibus. The Lords will take anything whatever from a Tory Government, and they maul or reject anything important that comes from a Liberal Government. That M not the true function of any Second Chamber—a fact which is asserted as much by Lord LANS- DOwNE'S notice ia by the PRIME MINISTER'S Bill.</p>
        <p>Unless there has been THE RATES AND a great saving in the RESERVE FUNDS. general administration of the Borough, a rise in rates of anything from 2d. to 3d. in the pound seems assured. Educational demands swallow up a sum equal to and the prospect is one at which the average ratepayer may well quail. Under the circumstances the suggestion made by Mr. K England, one of the late 'borough auditors,is worthy of serious atten- tion. Briefly, Mr. England's idea is that the Electricity and Tramways departments should each contribute £1,500 for the relief of the rates from their respective Reserve Funds. As these stand at .£9.000 and £B,OOO respectively, and the departments are paying their way in addition to maintaining plant out of revenue, it looks as if the money could well be spared. May be, too,the Gas Department could be squeezed a little tighter than usual under theextra- ordinary circumstances of the moment. On first thoughts it does not look sound finance to pay dividends out of Reserve Funds, hut the question arises: how farge a proportion of revenue may be justifiably locked up under such a head ? The ratepayers have certainly a right to expect substantial relief from the profit-earning departments of the Corporation and we shall be interested to hear what the Borough Treasurer and the Chairman of the Finance Committee have to say to the newest suggestion of robbing the hen-roost.</p>
        <p>So sure as we pass any THE CHURCH animadversions on tht AND THE Liquor Trade WE TRADE. receive a courteously</p>
        <p>worded letter frou either the Manchester or Liverpool districi which, in the most ingenious way imaginable tries to persuade our readers that WI are doing an injustice to the nationa industry of beer-selling and beer-drinking There seems to be a confederacy of persuasivo gentlemen who have taken the Trade to theb hearts in no half-hearted manner, and ac devoting themselves to defending it from th 4 aspersions of unprincipled teetotallers uk ourselves. Within certain limits, w welcome the contributions of these critic@ as they indicate the lines upon which th latest newspaper campaign is being con ducted. Last week we commented on th Rector's lucubration@ on the amount c drunkenness there is in Warrington, an, added that if the Trade " received les bolstering from both Church and State, th way would he easier." . .</p>
        <p>We are immediately assailed by a gentle man whose identity is concealed beneath th signature "A fortiori" which, interpretec means "with stronger reason." Our expon ent of reason says (you will find the 'Otte on page 12): "In the first place it can hardl be said that the Church bolsters up th Trade." The contradiction staggers us b its boldness, and by the absence of reason t support it. On reflection we can only imagir that the phrase is so unpalatable to " fortiori" that he must needs deny its trutt fulness whether he can or can not substantial his own statement by either facts, logi or reason. Perhaps, if we said the Trac bolstered up the Church it would suit liii better, as being rather more complimentar to the Trade. He may have it either wa: because the connection between Church at Trade, and the gratitude of one to tt other are so obvious, unmistakable at demonstrable that it would be folly to arm to the contrary. Proof of a peculiarl appropriate kind, occurs to us immediatel: In 1908, the Lord Bishop of Liverpo showed his freedom of spirit and thorougl ness of purpose by taking the chair at a hui gathering in Warrington in support of tl ' Government Licensing Bill. This was , measure that,' A fortiori" and his frien L hated with a hatred that had no bounds. T Bishop—who is Bishop of this diocese—w isolated 'and alone on the Temperance pis , form. His clergy were conspicuous • absent, for. the simple reason th only a fortnight before they had literal flocked to the platform of the Brewe, demonstration in the same building. Th, could not conscientiously support be sides so that went with the big battalions the Trade, and showed a praisewortl gratitude to the hand that fed them. The was a case of the Church bolstering the Trade in return for sulystanti bolstering of the Church by the Trade. was so significant that it will ever be frei in the memory of Warrington townsme Even politically the Church and the Tra, are great holsterers of the common fabric Conservatism, though our corresponde may not know it. Only this week the HOT Secretary made quite a passing allusion the " two pillars of the Opposition "--Chur and Trade. Even in the minds of Cabin Ministers they cannot be dissociated, and v feel sure that in death they will not divided.</p>
        <p>The trade returns TRADE RETURNS the past year are na FOR 1910. available for countries, and th show that Great Britain still leads the w, with a lot to spare. The following tal shows the values of imports and exports 1910, and the increases over 1909: 1211,,1T5.</p>
