        <p>•KUNZ: aa laorgesie c..rai.a.81.1.g Ms&amp; a withstands water. 61 poet. free.—Aavt.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hobhouse, on Mon- A BIIDGET STATE- day, made a MOSt</p>
        <p>MENT. important Budget statement on behalf of Mr. Lloyd George,whose return to the House in improved health has been welcomed by all Members, quite irrespective of their political views. Mr. Hobhouse was able to announce that for the year 1909-10, in spite of the Lords' disturbance of the normal revenue collection, there was a realised surplus of b1,300,000—a large increase on the estimate. The ratepayers will not be asked to recoup any of the cost of the pensions paid to those who had been formerly dis- qualified by Poor-law relief, but the I Exchequer will retain the half of the land- taxes yield, which was granted to the Local Authorities. A concession amounting to £400,000 is made to licence-holders,in order to , remedy any apparent grievance which they had under the new duties of the Budget of 1909-10.</p>
        <p>A hackneyed Tory SINGLE CHAMBER argument against the GOVERNMENT. Parliament Bill is that it will give Us Single- Chamber Government. That is true to a certain extent. When a Tory Government is in power there will still be the domination of a Single Chamber in spite of the limitation of the Lords' Veto. The Liberals have suffered for years from the domination of a single Chamber. When a Tory Govern- ment is in power, the House of Lords has never offered the slightest check to IPgislation—such as Mr. Balfour's Education Act—which was not only opposed to public sentiment, but was a violation of a distinct pledge. The Parliament Bill will obtain something like fair play for Liberal measures, though it gives ample scope for the Lords to ensure that no Bill can be hurried through Parliament without oppor- tunity for the country to declare its opinion. But there has always been Single-Chamber Government when the Tories have been in power. The Parliament Bill does not afford any remedy for that, but the preamble, in its reference to the House of Lords reform,gives a pledge that the Government have their eyes upon the existence of this abuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NolDnis.</head>
        <p>NolDnis.</p>
        <p>Heard at the Police Court. The Clerk : Are you married or single ?—Male Witness: No, sir; I am a widow now. Mr. E. England is now a Free Fooder. He informed the Debating Socieiy of the fact on Tuesday, and the sensation was not anything like so great d s it might have been. Mr. Furness has gone the whole hog on the ques- tion of Tariff Reform, but his erstwhile comrade seems to have landed between the devil and the deep blue sea. Even yet, apparently, he is inclined to consider Fret Trade a solid rock to. cling to in emergency.</p>
        <p>Such is the fascination of football that we hear of one fine father of a family who stripped his little son of every garment and pawned the lot to raise the money to follow the Warrington team to Coventry last week. This will afford a fine text for the moralist and deprecator of sport, but we think if the case were inquired into it would be found that the ruffian under notice had been debauched by other excesses than an inordi- nate love of football.</p>
        <p>• * • Mr. Balfour tried to prove on Tuesday that the Parliament Bill had not been approved by the electors, Unionist journals, however, have taken a very different view. The "Times,' for instance, said on December 17th:</p>
        <p>We accept the view that the enuntry has been consulted on the Parliament Bill, and has con- tinued the verdict of the last election. Two days later the Glavate Herald said (December 19) : We must, therefore, whether we like it or not allow that the Parliament Bill has been accepted by the country. • •</p>
        <p>Some curiosity has been awakened,having regard to the Ginnell incident, as to the status of a member suspended from the service of the House of Commons. Sus- pension caries with it exclusion from the precincts of the House, subject, however, in the case of members under suspension, to the proviso regarding their service on Private Bill Committees. Notices standing in the name of a suspended member are removed from the notice paper of each day, as it is made out, so long as his suspension lasts.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Brookfield, the author of one of the two new pieces ("The Young Lady of Seventeen") produced by Mr. Weedon Grossmith on Thursday night, has probably been responsible for a greater number of "good things' than any other humorist before the public to-day—even assuming that a liberal percentage should be knocked off the total number of witticisms ascribed to him. One of his neatest efforts took the shape of m entry in the visitors' book of a theatrical lodging-house in the provinces at which he once stayed. It ran simply: " Quoth the raven."_</p>
        <p>• 0 • A Canadian journal recently sent a reporter to Limbo (in Oblivion) to ascertain the views of Julius Cwsar—whom he found arguing with Alexander the Great as to the practical value of aeroplanes in warfare— upon Reciprocity. The eminent soldier was very frank. " What do you think of Reciprocity ?" the reporter asked. "He's a pretty good general, but I could lick him easy," Cassar answered. " Will it go through ? " "Yes," said Cmsar emphati- cally, "if they make a big enough hole for it." * • •</p>
        <p>There is every prospect of Warrmgton sending its quota to the Lancashire choir of 4,000 voices which is being organized to give a concert on the large orchestra at the Crystal Palace in connection with the Festiva of Empire, on June 17th. A local committeel has already been appointed and Mr. F. H. Crossley has accepted the position of con- ductor and trainer, subject to a sufficiently large Warrington choir being enrolled. In a letter in another column Mr. J. P. Reynolds (hon. secretary pro tern) invites all who wish -to join the choir to send him their names and addresses not later than Saturday next.</p>
        <p>In his " Budget " speech to the Education •Committee on Monday, Mr. A. J. Jolley rightly called attention to the increasing burdens thrown upon the shoulders of the ratepayers by the much-needed educational reforms of late years, and put in a plea— which was apparently endorsed by all his -fellow-members—for more adequate aid from the national exchequer. So successful has Mr. Lloyd Georges democratic finance been in yielding revenue beyond the country's immediate necessities that it is understood that the Government will very soon be in a position to afford relief to local authorities. Such a course will be welcomed with fervent gratitude by the long suffering ratepayer who is feeling the pinch somewhat severely.</p>
        <p>H. G. Well' novel "The New Machiavelli" has many references to current politics, one of which is embodied in the following signifi- cant quotation:—" The subsequent campaign of Mr. Chamberlain for Tariff Reform seemed calculated to combine the financial adven- _</p>
        <p>turers of the Empire is one vast conspiracy against the consumer." _</p>
        <p>The Mayoress asks us to remind those of our lady readers who are eligible to subscribe to the Coronation gift to her Majesty the Queen that the fund for Warrington and district will close on March Bth. The project, of course, forms part of the great national scheme whereby those who bear the names of Mary, Marie, May or Marion shall offer a present to her Majesty during this memor- able year of her Coronation.</p>
        <p>I The Rev. A. M. Mitchell has been com- plimenting the. Rector on his courage in advocating the closing of more public- houses in Warrington. "But if," he says, "they could only persuade the Rector to come out as a thorough-going temperance reformer they would be willing to carry him shoulder high through the town." What a chance for the Rector He might com- mence his reforms at the Parish Church gates?</p>
        <p>• • • Sir Benjamin Johnson, leader of the Liberal Party in Liv&lt;q.pool, always has a good story to tell. Here is his latest. On the occasion of a presentation, a worthy alderman, he says, delivered himself with great pomposity of the graceful remark that he was not there to pour forth a paregoric on the recipient. Somebody whispered to him "panegyric," whereupon the alderman con- tinued, gaily " My friend says" panegyric," but as the two words are anonymous it cannot matter very much !" Daily News.</p>
        <p>In another column we publish a letter from a Warringtonian in America who has been roused to anger by Lady Greenall's reckless statement regarding Tariff Reform and unemployment in protected countries. The facts he gives are so striking that they should cause Her Ladyship seriously to think before advising the working-classes of this country to adopt a fiscal system which so certainly brings in its train industrial oppression, unemployment, famine and death!</p>
        <p>It is curious to find Germany worrying about its "capital going abroad" and its market being "flooded with foreign securi- ties." The Tariff Reformers in England have represented the "going of capital abroad" as an evil, and have suggested that Tariff Reform would put a stop to it. Yet we have the "Standard" of February 14th reporting the official return of Dr. Delbruck, the Secretary of the Interior, showing an export of German capital during the last ten years of no less than £260,000,000.</p>
        <p>By a temporary arrangement in the postal services the parcels, letters, Sm. of all the United Kingdom for Jamaica are now being dispatched from Latchford Locks and not as heretofore from London. The letters are stored in London until a few hours before the liner leaves,Manchester, when they are sent by mail train to Warrington, whence they are transferred by means of P.O. vans to Latchford, where the ship is lying in wait. This step speaks well for Warrington, and probably if we had the much desired dock we should have more of this kind of trade, which would naturally attract other business.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ramsay Macdonald, M.P., speaking at the opening of a tuberculosis exhibition at Leicester, on Saturday,said consumption was one of the most deplorable and deadly of diseases. And yet there was boundless hope in it all. Give them fresh air, sufficient nourishment, and enough rest, and they could crack their fingers in the face of the fell infection. The great work of the future which the State was going to undertake regarding the public health was to remove the environments and circumstances of disease by providing good housing, leisure, and a sufficiency of food, and this must be done by the release of certain economic forces, and not in the way of charity.</p>
        <p>" Mysterious music has been heard in the streets of Warrington this week, and we have seen people gazing skyward as if the mellifluous chimes were of heavenly origin. Actually they were "The Bells of Comeville" mourted on a boxed-in lurry and played by an invisible musician. The effect was really remarkable. One's ears would be assailed by these delightful sounds—say in Bridge-street —and a glance around revealed the passers-by gazing spell-bound in all direc- tions, and perhaps wondering if Trinity Church had become bewitched. Then the sounds would be traced to a slow-moving lurry, and the secret was out. Congratula- tions to Crosfleld's Amateur Operatic Society on the smartest advertisement Reen (or heard) in Warrington for years</p>
        <p>Canon Rawnsley's suggestion that there should be an organised system of bonfires on the evening of the Coronation deserves every support. The bonfire is one of the oldest forms of rejoicing in the world, and used to be the recognised manner in which Great Britain celebrated her victories. Nevertheless, it has to be admitted that we do not do these things as well as our fore- fathers did. At the Coronation of King Edward VII. a great deal of muddling occurred. Some of the bonfires were ignited in open daylight, and consequently lost their effect. Others waited for a signal that never came, and were not lit until everybody , had gone to bed, so that they might as well not have existed. The great thing to do, if bonfires are to be used generally over the country, is to arrange beforehand that they shall be lit at a given moment. History is strewn with examples of mistakes that have occurred when the lighting of one was regarded as a signal for the lighting of another. The most notorious was the false alarm early in the nineteenth century, when, owing to a mistake having occurred, the defenders of the country assembled with their weapons at the various market-places and other centres, only to find that their vigilance had been in vain.—" Country Life." • •</p>
        <p>The improvement of publichouses is aimed at in a Bill presented by Lord Lamington. The memorandum of the measure states that at the present time the publichouse is in many cases "merely a place to drink in," and it is laid down in the Bill that in grant- ing permission for structural alterations upon or extensions to licensed premises, licensing justices shall not do so in such a way as to restrict the provision of accommodation for the supply of food or light refreshments; the making of such premises more open, airy, com- modious, or comfortable ; the substitution of the provision of chairs and tables, either wholly or partly, in lieu of existing bar accommodation; the provision of games other than unlawful games, newspapers, music, or gardens, or any other means of</p>
        <p>Tatrearrour: a New Liquid Cement for Mending and Making. In tuba Id. 2d, 3cl„ 6cl.—Advt.</p>
        <p>reasonable recreation. Where licensed premises are not merely places for the con- .sumption of intoxicating liquors, but contain adequate provision for the supply of food and light refreshments, and are airy, corn- modiovs, and comfortable, the Bill provides that the licensing justices shall, on the application of the holder of the licence, certify to that effect, and any premises so certified shall be exempt from the provisions of Section 120 of the Children Act, 1908.</p>
      </div>
