        <p>Territorial Changes. The " London Gazette" says "kb Battalion the Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) ; Quartermaster and Hon. Major A. F. G. Potter resigns his commission, and is granted per- mission to retain his rank and to wear the prescribed uniform. February 16th, 1911". Second-Lieutenant H. 0. Adams. of the 4th Battalnn Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) has been appointed lieutenant. Football Club Developments.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Committee of the Football Club am Thursday evening, it was decided to proceed with the scheme for widening the Wilderspool ground and erecting new stands on the land recently leased on the north side. We understand that the work;. to be put in hand at once. A sub-committee has been formed —with several co-opted members—to revise the rules of the club. Town Planning.</p>
        <p>Upwards of 6710 representatives of 200 local authorities in various parts of the country aimembled in the Small Conceit-room, St. George's Hall, on Thursday afternoon, at the inauguration of the notional conference on details of practical town- planning administration, under the auspices of the National Advisory Town-Planning Committee. Numer- ous members of the Warrington Corporation were preeent, including the Mayor (G.uncillor Dr. Joseph), Alderman Bennett, who is a member of the Advisory Committee, Alderman Pierpoint, Connoillors Birtles, Bellian, Pemberton, Cunnirigton and Craik, with Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk) and Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor). One of the most interesting features of the conference was an exhibition at the old Blue Coat School of plans and drawings of town planning achemes and other municipal improvements. These included the designs for the new Warrington Bridge and the Market.gate</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Soc In the heart of Old England" was the subject of Tuesday's lecture at the Society's rooms at the Old Academy. Mr. J. T. Peters, of Birkenhead, bad brought with him a case of 130 beautifully tinted lantern slides to illustrate a holiday in the Marches and the Shakespeare country. Commencing with Stokesay Costle, be took his audience through Ludlow, Hereford, Roes, Gloucester, Tewkesbury, Stratford and the Avon village., to Warwick. Ile left us at the "Heart of England" oak tree near Chelten- ham, and all agreed that no finer route, or one more worthy of the tit,n of the lecture could have been chosen. Not only the cathedrals and abbeys were shown, but odd little bits in each of the towns. Mr. Peters is well versed in architectural lore, and was able to describe in terse and exact language what a novice would have hopelessly mengled. The vote of thanks that the Plesident, Mr. W. E. Brown, put to the meeting was most heartily accorded, all agreeing that the tinting of shades, when tine by oge of artistic temperament, makes for stereoscopic rich- ness in the picture, its appearance of solidity being greatly enhanced by its presentation in colours some- what approximating to those of nature. Thirty members and friends were present. Cyclist Sued. .</p>
        <p>'At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour Judge H. Y. Stanger, K.C., Browne, surgeon dentist, of Newton-le-Willows, sued Mr. Frank Sntrkey. unhairer, 74, St. Mary-street, Warrington for E 5 ss. damages for personal injuries received by the plaintiff through the defendant's alleged negligence in tiding a bicycle •on November 7th near the Dog and Dart Inn, Grappenball. Mr. Longton appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. A. Browne for the defendant. The plaintiff stdted that be was crossing the road at , the point named when the defendant ran into him, knocking him over, with the result that he had to remain is bed several days. There eras a lighted road lamp nf ar the &amp;maw of the accident, hut defendant did not ring his bell.—Mr. Brown. for the defence contended that there was no</p>
        <p>light about and that "when Mr. Starker' -say 7-11; Browne in the roadway he was close to him. He tried to avoid him, hut the plaintiff jumped about no much that he could not.—Hie Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff. Wedding: Webb—Booth...</p>
        <p>A pretty thrugh quiet wedding was solemnised at the Holy Trinity Church, on Thursday afternoon, when Miss Mary Booth, of G, Arpley-street, daughter of the late Mr. Edward Booth, batter, Sankey-street, Warrington, was married to Mr. Harry Webb, son of Mr. Joseph Webb, of Forest-street Northwich. The Rev E.O E. Carleton officiated,and as the happy couple left the church, Mr. Frank Both (brother of the bide) played Mendellssohn's popular wedding march. The bride, who was given away by Mr. Frank Booth, was attired in an old rose cashmere empire dress, trimmed with and ninon lace. Her black hat with feathers of a like colour, contrasted nicely with her dress. She carried a handsome bouquet of white ' tulips, white carnations and lilies of the valley with asparagus fern. Her gold pendant was the gift of the bridegroom. Miss Jessie Booth (sister of the bride), served as the bridesmaid and looked ve.y pretty in a dress of blue cashmere. trimmed with ninon and passementerie and her black hat was trimmed with pink ribbon. Her bouquet consisted of pink and white dowers and asparagus fern and her gold pendent was the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. Joseph Webb discharged the duties of best man and the reception was held at 6. Arpley-street. The honey- moon is being spent at Blackpool. Warrington Grocers' Dinner.</p>
        <p>..The teeth annual . dinner in connection with the</p>
        <p>---- Warrington and District Grocers' and Provision - -</p>
        <p>Dealers 7 Asaociation was held a t—tbe .(c,17;;; Thureday evening and proved one of the moat success-</p>
        <p>ful on 'record. 'The chair was occupied by the President (Mr. J. W. Kay) who hod on his right, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. Others present were Alderman James EVIIOII, Councillor John Wilkinson, Dr. J. Come Hibbert, Mr. Walter Flood, Alderman Green (President of the St. Helens Grocers' Association), Mr. Cottam (secretary) and Alderman Hoyle (St. Helens). Numerous letters of apology for non-attendance were received, amongst others from the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), Sir Gilbert Greens)], Bart,, Mr. A. H. Crodeld and Mr. W. H. Lever—The toast list included : "The Visitors," proposed by Mr. John Jackson and responded to by Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Messrs. Walmsley, Adams. Garlick, Lightbody and Dr. Coote Hibbert ; "The town and trade of Warring- ton," proposed by Mr. Duckworth and responded to by Councillor Wilkinson ; " Kindred associations," proposed by the President and responded to by Alderman John Green (St. Helens); " The President and officials," proposed by Alderman James Evans and responded to by the President, the Secretary (Mr. W. H. Owen), and Mr. W, Monks. As the " Examiner" did not receive an invitation to the dinner, we are unable to give a report of the speeches.—An excellent programme was submitted, including songs by Mrs. Margaret Booth, Mr. John Babbitt, Mr. Arthur Frame, Mr. H. F. Lesion and Mr. Day, recitals by Mr. H. Smith, M.P., Mr. H. M. Ashton and Mr. C. W. Almond. Mr. I'. aylor was an efficient accompanist.</p>
        <p>Cliarfng Cross Bank .Smasit.</p>
        <p>An atridysis of the smaller :111301.1U in the Charing Cross Bank was published by the " Financial Time, ' on Thursday, and showed that from Warrington there ' were five accounts, totalling £843. Butchers Ball.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Butchers' Association the annual ball was held on Monday evening at the Lion Hotel. About 121) people were present, and a very enjoyable evening was spent. Dancing commenced at eight o'clock and was continued until 2 a.m, to music supplied by Mr. Taylor's band. The president, Mr. W. Shaw, Mr. H. Mather, and Mr. A. Mynekyme carried out the duties of M.C.'s while Mr. Lee catered in an admirable manner. Junior Liberals.</p>
        <p>The newly formed League of Young Liberals opened their session last evening with an impromptu del:ate at the Liberal Club. A syllabus of debates, lectures, etc., has been arranged to ran till April,' and arrangements have been made with Mr. F. Bower Alcock, M.A., to give three lectures on Colonial Preference.</p>
        <p>Liverpool Vestry Clerkship. The candidates selected for the position of clerk to the Liverpool Select Vestry in place of the late Mr. Hager are. Mr. A. Bottomley, clerk Warrington Union; Mr. C. W. Coster, clerk Dudley Union ; Mr. R. W. Maddox, assistant clerk West Derby Union.— The appointment will be made on the 7th March. GARDEN Rix..., special lies at 275. 6d. asob.—J. S. GA ALAN D, ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Next Week at the Palace. Ferguson and Mack, eccentric comedians, in an alieurdity " The dimple sisters" are the chief attrac- tion at the Palace next week. Other "turns" are Burns and Co., comedy acrobats and dancers, in "Hornell Bumbsti "; Shiela McNeil, Scotch comedy artiste; Stanley and Vandyke, in a comedy act; Sam Redfern, "The black boss of the benighted Bohemia."; Helen Martin, vocalist; Walwell Bros., gymnasts; Norman Osborne, in his ventriloquial speciality "The actor and the call boy." Perfection Picturea will conclude the programme. Employees Entertained. Mr. F. A. Frost, High Sheriff of Cheshire, enter- tained the employees of Messrs. Peter Stubs Ltd„ at supper in Messrs. Sutton's rooms, Legbistreet, on Tuesday evening. About 150 sat down lo an excel- lent repast and afterwards tobacco was provided for the men and sweets for the boys and girls. A clever ventriloquial and conjuring entertainment was given by Mr. Welfare of Liverpool. A vote of thanks was enthusiastically pissed to Mr Frost for his hospitality, at the!propositi. of Councillor Bellow seenuted by Mr. C. McLellan of R.ainbill. Tae githering was generally voted to have been most successful. Warrington •Patent, _</p>
        <p>In the wsekly lint of new p.dents for Lancashire and Cheahire is the following -A. E. Boardman, Warrington.—Process and Apparatus for the Manu- facture of Soap—The invention consists essentially in Inking the liquid soap from a manufacturing pan or vat, then leading it.to a asrmnd pan or vat, in winch it may be diluted with water, and bane colouring matters added if required. The latter pan has mecum for agitating the liquid and means for directing it either into a trough or on to the surface 4 a hollow- hr-axed drone, adnielt takes non, film of the liquid and dries it, leaving a coating of soap on the drum, which is afterwards removed by a scraper in the form to be submitted to the mmal stamping or screening process The Bewsey Shooting Tragedy.</p>
        <p>At West Derby Cenietert. on Saturd,ry afternoon, the funeral took place of Mr. Percy E. Cleaver, of Irevonohire House, Sefton Park, Liverpool, who died at the Warrington Intirmsry on the previous Wednesday, as the result of a gun accident. The Rev. J. W. Baker officiated, and, in the course of a sympathetic address, referred to the mad circumstances under which Mr. Cleaver lost his life. There was a large and representative gathering to pay a last tribute of respect. Amongst the numerous beautiful floral tributes were wreaths from family and relatives, friends at Bewsey, office staff at Brougham-terrace, the directors and members of the Liverpool Football Club, the house staff at Devonshire House, the medical officers of the West Derby Union, Ac.</p>
        <p>Popular Evening Concerts. These popular concerts, which are held every Satur- day evening in the Bold street Wesleyan Schoolroom, still maintain the high standard of efficiency which has been attained. On Saturday evening the follow- ing ell-known ai Listen if operatic hone gave another excellent concert, which WR-1 greatly enjoyed by a , large audience Mrs. A. Booth, the Misses Emily Taylor, Carrie Hastings, Ethel Taylor and Messrs. J. Draper, and A. Fieldhouse. The duet, "Watchman ! What of the Night was well rendered by Messrs. Draper and Fieldhouse whilst the last named won hearty applause for his singing of" Gln, ions Devon." Miss Emily Taylor rendered " On the banks of Allan Water " in a most praiseworthy manner, whilst the quartette—Mrs. Booth, Miss Emily Tay] r and Messrs. J. Draper and A. Fieldhouse—rendered a number of selections from the standard operas in a very pleasing 111111111 tr.</p>
        <p>VACUCM CLEANERS OD hire, As. per dav ; MARMA LADE CETTERS, 18. per day.—J. S. GARLAND, 51-55 Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. The Late Ilelor Nicholson. The death of Major J. E. Nich deem, J.P., formerly registrar of the Warrington County Court (which was reported in our last issue) formed the subject of an inquiry before the City Coroner at Bath on Saturday. Mrs. Msry Katherine Prances Shuttleworth the widow, said that Major Nicholson had a fall on , some stairs in January last and broke his leg. He was attended by Dr. Tabor up till the time of his death. Dr. Tabor, who attended Majsr Nicholson after his fall, stated that the deceased gentleman he had been for some time rather infirm on his feet. The place where he fell on the stairs was an awkward turning. He did 111 It get up oiler the injury, and contracted congestion of the hinge and inflammation f the brain, attributable to the injury and shock. The jury returned a verdict that Major Nicholson died from "Am accidental</p>
        <p>Warrington Woman's Mysterious Death. An inquest was held by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, at the Dog and Partridge Inn, Manchester- road, yesterday morning, to inquire into the death of Margaret Cousans (49), of 40, Dalton Bank, whose body WRR found in the Bridgewater Canal at Woolston ,on Monday last.—William Cousans, deceased's husband, stated that he last saw his wife alive about six o'clock on Monday morning, when he left home to go to work. She seemed all right at the time. She had been troubled with her heart, and had been medically attended by Dr. Anderson. She had been in the habit of taking drink, and for her own good he had spoken to her, advising her not to do so. She had never threatened to take away her hie. For the past two years he had had a good deal of trouble with his wife owing to her habit of drinking.—John Rathbone, canal lock keeper at Dobson Lock, said that he found the shsivl on the bank, and later recovered the body—The, jury returned RR open verdict, there being no evidence to show how deceased got into the water. - • • • - - •</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS ground by special machinery abed your machines now before the rush.—J. S. GARLANN Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt Academy Street Adult School. Mr. E. Wynne Jones provided the lectnrette nn Sunday morning last,and took for his title" Religion in relation to politics." He carefully discriminated between politics and party politics and averred that no man could afford nowadays to neglect politics ; no man could separate himself from the social conditions in which he moves, especially such questions as Housing, Poor Law, and Unemployment. By politics In. meant the sum and substance of all that goes to create life and its environment. Be drew attention to the oocupation of the land; he compared the care in classification of criminals with our present mode of treating decrepit, yet probably perfectly honest, aged poor ; and he pointed oat that it was essential to adopt the principles of Christ to</p>
        <p>bring about a true solution of all social problems. Ile did not agree with Lord Curzon's construction of Renan's remark " All civilisation is the outcome of aristocracy:. but rather he interpreted Reran to man, that it was due to the best intellect, improved moral fibre, and best character of the nation, and not of anv particular class; certain it was that the rich could claim no prerogative in this respect. Even Blatchford paid tribute to the principles enunciated by Jelling Christ who sought to improve the social condi- tions of the neople quite as much as to inculcate a religious, spiritual life. The speaker felt assured that a new movement was being established and that true democracy—the highest and best intellect would very soon govern the country more in the keeping with the ethics of the Son of Man.</p>
        <p>Visit of Mr. Alfred T. Dickens. We would rewind our readers that Mr. Alfred T. Dickens, eldest surviving son of the late Charles Dickens, will deliver his popular lecture '• My father's life and works," in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening nest, under the auspices of the Philomathic Society.</p>
        <p>CUTLIIIIRY, Joseph Rodgers &amp; Soos' celebrated kellnsa nod forks, otc.—Ageoc, .f. S. G•RLAND, I roonmosgsr, Bridge m 4, Warrington. —Advt.</p>
        <p>Salvation Army. A concert was given in the Salvation Army Hall on Monday evening by various friends and proved highly succesaful. The chair was taken hyllrs. Dickention previous to the arrival of Councillor Dickenson who was engaged in municipal datiest. Upon Mr. Dickenmn's arrival ha had a cordial reception. The items included vocal and Matramental music by the Apollo Quartette, Miss Williams' school children,Miss flynth (piano), Mr. Allen (violin), Misses Reuie Cuuningtun, Taylor, M Tynan, Bata and It. True. The Chairman eukgised the work of the Salvation Army. A most enjoyable evening was brought to a close by the nsual vote of thauks, proposed by ' Adjutant Valiance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MR. JOHN UPSON. Twenty-five Years as Headmaster of</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN UPSON. Twenty-five Years as Headmaster of</p>
        <p>Silver Street School.</p>
        <p>A PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Upson, J.P., wan last evening the recipient of a handsome present from the managers and present and former inembet• of the staff of Silver-street Wesleyan day school, in celebration of the 25t1. anniversary of his appointment to the headmastership of the school, which was made on Hecember 22nd, 1885.—The presentation was made at a social gathering held at the Winmarleigh Cali., the large attendance and the many letters received from managers and teachers, expressing appreciation of Mr. fjpson'a sterling qualities, being eloquent testimony to the esteem in which he is held, and to the high value placed upon his services for education by all who have come into intimate touch with the work at Silver-street. The gifts took the form of a tea service in silver and oak waiter (silver-mounted), together with a rase of pipes and pouch. Is the course of a letter addrered to the Secretary l(Me, C. w!lcockson), Mr. 11. J. Guest, RA., said : For SI or 35 years the school had done loud work In the town, but it was not until Nlr. Upson became : Headmaster in 116,5 that it began to take the foremost position it holds to-day. The inemniration of the school sets not very popular with our Methodist friends and in the esrly day. there was much up-hill work in connection with it. . . • Under Mr. tTpson's regime the day of prosperity began to dawn and it was not</p>
        <p>JOHN UPSON, J.P.</p>
        <p>long before we had to fled aocommodatlen for • large increase in the number of scholam. . . The appointment of Mr. Upson as Headmaster or the school in 1885 was Providential and he has proved hinmelf to be one of the greatest assets to Warrington Methodiant and the town itself. in a strong man, physically, mentally and spiritually. The day of Eternity will alone reveal the work a hich he hen done in intlitencing the characters of very runny hundreds of children who have passed through hie Lands. Not only so, but his example has been a constant stimulus to those who have had any desire to work lor the uplifting of their fellows. I have had abundant opportunity to note the gradual improvement in the morels of Warrington, end especially ni the neighbonrhood in which Silver-street M situated. Though there 111 much still to be desired there in no question that considerable advance bes been made. I cannot doubt that much of this is due to the existence of tke school over which Mr. Upson has presided for 25 years. Many men would have thought that toe Work of the day school was sufficient in Heel!, but Lis labours, as you all know, have been incessant Is season and out of season. Time would fail me to tell of Lis achievements in the world of politics,soinial reform, educational improvement and spiritual development. One of my greatest joys is to know UAW I Dad muck to do with the appointment of Mr. Upson to be headmaster of Silver-street school." the Rev. E. B. KEELING presided and in making the presentation bore testimony to the good work Mr. Upson had done and wee doing in connection with the day school, the Sundey school and the church. Mr. UrsoN scsnowledged the piesentation in feeling terms.</p>
        <p>A most enjoyable programme arts rose through, consisting of curies, recitations, glees and instrumeotal pieces contributed by members of the staff and their triends, dances, charades, games of pit, waist 02.</p>
        <p>, ' * , • • • . NNW '</p>
