      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>HONOURED BY THE KING.</head>
        <p>HONOURED BY THE KING.</p>
        <p>SUPERINTENDENT FRANK TURNER RECEIVES THE EDWARD MEDAL.</p>
        <p>The King held an investiture at St. James'. Palette on Thursday for the personal bestowal of various orders and decorations in connection with the New Year honours, and also for the bestowal of rewards for gallantry in saving or attempting to save life, in the form of the Albert Medal of the second class, the Edward medal of the second elan, and the Special King's Medal for police and firemen. The Lord Chamberlain and many State officials remind the King and conducted him to the Throne-room,where the insignia of the various honours bestowe.d at the New Year were personally handed by him to the recipients. Afterwards the rewards for bravery were bestowed, the Albert Medal first, then the Edward Medal, and subeequently the King's Police Medal. The Home Secretary we. in attendance, and as each candidate for decoration wasi announced by the Lord Chamber- lain, Mr. Churchill read to the King a brief record of the deed for which the medal was awarded. la each /Ilse the King personally pinned on the medal "1 he proceedings had a special interest fee Warring- ton people, as the recipients of the Edward medal included Superintendent Flank Turner, Warrington Fire Brigade, who was decorated for fifty years' service distinguished by success in the organisation of fire brigades in the town and district by repeated acts of gallantry in saving life from fire, The Superintendent darn not return to Warrington until this (Saturday) evening, but in a telephone message home on Thursday Le expensed himself as highly delighted with the honour bestowed upon hint !and with the way in which he wan received by hie Majesty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The World, the Flesh, and the Devil."</p>
        <p>This melodrama is what it claims to be —remarkable and sensational, but some of the situations are altogether too farfetched. For instance, his Satanic Majesty figures in the drantoris persona e, and by making a weird and awesome appearance in the police court, where impossible things are being enacted, muses the villain to commit suicide. Another exciting .ceue is the novel escape of the hero and heroine from the old mill, by mean. of the water wheel. The humour of the play. although it appeals to a large section of the audience, is somewhat crude. There se some really clever acting by Mr. Charles Ninian, las the dissipated old man Rupert Dyke, his !" make-up " being wonderfully good, while he infuses /rent dramatic power as well as pathos into his characterisation. Mr. Horatio Sinclair ha.. somewhat thankless part as Niceolae Brophy, the arch-villain, hut he plays it in thoroughly convincing style. Miss Dora Hammansley, who impersonates Gertrude Grant, proves a great favourite,her emotional acting, while not being overdone, making a very strong appeal. She is possessed of superior histrionic talents and makes the most of a difficult role. Mr. Will Harman, as the irrepressible Mr. Johnson, causes great merriment, especially in the Court scene.</p>
        <p>_ NEXT WEEK—" THE ARCADIANS." Undoubtedly the most attractive and enjoyable of the many stage comedies associated with music in recent years is "The Arcadians," which has attained over its fillOth performance at the Shaftesbury Theatre, London, and has been revoived with delight by , audiences in the provinces wherever it ban been pro- duced. Its cetera •isit to the Royal Court Theatre is next week, and we can confidently m00:6.e those who desire a stage performance of refined taste and poetio fancy, combined with sparkling music. a great treat The plot of the piece centres upon a Mr. Smith, a London caterer, who takes a cruise in his aeroplane and falls into Arcadia, and becomes so enchanted with its airily-clad inhabitants that he is very loth to lease. Having told a lie, he is dipped in the well of truth and rejuvenated and sent back to London, accompanied by two Arcadian girls, to preach the simple life. Arriving at the racecourse, his appearance muses a sensation, he being so altered that his wife does not recognise him, and makes lova to him. In the last act we find him proprietor of a restaurant modelled on Arcadian lines, with his two Arcadian girls as an attraction. The denouement is very amusing. Messrs. Arthur Hart and Leslie Moreton's company is a strong one, with a large chorus and an augmented orchestra. The out includes Mr. Chas. A. Stephenson as Simplicitaes" Mr. A. Beers as "Peter Doody," the disconsolate jockey; Miss Gertrude Aylward as "Mrs. Smith," who were such favourites on the last visit ; Mies Vivian Carter as " Smibrs," Hiss Nora Parker as " Cbrysea," and Miss Tina Lincoln as " Eileen Cavanagh," the lively Irish girl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Liberals to Win Back Southern Counties.</head>
        <p>Liberals to Win Back Southern Counties.</p>
        <p>MR. MASTERHAN ON THE DOOM OF PLURAL VOTER.</p>
        <p>With the plural voter out of the way, a new hope arises for Liberalism in the Southern end HOllle Counties, and North Berkshire Liberals sheered lustily on Wednesday night when Mr. Msaterman, Coder Secretary at the Home Office, told them, in the Corn Exchange at Abingdon, that the Goeernment had the great offender on their list. His message of hope to them from the Government was that never again would they have to fight under such conditions as they did last December— (cheers)—grith the utterly unfair handicap of the plural vote. A new i ndu•try had driven the motor-car through the Corrnot Promises Act of lbB3, and now, although a rich man could not pay tfd. to take a votor to the poll by rail- way, he could bring into use his motor-car in order to swamp the local vote of any district. Liberals had won the last three elections, and to a great extent had made their position impregnable in the great towns of England. Scotland and Wales. Now one of the great efforts to which the Liberal leaders were going to turn their attention was to win back the Solvh country to Liberektn. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Band of Hope. The above society wle favoured with a visit from the Warrington Band of Hope Select Choir on Monday evening last. Under the able conductorship of Mr. A. SM.:O, a first-rate programme was presented. The choir contributed three part songs, " The shepherd'a lullaby," "Springs delights," and " Oars flashing" and Miss Eva Tanner, a very promising young vocalist, very sweetly rendered two solos. Misme Annie Edwards, Jeanie Swarbrick and Amy Littlnwood gave recitations at good style, whilst Misses Gertrude i Lawton and Ethel Lightfoot displayed much ability i their pianoforte duet. Mr. F. Simpson presided dale jog the evening and at the close of the entertainment a hearty Tote of thanks was accoreed to hi ram 3 and the choir for their splendid services.</p>
      </div>
