laces, added the Chief Constable she took two umbrellas. Other charges could be preferred against her but he did not propose to go into them. It was proposed that the girl should he seot to a home for three months at the end of which a :wisher, who wan the father of her illegitimate child, was pre- pared to marry her, iakOn the i ench taking this coure, the prisoner replied " I appreciate your kindness, gentlemen." SATURGAY.—Bstore Messrs. J. W. Hayward (is the chair), R. T. lairclough, J. Upson, A. J. Jolley, and T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>In th</p>
        <p>A Lopc inn Horse THEFF.—liate Sumner, alias Collins, who was charged with stealing three blanket s and two quilts,value 6s.,the property of Mary Rogerson lodging-house keeper, t, Shipyard, when asked to plead said she did not steal the property but took it as a loan.—The Clark : Without permission y—Prisoner : Yee—The Chief Constable said that the prisoner was engaged at Mrs. Rogerson's lodging-house as n deputy in August last. From time to time various articles of bed clothing were missed, and Mrs. Rogerson ques- tioned the prisoner about some linen which had disappeared but Sumner denied all knowledge of it. The next day, however,she absconded. On three differ- ent dates she pawned bed-clothing at Mr. Houghton's shop in Mersey-street. The prisoner was traced to Liverpool where she was in custody on another charge. —Prisoner was sent to goal for smooth. Hz MA DR A MI., E.—A charge of being drunk and disorderly and with stealing See ready reckoners and a year hook, value lid., the property of Alfred Downs, Church-sl.eet, was pref,irred against Sydney Ashton, 10, Howley-lane.—Constable Edwards said he saw the prisoner shortly before midnight tampering with a , show case outside Mr. Downs's shop. Ile forced open ' the case with a knife and took Borne books from it. When witness went up to him he found that Ashton was under the influence of drink nod on tho wry to the Police Station he was very violent. —This prisoner denied.—Ashton was sent to gaol for a month. The Chief Constable asked that a tine might he inflicted as an alternative. Whilst the Bench were considering the Chief Constable's request the prisoner hotly denounced the constable by whom he was arrested. The Chief Constable said that after such an exhibition he would withdraw his plea and Ashton was not allowed any option.</p>
        <p>MARGARINE, 100 PER CENT. CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON GROCER At the Borough Police Court, on Thursday, before Messrs. Rymer Young (chairman), W. Roberts. A. J. Jolley, W. T. Williams and Dr. Naden, William Upton, a grocer, of Knutsford-rond, was prosecuted under the Foods and Drugs Act, with selling butter which con. tained a quantity of margarine. Mr. A. T. Hallaway, who appeared for the Warring- ton Corporation, Mated that on January 14th Inspector Flood went to the defendant's shop, and employed OD agent—a roan named McElwee—to go in and buy three. quarters of a pound of butter. The person who served him put the price on the back, and McEwan, who was a very illiterate DUD, went out and was met by Mr. Flood at the door. He was taken hick into the shop and asked who it was who had served him. He pointed out the defendant. Mr. Flood then told defendant that he was going to have the butter onalysed.The defendant there- upon enewered "It is a retxture,not butter." The sample was divided into three parts and one part was analysed, end it wee found by the public analyst that the butter in question contained 100 per cent of margarine. They would find, mid Mr. Halloway, that it was proved that the butter was asked for, that it war paid for, and the mergarine wan handed to the purchmer in ordinary plain Mu) inspector Flood corroborated this statement, and Meßvran gave evidence to the effect that he went to the shop and purchased the butter for the inspector. Mr. T. S. Steel, for the defence, submitted that there was DO cam agaimt defendant. He said that the certificate of the analyat was not conclusive enough. He (tbe anal rat) simply said that it was margarine and nothing else. Ihe atiairman I think that in very Mr. Steel : I should like to point out that there are many no lit ifs of margarine. H:llaway : But there are not. Mr. Steel, continuing, aubmitted that there could he no convictiou in the case. If there eras a moral CIAO for OODViCtiOO, legally there was not. There was no intention to defraud by the defendant. He was dealing honeatly with Mr. Flood's agmt.</p>
        <p>LINSIED COMPOrND' for Coast., Colds, snd 13m:whirl! ttoobles. 9,11,1„ Is. lid. Of Chemists.—Adv</p>
