      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ELECTIVE AUDITORS.</head>
        <p>ELECTIVE AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Messrs T. Pemberton and B Silcock</p>
        <p>Returned Unopposed. MR. E. ENGLAND WITHDRAWS.</p>
        <p>Meyers. Thomas Pemberton and Bertram Si!rock will be the elective auditors for the borough of War- rington for the ensuing year. Five o'clock on Tuesday afternoon was the latest hour for the receipt of nominations, and papers were handed in on behalf of the following -- .</p>
        <p>Mr. Ebenezer England, accountant, Walkden. Fearnhead, proposed by Mr. James Seed, seconded by Mr. E. Wynne Jones ; and supported by Messrs. J. Upson, N. Griffiths, W. Maxwell, G. T. Rushton, J. Jarman, J. Moorfield, T. Sergeant and S. Caldwell. Mr. Bertram Siiconic, F.1.C.A., Old Bank House, Winwick-street, Proposed by Alderman Forsbaw, seconded by Mr. A. H. Cline ; and supported by Messrs. W. A. Leslie, G. Weaver, G. P. Dolan, A. J. Willett, H. Young, H M. Ashton, A. J. Silcock and J. Resin irk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Pemberton, estate agent, 115, Bewsey- road, nominated by Mr. J. Rymer Young, seconded by Alderman Bennett; and assented to by Messes. C. Mounheld, J. Finlayson, J. S. Hill, J. Critchley, A. Browne, C. H. Page, I). Owen, and R. Wood. The candidate was further nominated by Messrs. E. Pickavanee and J. H. Smethurst; and seconded by Messrs. W. Warburton end Thomas Locker.</p>
        <p>The holders of the office last year were Messrs. E. England end Laurence Bolton. On Wednesday afternoon Mr. England intimated that in order to avoid the expense to the town of an elsction. he had decided to withdraw. Messrs. T. Pemberton and B. Silcock are consequently elected unoppoied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FIDDLERS' FERRY SAILING CLUB</head>
        <p>FIDDLERS' FERRY SAILING CLUB</p>
        <p>The seventh annual dinner and distribution of prizes took place at the headquarters, the Ferry Inn, on Saturday evening last when a party to the number of 41 eat down to a subetantial repast, •ery creditably served by the hostess, Miss Johnson. After the tables were cleared the party indulged in music the remainder of the evening, the committee having arranged a capital programme. Mr. S. Auldand opened with a selection on the concertina. The toast of "The Club" was very ably proposed by Mr. L. D. Thompson. Mr. W. H. Smalley replying mentioned that he had heard it said ia town that the Fiddlers' Ferry Sailing Club was dead, but looking round that room be thouaht it ; , wee very much alive. Mr. W. Baines proposed "The 1 Kindred Clubs" and brought the Tranmere Sailing Club !very pronounced before the company. Mr. Hitchmoug 'of the Tranntere Sailing Club replied with a ve hontorma apeech. The artistes pelmet were Mr is i Critchley, Mr Eaton (banjo, Mr A 1-2 Hill. Mr W Sutton, 51 r H Bennett, Mr 1..; Davies. During the e fl- ing the distribution of prizes took place, M 11 Barthel= (Comrade) coming in for the major numb ,r of prizes, he taking four firsta and one second • A r J Whittaker (Hind) one first and two secon'de; Mr Ileaoock (Maggie) one first end one ercojnd i Mr H Warburton (Thelma, 2 seconds. Mr W K4lsnll</p>
        <p>(preaident of the dub) was then called upon to pi, to Mr H Burthem the championship trophy, which mists of a splendid silver cup, which was present.' biro (the president) for the boat gaining the grei number of points &amp;ring the season. Mr. 1e1n.11,1 i• only new at the game, bas turned out a veryi yachtsman, baring for his first season aim:nattered very had weather. The remaining boat list being completed, the corn broke up at eleven o'clock. after spending a pleasant evening. Mr. W. Pemberton was an accompanist.</p>
