      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CLERGY OF ET. PETERS CHURCH. REV. B PERCIVAL. REV. J. 11. GARNETT.</head>
        <p>THE CLERGY OF ET. PETERS CHURCH. REV. B PERCIVAL. REV. J. 11. GARNETT.</p>
        <p>The congregation of St. Peter's Church have this week celebrated the twentieth anniversary of the consecration of their church, and on Thursday even- ing they had a visit from the Bishop of the diocese who spire in most encouraging terms of the self- sacri tieing work which is being done in this thickly populated and very poor parish. His Lordship's praise was fully deserved for St. Peter's Church and its manifold activities are a great power for good in a drab neighbourhood where houses are closely packed t gather and the community is of a purely working-class character. The vicar (Rev. B Percival), who has had charge of the parish for eighteen years, liven amongst his people and has inspired their respect and confidence by his conscien- tious and devoted effnrts. He was born at Burgh-by- SandaCumberland,and in a son of the late Mr.Mattliew Percival, of Burgh-by-Sands. He was educated privately and for threeyears at Ayernt Hall. Cambridge, under the guidance of the Rev. W. Ayerst. M.A. Having reseed the Cambridge Theological Preliminary he was ordained deacon in 1881 by Dr. Pyle, the late Bishop of Liverpool, and took up his first curacy at Holy Trinity, Liverpool. He toss: ordained vi the prientlrood in 1858, and the following year he was appointed curate-in-charge of St. Pact's Mission Church, St. Helens, by the Rev. J.C. Bigot, M.A., Vicar of St. Thomas', St. Helens. The district served by the Mission contained a working-class population of 5,000 and during Mr Percival's ministry there the congregation increased from a mere handful of people to 400—the full extent of the seating capacity of the church. For several years after Mr. and Mrs. Percival came to Warrington the members of the St. Helens mothers' meeting paid them an annual vinit—a striking evidence of the high regard entertained for them. In October 1892, the benefice of St. Peter's became vacant and was offered to Mr. Percival by the Rey. H. V. Pigot, M.A., who was then vicar of St. ?aura, Warringtonned alternate Putt on of the living. He was instituted to the living by the late Bishop Ryle on December 10th, 1891, at the Palace, Liverpool. It was deemed inadvisable to hold a public institution at St. Peter's, as War- rington was just recovering front a severe epidemic of small-pox. A formidable task confronted Mr. Percival but he addressed himself to it</p>
        <p>manfully. For six months he was single-handed. as there were no funds in band wherewith to pay a curate. The Sunday school was in a backward state but it has gone ahead by leaps and bounds, the scholars on the registers now numbering 700. Immediately after he had obtained the assistance of a curate MrPercival inaugurated the men's Bible class in the Church on Sunday afternoons, and this has an average attendance of about forty. There is also a Iwomen's Bible class of seventy members. In the size of the congregation, and the amount of voluntary contributions the church has made gratifying progress,_ the offertories having risen from about £7O per annum to £2OO last year. The numbers of communicants, baptisms and marriagea have advanced threefold. A mission has been estab- lished in John.street under the direction of a Church Army officer, and meetings are regularly held. The vicarage was built the first year after Mr. Percival's arrival. Most of the money was in hand for the purpose, but the Vicar raised a little over £.320. the total c..st being ribaldly over £1,600. Through the kindnees of friends Mr. Percival has been able to raise £2,500 towards the better endowment of the living, this producing £75 per annum. The day school has been refurnished and enlarged at a cost of £3OO, an organ built in the church at a cost of £240, and a church boundary wall erected at an outlay of £250. Mr. and Mrs. Percival hose repeatedly been the recipients of token. of kindness and affection from the parishioners, the gifts including a tea and coffee service, a dinner service, isn't a lounge chair—the latter being presented to the Vicar when he returned from a holiday in Switzerland. Mr. Percival 'rasa loyal and hard-working colleague in the Rev. J. H. Garnett. M.A., who hes worked in I the parish since 1908. He was educated at Lincoln College, Oxford, and Wycliffe Hall, Oxford, obtaining the degree of B.A. 1n1895 and MA. in 1900. He was ordained deacon in 1895 and priest in 1896 by the Bishop of Manchester. Mr. Garnett's first curacy was at St. Matthew's, Bolton-le-Moors, and he ha.. since laboured in the parishes of St. John's, Carlisle ; St. Cuthbert., Everton ; and St. Philip's, Litherland, and in the mission district of Orrell. Another faithful 'worker in St. Peter's parish is Captain Pallister, the Church Army officer.</p>
        <p>WA RRINGT ON, ---1 E Church Miectv‘..s. REL/C/OZ/S cSOC/AL • ; C t'.'117110)1\ ' 11. IP orkinon. Photo) Tango. Photo]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>St. James' Baud of Hope.</head>
        <p>St. James' Baud of Hope.</p>
        <p>St. James' School was well filled OD Tuesday night on tes occasion of the annual entertainment giv”n by membera of the C.R.T.S. Jonior branch. The "Rand of Hope" comprise. about 400 members and is under the charge of the Rev. and Mrs. E. Id ilner Swift. with Mr. Arthur Todd as secretary. The first part of the pro- granime was composed of songs, recitations, S:c. The Fecund part was the illustration of a story entitled " The Land of play," by moving tableaux and songs. Ibe atorr, and several of the songs were the COM' position of Mr. Swift himself. The whole entertain- ment was well presented and much enjoyed. Daring the interval. membership cards, temperance medals •nd bars, and three special prises for essays won by Misses Adeline Bennett and Evelyn Oakes. and Nlnater Prank Davenport) were presented by Mrs. W. H. Stansfleld, who gave a very practical address on Temperance. Church Society's Lenten Services.</p>
        <p>One of the most successful and highly valued features of the Waviest. Church Society's work is the sense of special services for into and women which are held each Friday in Lent and attract very Urge concresations. The services for the coming Lent will commence next Friday when the Bishop of Manchester will preach to women at three in the afternoon and the Rev. Dr. Thorp, Vicar of St. George's, Stockport, will address a men's service at eight in the evening, his subject being, " Is worship a necessary part of religion ? " The preachers on the subsequent Fridays will be :—Mar. loth, Bishop Taylor- Smith, chaplain to H.M. Forces : Maxllth, Rev. 0..1. Howson, vicar of St. Andrew's, S.thport intorno.) and Re, J. B. Lancelot, principal of Liverpool College (evening) ; March 2lth, Rev. A. J. Waldron, vicar of Brixton, London : March 3191, RP.. R. Catterall, rector of Middleton ; April 7th, Rev. S. Rogerson, vicar or Pateley Bridge.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Teaparty. The annual congregational teaparty in connection with Holy Trinity ('torch iron held on Thursday even- ing,when finites large gathering assembled in the Infant Schools, NI (mem-street—After tea the usual concert took place, when some admirable musical items were submitted. The programme, which had been arranged by Miss Longdin included the following :—Duets, Misses Adams end Browne ; song, Miss Lonadon ; song, Miss Adams : song, Mr. J. Rabbitt ; violin solo, Mr. It. 11. Pearson ; humorous longs, Mr. W. Moores ; trio, Misses Adams and Browne and Mr. W. Moores. Miss Longdin and Miss Wenharn acted as efllcient accompanists. In the course of a short speech the Vicar the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) thanked the artistes and all those who were contri- buting to the evening's enjoyment. They were thankful to those who had helped the funds, which had been most welcome. Refreshments were provided and a whist drive and dance were ale., enthusiastically indulged in. St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>In Felebration of the twentieth anniversary of the consecration of St. Peter'n church e special service was held on Thersday evening. Despite the inclement weather, there was a good congregation. The Bishop of Liverpool (Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse, was the preacher. The service was conducted by the Rev. B. Percival, the lessonebeing read by the Rev. J. H. Garnett. The Bishop took fpr his test," Therefore, my beloved brethren, he ye st,K,dfast, unmovable. always abounding in the work of the Lord, for as much as ye know that your labour in not In vain in the Lord." He said he gladly accepted the/ invitation to preach at that service because he kripw of the good work which was going on in that ehiirch and parish and the CiMculties which they to confront. They had e past of which t. ev need not be ashamed, for which they could thank ~ . .</p>
        <p>take courage and ;mese forward.-' His lordship hee traced the history of the church from its founda- tion as. mission of St. Paul'a church, and dwelt upon the many improvements which had been effected during the incumbency of the present Vicar, including the er.tion of a new vicarage sod a boundary wall for the church, the provision of en organ and the ioauguration of minion services in John-stieet. The number of communicants wee three times as greet m it was eighteen years ago. "I know that your difficulties are great;' continued the Bishop. Perhaps no churches in Warrington has greater difficultim to confront. Your population is exceedingly large. You are one of the 51 parishes in the diocese of Liverpool whom populetion is over 10,000. What can two clergy, maisted though they may be by some devoted lay people, do amongst so many ? And if yon I are one of the largest perishes in the diocese you are certainly one of the poorest. I question whether there is any parish in Warrington quite no poor as St. I Peter's. Yon:have to face the squalor, discontent sod misery that ever accompany poverty. No parishes are so hard to work is as those which are desperately pour.'. His lordship touched upon several prominent fmture of the cherub and put ins plea foes larger congrega- ,tion at the morning services. The people leered:o mid, wonderfully generous according to their means. Their Vicar told him that there was not</p>
        <p>farthing of debt upon the church at present and every year they sent to the Church Pastoral Aid Society £4O and £24 to the Church Minion- ry hociety. He rejoiced to know bow keen and united ues congregation they were, and how they loved their</p>
        <p>church and desired by God's blessing to support it and increase it. usefulness. The Bishop then turned to his tent and spoke some homely and encouraging words to the congregation. He said John Wesley wan once asked why it was that his follower, made so much progress in England in the eighteenth century and hie reply was " Because we are all at it and we are always at it." that was the secret of progress. They had a great work still to do. They had to fill that church out of the great and poor population that surrounded it. They I.d some day to gather such s big congregation within , that building that they must burst through the northern wall of the church and erect a new aisle. Tbe Bishop concluded by urging the people to work as part of the mighty forces which Hod ens using in Warrington and elsewhere to help the men, women and little children 'around them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Indifference of the Present Day.</head>
        <p>The Indifference of the Present Day.</p>
        <p>Preaching at the IVarrington Parish Church no Sunday morning, the Rev. A. J. Brett referred to the unresponsiveness to spiritual things which was such marked and deplorable characteristic of the present day. Amongst the chief causes to which he attributed it were: The craze for amusement and excitement doe in a large measure ta the stress and strain of modern life which led men and woolen to seek relaxation in forms of entertainment, often crude and inane: chronic poverty in the shims where the conditions, to ' use the expression of George Bernard Shaw, were often " damnable," and incressing luxury at the other end of the soeial scale; and—rarbspe most important of all—, be purely utilitarian state of mind induced by present.dav absorption in business cares and responaibi titles, which tended to deaden the susceptibility to religious influ- ence.. How,he aslied,could people who were completely occupied with worldly affairs, view the prospect of life ins future world, where money and fashion would have no part? lie made a strong appeal to the congre- gation not to allow their spiritual nature to be over- come by that which was .ly material and transitory, hat to develop the diviue life within them to the fulleat extent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>St. Paul's Church</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church</p>
        <p>Interference with Marriage Law Condemned</p>
        <p>The Rev. S. E. Dymott, M.A., of Liverpool, was the preacher at St. Paul's Church on Sunday evening and dealt with the marriage law, condemning any further interference with it and also deprecating the tact that we had departed from the marriage law which was sot up at the time of the foundation of the Christian Church. Takirm as his text " What God hath joined I together lot no man put winder," he said that the con- dition of the marriage law affected the whole future of our nation, and was, therefore, of vital importance to everybody. The strength of every nation depended upon its family life and the strength of family life depended upon the relation between those who brought the family into being and those by whom the family life was regulated. If anything were done to weaken the relationship between husband and wife Unmet neces- sarily undermine the family and if history ehowed one thing more clearly than another it eves this:—That when the family life of a nation went the nation went too. It was a subject upon which most people had ideas,but few people bad really thought it out carefully. Some marriage law was necessary ; for if there were no reetriction between people marrying and intermarrying it ended with disestrous results both to the minds and bodies of those who were born tinder such conditions. A marriage law was universal throughout the world. When the Christi. Church came into existence some 2,000 years ago things were then eta very low ebb and family life was at a very low level. The Church set itself to raise the standard of things and it did it by formulating a marriaxe law drawn from Holy Scripture and based upon the following three principles It The indissolubility of marriage, i.e., at was a life relationship until "death doth us part '' (2, the principle of affinity, i.e , the relation between the two was tube such that the blood relation, of the one became by affinity the relations of the other —in that manner we got our brothers-in-law and our eiatnra- in-late and (3) the principle of consanguinity, i.e., that the blood relations within certain limits muat not marry one with the other. That marriage low of the Christian Church worked exceedingly well, raising the standard of family life to the highest level. It worked no well in this country that the State said it would accept the Church's marriage law, and until 1857 there was only one marriage law in this country. The first false step was made in 1357 when the Divorce Act was pastel allowing people to be divorced. That threw over the first pricciple, and the Deceased Wife's Sister Act broke into the .cond principle. If the third plinciple, that of consanguinity were, thrown over there was nothing to prevent things in this country gradnally reverting until you have a condition of things existing in this country , that existed in Rome when the Christian Church came Into existence. The preacher then quoted the opinion. : of Lord Justior Bargrave Dean and Lord Justice Bigham who, asked at the recent Divorce Commission which they would chooee between no divorce and the present system replied : No divorce, but divorce having been brought within the reach of the rich wedo not see how you can keep it from the poor. By experience both of the judges were of opinion that the 1v.37 Act was a step in the wrong direction. rh. paining of the Dec./wed Wife's Sister Act was also a step in the wrong direc- tion. The argument for it was that if • wife were to</p>
        <p>die no one was no etted to take care of the children. her Mater ; hut the people who passed the ACE did not see that they really defeated their object, for it made it impoenible for a self-renpecting deceased wife's Sister to go into the bonne of her brother-in-law and live there. People only thought,avbee they peseed the Act, of the 12 or 20 people who wanted to marry their deceseed wife*, sister and did not see that by that Act they were altering the ntsndards of marriage in the eyes of 40,000,0041 of people. The proof of that had come with striking rapidity. In all things the Church of England had gone ahead during the past year except in the number of admiring. in Ehurch.The Nonconformists went back even more than they and there was a great increase in merriagee in the registry office.. It was obvious that a marriage in the registry office,without a word of prayer, was nut going to be as binding as • marriage in church where Goil wascalled an. witnees.The appalling state in America where it was said that one in every 10 marriages ended in divorce wan entirely due to dt•orce being easy to get. if people were separated there was always the opportunity of them coming together. A little time ago in one year there were 11,00 separations and all were reconciled except 1,800 whereas:! divorce had been easily obtainah le they would have been serarated for ever—and what would hate become of the children ? It was said that they nhould equalise and put the poor iii the name poeition an the rielt.Yesee supposed they must,but there were two ways in which they could equalme—erst by drugging down the poor to the level of the rich or by mining the rich to the level of the poor. The view of the Christian Cuurch was this: that marriage watt no contract entered into between two parties to be brought toss end be the will of the pnrties. Marriage way n relationship .• ill death doth on part," And the heshand could no more cease to be the hosbend of the wife, and the wife could no more cease to be the wife of the hunhand, than the children could cense to be the children oP their parents. /letting quoted several texts in support of his contentions the rev. randoma, said tint they should not allow a small minority to do tiat which by weakening the person's sense of the sacredness of the relation between the sexes would open an apptilline future for women and which by undermining the feruily life would undermine that on which the futn e of the nation re-tryout-I.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Gerrard gave an interesting paper on Mon- day evening to the members of the Bewsey.road Wesley Guild. He took RS hi subject," Land Values," and dealt with it in as non-partisan a manner as possible, explaining the single mx position from an economic standpoint. Mr. W. Healey presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild</head>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild</p>
        <p>The monthly literary meeting of the Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild is invaritily mcli attended, as was the ca. on Moodily evening last. Mr. S. Blythe pre- sided. The meeting took the form of " Queries," each member writing out questions, which were transferred to a hattaken out and answered by those who got them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There web again -a good meeting of thin aociety on Sonday. presided over by Owen, w ho spoke upoit " Peraonal influence.' Mr. if. S. Atkin gave a Bible reading address on the 34th Psalm. He said the prni. of God should be continually in their mouths. They should live in the spirit of praise. That did not mean they could always ha talking .d singing, but they ehould live the life of praise and thanksgiving. Mina Jones sang sweetly " When I survey the Wondrous Croon" and "Come unto lb." Mr. Payton, Mon. Ilan., presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Mission.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Mission.</p>
        <p>At the school, Ellesraere-street Mission, on Sunday prizes were distributed by Mrs. Ferguson and Mrs' Pattison, for regular attendance and good conduct. Dr. Ferguson gave on sddresejof distinct merit. During the afternoon the children sang special music, and Mica Whitmore rendered sweetly " Behold me." There wag a line attendance and sri excellent collection. Others taking pert were Mies Ilurnphries, Mr. Jones and Mr. Hulse. During the week were rendered "The Brahman Wedding " end " Doctor and Devil Prieet " reepectively by Bold-street Guild Party and the Bewsey-road friends, ton full house. Each group did ezceedivgl3 well. The school Superintendent, Mr. Levi Richardson presided, and Mr. G. Welker and Mr, John Barlow gave addresses. The children rendered appropriate songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Buckley street Sunday School.</head>
        <p>Buckley street Sunday School.</p>
        <p>The annual prize dietribation took place last Sunday in the chapel, which was crowded by scholars and friends. To 113 scholars as many rewards were given for good attendance, this number of hoys and girls having made the requisite number of attendancee. viz.. 110 and over during the year. Mr. H. Taylor presided as the opening of the meeting and an address was given by Mr. Brigga, of Leigh. who urged the scholars to always strive for the attainment of the highest and noblest rewards. The prizes were distribitted by the teachers and Sunday school official; than stimuleting the mutual regard for each other already existing !between officiale and echolars. Before concluding the !happy gethering,the thanks of all preeent were accorded to the School Secretaries, Messrs. S. liindon and S. !Adams for their untiring efforts in the intereete of the school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Bewsey road Wesleyan Teaparty.</head>
        <p>Bewsey road Wesleyan Teaparty.</p>
        <p>Tan annual congregational tenparty in connection with the itewsey-road Wesleyan Church is tawnys popular feature, as was further evidenced by the large number of people who attended the function on Thurs- day Between 150 and 200 sat down tote., in the Lecture Hall, after which the prize distribution took place in the chapel adjoining, Here the numbere were greatly augmented the area of the church being well filled. , The Silver-street Male Voice Choir and the SliVef - street Children's Choir under the leadership of Mr. W. Hipkins rendered two selections each, which were much apprecisted. Miss Fanny Keeling kindly distributed the prizes to the children for regular attendance and good behaviour and Mr. Stevens submitted the secre- tary's report, which stated that the past year had been one of the most successful on record. The number of scholars on the books was 460, and the average afternoon attendance was 101. The amount of financial help received had just about met all claims. The usual vote of thanks to those who had rendered assistance for the carrying out of the Church and Sunday School work during the year, to those who had provided trays for the tea and to those who had helped to make the evening's gathering a swum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Bold-street Cripples Guild.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Cripples Guild.</p>
        <p>I The promoters of the Bold-street Cripples' Guild are to be heartily congratulated on the good work they hsve been doing since the Guild's inception some twelve months ago. Meetings are held every alternate l.Cednes• day, when several crafts are skilfully taught these unfortunate children, no that despite their greet handi- cap they might be placed in the severe position of depending upon none for their livelihood. Of course there are some who will never be able to work, in the ordinary sense of the word, but nevertheless they enjoy immensely the m.tings, which happily combine spiritual atmosphere with the hope of materiel advancement. •ro vary the procedure the social element is occasionally introduced end this was so on Thursday evening, when over one hundred crippled children of all denominations net down to an enjoyable tenths Cripples Guild committee (appointed by the Wesley Guild) having riti.d sufficient money to provide a substantial meal. After tea a concert was held, presided over by Mr. M. 0, Bennett. The items were admirably presented. They included : .Iction song by the Thewlis-street Band of hope (trained by Miss Vane); songs, Mies Eva Turner and Miss Davy ; recitations, Miss Corney ; action songs, by the stuckuts of the Peoples College (in charge Mi. Wallington) ; and Mr. S. Ackland (humorist). Those who kad helped to make the proceedings the success they undoubtedly were, were thanked on the proveition of Mr. liamblett seconded by Mr. W. Twit..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Emmanuel Reformed Church.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Reformed Church.</p>
        <p>Rev. William T. Jaye and the Mormons</p>
        <p>Concluding his subject Mormon Blasphemy "at Emmanuel Men's Bible Class on Sunday afternoon, the Bev. William T. Jaye adduced reasons why Christians cannot have communion with Mormons, and laid particular stress on the teaching of Mormonism that God is an exalted man who was once as Co are now,who is for ever changing, ever advancing, becoming more and more perfect, but never reaching absolute perfection. Ile quoted from a sermon preached by l'rophet " Joseph Smith, who declared, It is the first principle of the Gospel to kn.- that He Godl was once a man like us ; yea, that God, himself, the Father of os all dwelt on an earth, the stone as !es. Christ himself you have got to learn how to be good yourselves, the same as all gods have done before you. namely by going from one small degree to another, and frotoot small capacity to a great one." Alr. Jave said it would bean utter impossibility to accept such teachiag, and at the same time profess belief incur Articles of Religion which be trusted they all as loyal churchtnen,didifor Article fin &amp;declaration that "There is but one living and true God, everlasting, without body, parts, or passions." Many pasaages or Scripture Avers referred to as supporting the orthodox position. To suppose that Almighty God, who hom exiated from all eternity, and will continue to ellst to all future eternity, is circuraacribed by body, consists of material parts, or is liable to passions, is utterly inconeistent with our ideas of infinite perfection, because a body is subject to certain laws, and can only be in 03A place at a time, whereas Clod is a being equally present everywhere. In conclusion, Mr. lace asked them all ever to remember that the Mormon Church claims to be infallible, and that its Prophets "are inspired men and that their utterances and writings are superior to the utterances and writings of the holy men of the Bible. As recently as 1,97 a Mormon " elder " said, "The living oracles are worth more than all the Bibles." lie (the speaker) could not do better ! than close his addre. hy reminding them that the original Protest, presented to the diet at Spires in 1529 by the Lutheran Princes of Germany, and from which the name of Protestent was derived, contain. the following plumage : "There is no sere doctrine hut such as is conformable to the Word of God. Mi.</p>
      </div>
