      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RT. HON. JAMES LOWTHER, M.P.</head>
        <p>THE RT. HON. JAMES LOWTHER, M.P.</p>
        <p>tLonsiderable excitement has been caused tins week in the House of Commons by Mr. Mandl, MR. for North Westmeath, publishing a private letter written to him by Mr. Wedgwood, M.P., and in which the latter charged the Speaker with partiality. Mr. Wedgwood apologised for having written the letter, but Mr. Ginnell reinained defiant, and was, on a motion of Mr. Asquith, suspended from the service of the " House '' for a week.</p>
        <p>:R.WEDGWOOD BENN, M.P.</p>
        <p>In the recent I.hel action hetweeni Mr.</p>
        <p>Simmons and Mr. Wedgwood Bonn, M.R, Junior Liberal Whip, Mr. Benn has agreed to pay £2,500 damages and to withdraw the statements complained of. It will be remembered that the action was taken in respect of the distribution and circulation of a libel when Mr. Simmons was the Unionist candidate in the recent election Ice St. George's-in-the• East.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolverhampton, whose critical conditton is causing grave anxiety. He is, perhaps, better known as Sir H. H. Fowler, Member for Wolverhampton for twenty- eight years. He has held many important appolntinents, being Pcrsidrnt of the Local Government Board, Secretary of State for India. Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, and President of the Council. He became a Peer on the reconstruction of the Ministry by Mr. Asquith.</p>
        <p>, • lOW • 1 • • 4.c.:</p>
        <p>LORD WOLVERHAMPTON.</p>
        <p>. 4,1</p>
