      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RAIL-LESS TRAM CAR</head>
        <p>A RAIL-LESS TRAM CAR</p>
        <p>The above illustration is that of the first rail-less tramway car built in this country, and is undergoing its trial runs in the North-West of London. The car is driven by electrical power supplied by means of overhead wires. A wonderful revolution in transit is shortly to take place by this system. Corporations in various parts of the country are considering the system for reaching outlying districts by easy means.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>itriaGICTON CHAPTERS.</head>
        <p>itriaGICTON CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>THE BATTLE OF LAND AND SEA.</p>
        <p>THE GREAT SUBSIDENCE OF THE SIXTH CENTURY.</p>
        <p>We shall begin our narrative of some ancient tragedies by asking our readers to picture the results of a rapid subsidence in the corner of the world on which we happen to live. Taking the coast of Lancashire from its northermost limits and extending our survey to the Island of Anglesea, we are to imagine a fall of the land of sixty to eighty feet.</p>
        <p>By the aid of a physical map we could form the new coast line, though our courage would scarcely be equal to a contemplation of it. A great arm of the sea would wash inland beyond Manchester, above which the tallest buildings of Birkenhead, Liverpool, Warring- ton and Manchester alone would show them- selves. Hill ChM) would stay the tido, and there would be seven or eight miles of land behind it, hut beyond this would spread another inland sea engulphing Northwich and reaching to Middlewich. Another arm of the sea would reach far up the valley of the Dee covering Chester and reaching a dozen miles inland. We ask for a consideration of such an alarming inundation because it is the only way of realizing the vast changes which have taken place within historical times. Dis- comfiting as the thought may be the facts force us to recognize that we live upon sinking land. The coasts of Lancashire, Cheshire and Wales have lowered fifty feet in historical times, and are sinking still at a rate of 3 feet in a century.</p>
        <p>The coast line was several miles beyond the Great Orme. A river called the Ell (now the Ogwen) flowed between Anglesey and Puffin Island to the sea while the Conway found its way across the low land east of Little Orme. Between these two rivers lay the delightsome smiling vale where Helig reared his palace, the monarch of all he' surveyed. Such is the picture here drawn, and every modern enquiry tends to confirm it. But we shall not do our full duty unless we copy also the legend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>CHESHIRE COAST.</head>
        <p>THE CHESHIRE COAST.</p>
        <p>We propose to mention in this article the historical and traditional evidences of coast changes, but before doing so we may take note of some evidences which lie on the Cheshire coasts.A walk from New Brighton to Hoylake at low tide will enable the visitor to inspect the tree roots of the forest in the midst of which the Liver Pool was a fresh water lake. Nor is it only an ancient forest that has been submerged. Not long ago a number of skeletons laid in rows showed that a churchyard and presumably a village had stood at a spot opposite to Leasowe Castle. A little further, upon what is called the ' Dove Spit stood a Roman town of some importance, and relics in the form of Roman coins are constantly dredged up. Some 7,000 antiquities, we are told, of British, Roman or Saxon date have come from beneath the sea line on the Che Shire Coast, and relics of the same kind come from every quarter between us and the far-away Cardigan Bay. The great insweep of the sea following upon the land subsidence is the explanation of the puzzle we described in outlast chapter. When Ptolemy made his map in the second century there was no Mersey, except as a tributary to the Dee, but with the sinking of I the land the sea swept in as far as Runcorn, making an inland salt water estuary and giving a separate outlet to the river. Whether the Mersey at once abandoned its old channel we have no weans of know- ing, but it seems to have flowed past Liver- pool for some fourteen centuries and by the aid of the tides has scoured out the fine channel which is the pride and glory of Britain's second seaport. The only historical reference to the inundation which we have been able to find is that of Camden who records under datel A.D. 563 : "There occurred in Cheshire an inundation of the sea by which 5,000 persons and an innumerable quantity of cattle perished." In Wales, however, has been preserved a story ever so much better than a bald and unpicturesque statement, and it is this, we imagine, in which our readers will find the chief interest. THE WELSH LEGEND OF A SUBMERGED PALACE.</p>
        <p>Through uncounted —generations a tradition , has persisted in North Wales that there lies near the foot of Great Orme's Head, deep under the sea,a fair palace called Llys Helig. In the songs of the ancient bards not only the fact but the details of the great disaster have lingered, and we owe it to the care and interest of a Welsh clergyman, whose; name cannot now tv ascertained, that these bating statements were gathered and com- pared. It is first mentioned in a poem by the British bard Taliesin who flourished A.D. 520 to 570. He refers to the event as having occurred within the memory of his contem- poraries which would put the date about A.D. 520. It occurs in the Welsh Triads and Gorhoffet six centuries later.</p>
        <p>The traditions describe the land that lay beyond Great Orme's Head 3 to 4 miles to the north and gives names to the "delicate vale abounding in fruitfulness" that is now the Menai Straits. This is the narrative as it comes to us:</p>
        <p>HOW HMG LOST HIS GREAT ESTATE.</p>
        <p>" Helig ;as; grandson of Caradoe the Valiant King of North Wales. He himself was Lord of Abergele, Rhos and Lleyo and was also Earl of Hereford. In his time happened the great inun- dation which surrounded Morla Ithianedd, the most delicate, fruitful and pleasant vale lying from Bangor to Great Orme's head to go to Deganwy in length, and in breadth from</p>
        <p>Penmaemnawr to the point of Flintaffire which came up from Rhuddlan to Priesthnlme (now Puffin Island), in the upper end whereof did extend in breadth from Aber and Llanfairfechan to the river Ell, which did divide Caere arvon horn Mon (now Anglesey) and did likewise divide Mon from Flintshire, running between Priestholme and Penmon and so discharging itself into the sea a great way beyond Priest- holism, and did surround many other rich aid fruitful bottoms and vales within the counties of Carnarvon,Fliot and Anglesey moat of them being the land of Helig at Glanawg. Belig's chiefest palace stood in this vale much about the middle way from Penmaenmawr and Gogarth (Great Orme) the rains whereof are now to be seen upon a ground about some two miles within the sea directly against the Point of Wailing, whieh is a hill lying in the midst of the parish of Dwygyfylchi (near Penomenmawr) to which hill Helig at Glanawg and his people did run up to save themselves, being endangered with the sudden breaking in of the sea and there saved their lives, and being come up to the point of that hill and looking back, beholding that dreadful and ruthful spectacle instead of their incomparable vale, excelling all other sales in fertility and pleasantness, Helig and his people wringing their hands together, bewailing their misfortune, the point of which is called to this day Trwyn yr-Wylfa, the point of the Dolefull Hill. Helig bad another manor house at Pullheli, the ruins whereof are to be seen on the right hand as you go out of the town towards Aberech ; this own was called Pullhelig, and of late Pullheh. He lived for the most part at either of these houses.</p>
        <p>With such a subject the scribe might have made much more of his story.. But we are concerned here not with the human or dramatic side so much as the geographical. On this side the document is illuminating.</p>
        <p>NORTH WALES COAST LINE IN STH CENtt;RY WITH POSITION OV LLYS HELM.</p>
        <p>THE SUBMERGED PALACE OF HELIG.</p>
        <p>It was not until 1864 that any discovery was made which gave confirmation to the story of Helig's palace. In that year Mr. C. R. Hall with several friends sailed out at an abnormally low tide and was able to trace dark lines of seaweed that seemed to mark the dim outline of walls. It remained for Mr. W.Ashton,whose book on the subject we commended in our last article; to demonstrate beyond reasonable doubt that a palace did really stand in what is now he bay of Penmaenmawr. Walls 130 yards long and perfectly straight were traced clearly, and within them on one side the base of a large round tower. Mortar from the joints of the stones proved the walls to be of human formation.</p>
        <p>A raised road or causeway of stone was found, which ran in the direction of Penmaen- mawr, and though enciiisted with marine deposits, its stones were scarcely displaced by fourteen centuries of moving tide and storm. A ROMAN TOWN UNDER THE CARDIGAN BAY. _ _</p>
        <p>During the past few days Mr. Ashton has furnished to us particulars of the results of another exploration in the Cardigan Bay,the interesting details of which have not yet been published. In one of Taliesin's poems is the mention of Caer Arianrhod a Roman town or fortress. The name and memory linger in tradition also, but the place has long been lost to sight. Eight miles due west of Barmouth the ruins have been located under the sea. The discoverer approached them by the causeway which runs out from Mochras Island, south of Harlech, a road well-known to antiquarians and which can be traced for 18 miles under the sea. Two other such Sams or causeways are known under the Cardigan Bay and some day we may follow them far enough to know whence they led. Ptolemy's map which we referred to in a previous article shows a coast line which roughly corresponds with what is now the 10 fathoms line, and this was probably the extent of Roman England. The submerged towns and villages, Roman and British, awake a deep and melancholy interest, and the relics of other days that strew our coasts form a subject and a speculation so great that we are here only able to touch it in veriest outline. A. M.</p>
        <p>e #0 Amcf..... . . ..,:,.,,, /o'4 , : ....:'. s Nap...ir 0 L•fri ....., 51,.. S' • 043 OM, - : t C. • . NORM RniANNEOO 47 X .. \-• ...,..;iyiei •*-4i. a `,.,;,,,, -t. ••,. ":.. '.... • 'I-. '7. P ' ' = \P' X</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>L Y. 5 MEI/6</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>TRAGEDY OF LLYS HELM.</head>
        <p>THE TRAGEDY OF LLYS HELM.</p>
        <p>The fall ci Lly. iffeli—g—tv—as-PWPbesied for generation.; and a threat had gone forth, that vengeance ehould overtake the family of Ilelig alt tilanawg for the crimes of his ancestors. Night after night on wild rocks and shores., amidet the hills and in the valleys, was heard the fearfol cry of " Dial a ddaw dial a ddaw ! " but the wailer wasinvisible to all. At length it came, and suddenly, as mighty calamities, even though dreaded, generally do. There was a great feast in the house of Ifolig, and the guests forgot in their jovial carousal, that fate was only pausing to overtake Mem. They called for more wine, and a servant was des- patched into the cellar to procure some, while the old harper sat leaning on hie harp, and the tears ran down upon the strings for his spirit bre- saw some coming evil, They reproached him to hie silence, and he put forth his hand to awak, the chords, when a cry struck hie ear, and he vex: moment the servant who had gone for wine rushed wildly into the ball shrieking " The tide ! the title !" Those two alone had time to quit the house of Ifelig, and found safety in the mountains ; all besides were swallowed—lands, flocks and villages—by the tempestuous torrent ; and the fertile vale of Conway for miles was all one eheet of foaming waters as it remains to this day.</p>
        <p>ST. TUDNO'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Several local names keep up the memory of the tragedy of Llys Helig. The Hill bf Wailing to which the dwellers of the plain fled still bears that title, and the name Lavan Sands has the same meaning "Point of Wailing or Watching," while " Traeth Wylofan " means "inlet of lamentation." Yet another relic of that distant day sur- f fives in the quaint church of St. Tudno, perched on the edge of Great Orme's Head. This could only have been built for those who lived on the plain at the foot of the rocks, and the road by which the worshippers ascended may still be traversed to the water edge.</p>
      </div>
