      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS NEGLIGENCE AND LOSS OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>The Report of the Chief Inspector of Mines on the Whitehaven Mine Disaster is an extremely serious document and places those responsible for the management of the pit in an unenviable position. The cause of the explosion was initiated by the ignition of fire damp, and the explosion was carried by means of coal dust. The Report says that the precautions against the accumulation of coal dust were of a haphazard and unsystematic character. The haulage road had been allowed to become choked with dust, and the watering had not been carried ' out. The ventilation of the mine is stated to have been inadequate, and the manage- ment generally appear to have been indiffer- ent to the dangers of the mine. The ventila- tion, in fact, is said to have been inadequate to meet the requirements of the Mines Regulation Act. Mr. Popc,the legal authority, while admitting that there has been breaches of statutory duty on the part of the manage- ment, does not think that a criminal prosecu- tion could be sustahed, partly owing to the fact that the law does not insist upon certain things being done which experience has shown ought tube done, and which are done where the management is efficient and where safety is a first consideration. The Report calls attention to the almost comolete failure of the rescue operations, to which it will be remembered a good deal of prominence was given at the Coroner's inquest. Rescue parties with breathing apparatus arrived at the pit 27 hours after the explosion, when it was far too late to be of any service. The Chief Inspector now reports that the unfortunate experi- ence at Whitehaven is a convincing argu- ment in favour of the provision at every colliery of trained rescue parties, and means of summoning them at short notice. It should not have required an explosion and the loss of 136 lives, to get an argument in favour of the provision of rescue appliances and trained rescue parties at every pit. This has been done on the continent for years, and it is a serious reflection upon the British mining industry that such provision should be wanting here. The miners' leaders have been demanding the compulsory provision of these appliances for a long time back, and at last the Home Office is beginning to move in the matter, or at least they have promised to begin to move. SOME OBVIOUS DEDUCTIONS.</p>
        <p>As an outsider who knows little or nothing about practical mining I should not venture to express an opinion upon any technical matter, but there are certain things which strike one in reading this Report which seem to suggest that there has been neglect on the part of somebody else than the management. One is inclined to ask if there is such a thing as mine inspection, and if so what the Inspectors were doing to allow the state of ' things in the mine, which is described in the Report, to continue. It seems to me that , practically every complaint, every possible I or contributory cause of the explosion which is indicated in the Report, should have been known to the Inspector and ought to have been attended to before. The Inspector knew that there were no rescue appliances , nearer than a hundred miles away. Why was not the necessity of better provision urged by him upon the management and upon the Home Office. The absence of a second main intake air road was a fact which was known before the accident, and it ought to have occurred to a practical man that such a means of access would be necessary in case of an explosior, but the making of such a road never appears to have been previously suggested. The road had not been systematically cleared of dust, and the water mains were not in use for drenching purposes. Surely inspection ought to have revealed these things before the accident. It is now reported that the ventilation was inadequate. Why was not that report made before? I could not imagine anything more conclusive of the inadequacy of the inspec- tion than this Report which is compiled by the Chief Inspector of Mines. But I am not blaming the Inspector who had the supervision of the mine. The blame attaches to the Home Office. The number of mine inspectors is so inadequate that it is impossible for an inspector to keep a strict oversight upon the pits in his district. As a matter of fact, on the average it is only possible for the inspector to make an examination of each mine in his area about once in four years. Mr. Churchill, in reply to the Trade Union deputation which waited upon him last week, said that his Office had got a Coal Mines Regulation Bill drafted, and he hoped very shortly to intro- duce it. It was, he said, a very bulky document, but he gave no indication of the scope of the Bill. This Report upon the Whitehaven disaster, coming at the time when such startling evidence is being given in the Pretoria pit case, should surely secure for the Bill, if it be worth supporting, a very favourable reception from the House of Commons. THE OBLIGING HOME SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The deputation which waited upon the Home Secretary last week, and to which I have referred, urged upon his attention a number of other questions which had been the subject of resolutions by the Trades Union Congress. The Home Secretary is a past master in the art of using words to con- ceal information, but he probably never gave a better illustration of his ability in that direction than in his speech in reply to this deputation. It would pass the wit or wisdom of any living man to find in the column report of his speech any definite statement as to the intentions of the Government in regard to the many questions upon which le islation was asked, beyond the intimation th t a Coal Mines Regulation Bill is drafted. On he questions of "one man one horse," the licensing of carmen, state insurance under the Compensation Act, half-time labour\ and Industrial schools, Mr. Churchill was Olightfully non-committal, but I am not an need to see at the end of the report of the in erview that the deputation "very cordiall thanked the Home Secretary, because they must have felt that the tulsome raise he showered upon the wisdom and use mess of trade unions was a more than su dent compensation for coming away e tyhanded of practical concessions. T USUAL INDIKMRENCE. Burin the many years I have taken an interest in the Unemployment question I have in ariably found that there was very little ge eral interest in the subject when trade w sin a fairly good condition. Two years • o, unemployment was the most talked ut question in the country. Every newspa r was making this one of its pro- minent atures. Government departments were m king desperate efforts to create an Ipres ion that they were anxious to do ' ALA .</p>
        <p>were imr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>something for the unemployed. Nos is good. The percentage of unem returned to the Board of Trade for Ja last is less than half what it was two ago. Now the cry of the twiemplo silent in the Isnd. There are no proct of the workless, and the Distress Corn and local authorities are free from ti All this is really very sad ; for as an trade is better to-day it will Is to-morrow. New is the time to be something which can be useful for a of extra unemployment. I The difficulty of doing something the need is urgent is that it is not el put large schemes of work into motio moment's notice. If trade were to I next winter, there is not a local autho the country which would be in ono better position to find work for the ployed than they were in the last bad And the Government would be in jL same position. The unemployed qu can only be properly treated by sus and well-thought out action. Times o trade should be used for preparation f lean times which will follow. It incredible, but it is the fact, that matter public bodies and the Goveri departments will not learn by expel as soon as ever the pressure of the agi is removed these bodies cease to tak interest in the unemployed question after all it is the indifference of the which is really responsible.</p>
        <p>WHAT BOY SCOUTS PROM IS</p>
        <p>I know that some of my nephews are ested in scouting. I believe that a many of them are. This being so tilt like to have tie "Boy Scouts Pm which has been prepared by General I Powell. The following is the promi code of honour. I hope every ho: becomes a scout will observe it careful press it upon the notice of his frien comrades.</p>
        <p>1. I will do my duty to Cod and the King. 2. I will do my best to help others, win costs me.</p>
        <p>3. I know the Peout law and will obey it. The points of scout law referred to are: A scout's honour is to be trusted. 2. A scout is loyal to the King,and to his to his country, and to his employers. 3. The scout's duty is to be useful, and others.</p>
        <p>4. The trout is a friend to all, and a br every other scout, no matter to what social I other belongs..</p>
