        <p>" 00, yer thinks so, does yew, Mr. Go-as- yer-plesee—not for this 'ere 'virtuous child— you stump up and mighty sharp. Do y' year?" he shouted, pushing his ugly face into Bland- ford's. Yer thinks to bounce Joe Niggins, but yer jest made a 'ell of a mistake." Blandford took not the slightest notice of this tirade am: the rogue changed his tune, saying, while a cunning leer distorted his tea-</p>
        <p>turn; " It'd scarce be safe to cheat me. I l. ways sed yes was a sharp 'no. Tip me and get yew the brass, there's sich a thievish let sushi. 'ere." "My dear Niggins, you're deluding your- self, my friend is only sleeping off a booze.Niggins Flanced at the bed, and seeing the strsuger, sitting erect, he buret into a string</p>
        <p>of blitspheinies-.. Blannford remained perfectly collected, and said gently. "we arc hungry, go, there's a good fellow, and purchase some provisions, a bottle of thwar's whisky, and three J.S. ?Juries cigars." The shifty-eyed ruffian glared, his features convulsed with evil passion. "Wort!" he snarled, his teeth showing on either side of his mouth like the fangs of a vicious wolf. " Yer'd make a mark o' this 'ere child, would yew, I ain't a-goin' to do a stretch for yer, not me." "Nor shall you, my esteemed friend," said Blandford, smiling a smile that set the animal's blood blazing. aht,'' growled Niggins. " 11l temper has nothing to do with the pre- vent ease." Nothin' ter do Aviv the else, why Mime, it 'as my vital spark, an' me a tryin' ter earn my livin' by the sweat of me brow. I ain't gain' to give yew a chnunce, not me." blandford took the ruffian by the arm, and gave it such a squeeze as both astounded and cowed him. "Bniggin—Niggins, I mean—the alteration of name is forgetfulness on my part," said Blandford in his best society tone, "we are not going to quarrel." " On, ain't we. 0' course not, oh, no," sneered Niggins. " No, we are not. In short, my dear and amiable landlord, we will, to URA your own vernacular, be pals. Understand?" " Bloomin' Walker," sneered Niggins. 'Quite so, and to show there is no animo- sity, you shall join our festive board. Niggins wrenched his arm from the other's grip, but before he could open his mouth to pore forth a flood of oaths, Blandford maid sot thingly "There's a sovereign, otherwise twenty bob, good, honest gold, with which to go maiketing. Borrow plates, knives, forks, and Otte glasees." - 'nay</p>
