      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>(ALL RIONTS RERISRVEDI. •</head>
        <p>(ALL RIONTS RERISRVEDI. •</p>
        <p>A MYSTERY OF MAYFAIR,</p>
        <p>JOLIN STRANGE WINTER.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OP OPENING CGAPTBHS. The lion. •Icc Blandfoni, having squandered his fortune, mgr. teem Neeisity under • cloud, and Is eventually dsivon es seek shelter le a lodging.houte kept by a' fence" in the 811Ve• Innis—one of rho must notorious &amp;arida of London. tine wet night, ea he is creeping stealthily up the 1111-ty states 10 .0,14 his landlord, to 1•110.1 he owes several weeks' rent, he st wattles over • testy lying on the landing outside the deor of his own ram. A lighted match reveals a well. ' dmsowd nun apparently dead. Lie is hilly clothed, hot hie testis hate been removed by his Sgl.ll/111t to prevent ktrunfiestimi. Blandioni Mule, slier conveying the man late ilitl M 1 KIM, 1111.1 he is still alive. On reco.rlng conisqounitens he tell. • rambling story of having been attacked by I IS oh a uffeur, and of a Mayfair inansion wherein is kleiden IngeM of gold and other wonders. Blandford ttlinini that the stranger I. fluttering from hallucinations, and endeavours to extract his name and from whence he came. But the litture to his limd has cones,t toirtial Ims .4 memory. While BUndford Is turning over the whole matter in hls nand • terrific knocking at the door lakes Oleos.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IV. WM 14100105, TER LANDLORD. The stranger's throat was dry, and his eyes darkened with apprehension, as lifting him- self on his elbow, he demanded wildly, Who Ii there Say." To this appeal Blandford gave no heed, his heart was bounding in his bosom, he was anxiously thinking of the perils dwelling in the Abode of Wickedness. Another bang on the panel, a hoarse curse from the party demanding admittance, and Blandford uttered an angry exclamation be- tween his teeth, roughly pushing his guest on the bed, intimating to him he should lie quite still. Spite of his apparent calm, Blandford was in an agony of suspense. Standing motion- less, his hand resting on the table, arrang- ing a plan to avoid discovery. A knock fell on the door, louder than the last. " Biases take yer, do yer year, I wants my rent." "I am involving you in an unpleasantness," said the man from the bed. "Not the slightest," rejoined Blandford, cheerfully; now he knew for certain who was on the outside of the door, he could afford to</p>
        <p>laugh. " This man's visit is anything but a bles-</p>
        <p>sing." _ _ " No, I'll settle the boast's rent, and the sooner we shall have the room to ourselves. All right, what the devil do you want?" Thin in answer to another bang on the panel. The stranger raised hie white face from the pillow, saying: "I'm infernally hungry, and the cut at the back of my head is smarting." "Hungry is not a name for the gnawinge of my emptiness," replied Blandford, rue- fully. " You shall have some hot water presently and I'll dress tour wound. Leave me to manage Hie creature ramping outside. I'm going to ad- mit the landlord. Hide, I'd rather he did not see you. Do you object to my covering you from head to foot with the counterpane?" "Not at all."</p>
        <p>" Blandford pulled the ragged coverlet well over the body of the man, and then threw open the door, at the same moment drawing back well into the centre of the room. "Good evening, Mr. Snigginn," said Blandford, promptly, not giving the ruffianly visitor time to speak. " yer, where's me rent? No b---- cheek, pay up, yew 'owlin' toff, or the chuck," spluttered the fellow, beside himself with rage. "He who pays promptly, Mr Bniggins, pays twice the aum." " 'Oo, does 'e? Well, that 'ere ain't yer case. Look a 'ere, my name's Higgins, Mr. Harpy Soft-soap." "Is it written Niggles in the Newgate Calendar?" asked Blandford, sweetly. "Non of. yer bloomin' larks wi' me, I ain't i • cove as s to be jumped on," speaking in an aggressively bullying tone. "I want the brew," bawled the truculent, closely-cropped ruffian, shaking his sinewy-knotted fist sav- agely at his tormentor. "Fifteen shillings, I believe, Mr. Snig—l beg pardon, Niggins. Three week's rent at fee shillings per week is fifteen bob. I'll trouble you for a receipt." "Not me, Mr. Impudence, yew knows darned well I can't rite." "Them affix your cross here," said Bland- ford, pointing to a scrap of paper upon which he had written the amount in pencil. Niggins hesitated, bat evest•ally did as he was requested, and grabbed the money from the table. "'Ow 'ave yer been a-robbin' of, Mr. 'igh and mity?" "Unlike most of your lodgers, no one."</p>
        <p>Higgins fired up at this insinuation. " Don't yew say anythin' agen the respect- ability of my 'ouse, ver slippery, black-coated beetle. 'Elio!" he shouted, then in a shrink- ing, trembling whisper, "What's that 'ere on yer bed?" Blandford laid • finger on his lips, dolled and locked the door, and looked mysteriously into the empty and rusty grate. This scared the bully. "Look year, yew jist clear out o' this I crib," said Higgins in • menacing voice of alarm. " I shall leave here to-morrow," said Blandford, intensely polite. " 'Oe, will yew?" rejoined the rogue, sneering. " l'cr'eps Joseph Higgins will 'eve somethin' to say on that 'ere point." "Take the approaching separation lightly, as worthy landlord." "Ter thinks yerself • funny cuss, I don't see miffing to laugh at," and here Mr. Hig- gins began to show signs of grief. "I've paid you a week's rent instead of notice." Niggina opened his mouth. and his eyes positively bulged, he could only mutter, " '0o," he gasped. "Now Mr. Higgins, this is my room, my private property, you are trespassing, there- fore I shall be obliged if yon will clear out." "Well, of all the bloomin' cheek! Clear out! Not before I knows wtrat'• stretched on that there bed ef mine." "For Heaven's sake, don't," cried Bland- ford, pretending great uneasiness. "Got the swag under the blankets," asked the ruffianly landlord, jerking his thumb over his right shoulder in direction of the bed. "You've no right to be prying into my business," said Blandford, with dignity. "No right! Am I to 'ave a 'onest man's crib raided by cops? Not Joseph Higgins. lam a-grin' to the station," making believe he was leaving the room in hot haste. "No, don't, Mr. Higgins, pray remember that your house would be at once put on the visiting list of the police." "Well, I won't. Alves, yew know, and I'll chine up my dooty," winking and sidling to- wards the bed. "Stop, halve, be it," exclaimed Blandford, smiling and gently drawing down the counter-pane from the stranger's face. Higgins recoiled, his teeth chattering. " Bo 'elp me—it stiff 'us—" The blood-streaked face on the pillow was ghastly in the flame of the expiring candle. His eyes were closed, he was the picture of one who had been done to death by violence. Blandford retreated to the door, and Higgins yelled: "Don't yew go for to leave me along o' that there on the bed." Hurriedly gripping his lodger's arm and grunting in terror. . . " I say Cully—this 'ore's a 'anging job. Were's tke blunt? Gorblime, I'll 'are my whack ou of it." "Ton have your rout, and you'll obtain ne</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>/lintened, he pt</head>
        <p>/lintened, he pt</p>
        <p>'I be burry ruffian'. eyes Ohne"-, on the coin as it lay in the palm of his hand, tossed it into the air and tested the gold be- tween his strong teeth. Satisfied that it was not a duffer, he said: "Gorblime, yer chummy—no balmy kiddin?" he asked sus- piciously. " Real grit, my esteemed friend," rejoined Blandford, promptly. "Spivini grinned the landlord. Bland- ford 's cheerful comradeship reducing the surly ruffian to complete friendliness. "Gor- blime, that bloke's spry," said Niggins, ad- miringly. " Lor-a-mighty only knars 'is gaitne. Look a per, Mister Clever, I gits a livin"onest, de yew see: I'm keerful, s'welp me." " Take care of the change, Mr. Sniggins," remarked Blandford, warningly. " Wort do yer think I'll glue the bobs in my pocket," said Niggins, indignation suffus- ing his dirty countenance. " Yer blessed fast takin"onest bloke's carikters away. 'Ovr- somever I'll bring tfie grub and the boose in a jiffy," and Niggins bounced from the room, his iron-shod boots could be heard clattering on the uncarpeted stairs, to his den below. Niggins out of the way, Blandford became strictly esrious. After glancing at the dozing stranger on the truckle bed, he lighted a partly-consumed cigarette, which he had al- lowed to die out while the discussion was tak- ing place be-tween the truculent landlord and himself on the subject of food. Flicking the ash from the r-ul of the Egyptian, he moralised. "There's a saying that a man makes the world according to his liking: Fudge! I al- ways had an inclination for riches, simply ab- horred poverty. Yet I've got a Superabund- anee of the latter. So much for musty old savings. My firm belief is that things of this life are preordained. Written in the Book of Fate. ere we are born. Man is but the victim of circumitances, not a creature of impulse, as most people imagine, though at times our inclinations are rest rained by a power stronger than our own. The unexpected event of to-day has entirely effaced the yesterdays of my existence. I have pawned myself as it were to this stranger, this man who has a spell upon his memory, a spell which I must labour to break. A bore, yes, decidedly • bore. Yet I must not be ungrateful to this unknown, for he has enabled me to throw off the stinging cloak of poverty, to pay Niggins his money, and in a few more hones to say farewell to this slough of de.</p>
        <p>" Mr.—Mr.—" said the man on the bed, rousing himself from his lethargy. "Your name?'</p>
        <p>" Blandford." " Pardon me, my head is not quite right yet. Where's the food? I am deucedly hungry and thirsty." " Niggins has just returned, I heard the street door close." Going out on to the landing, Blandford looked down the well of toe staircase, and saw the panting form of his landlora in the passage beneath. Evidently he had been running hard and was trying to recover his breath. Depositing the parcels and the bottle of whisky at the foot of the stairs, he un- laced his coarse boots, tying the ends of the laces together, and dangling them round his neck. In stockinged feet ho stole to the front door and lietened. Stealthily he cleepned into its socket across the door a heavy iron bar, and again he listened. Blandford, intensely curious, watched these maneeuvres. and hissed through his teeth, causing Biggins to upraise his crafty face. "What's wrong, my worthy landlord" Blandford asked from the landing, and lean- ing far over the banister that he might see more plainly. "The cops, a nark 'as guy us away—Gor- blime, Cully, we're in a 'ole, and them there not in the meltin' pot. Gorblime!" yapped Niggins in affright, for a blow on the door re- sounded from top to bottom of the house. The police were demanding admittance.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. TIM UNDERGROUND DASSAGE. The thump, thump, of Niggins' booth feet on the grimy boards sent a ghost-lit echo through the partly deserted house, „V. dashed into the room, scared as a hare cha d by the huntsmen, with the pack in full ory at 111.4 heels. He flung his heaving body on, the bed, grovelling in terror, but retaining i his arms the provisions and the whisky, of hich Eiandford relieved him. His blue-black jaws were trembling, the ungainly brute was hik- ing frail head to foot, and he commenc d to mouth something about the ill-luck of Wing with low-class parlour-jumpers and hi chen- sneaks. The Unknown, who had made no move mast. steadfaslly regarded the two men, corn riring the difference of class under the tree, of danger. Blandford stood in his threai bare, white seamed garments and sodden nets, unshaven cheeks and chin, his hair filed and uncombed, but notwithstanding therm disadvantages, class was stamped on ver- line of his features and his well-knit to . was all repose and self-command. He w dismayed or cowed like Niggins at th preaching police raid, though he foresa imprisonment as a suspected confeder the nefarious dealings of the ruffianly fens.</p>
        <p>fl;are,</p>
        <p>!very . He not that</p>
        <p>might be his portion in the immediate future. If not that, arrest was certain, and how could ho clear himself of complicacy, and at the same time continue to conceal his identity? Ho was determined that before such a dis- graceful disclosure should occur, he would suffer penal servitude. There flashed into his active mind his parents' sorrow, his base in- gratitude, and he resolved there and then to east behind him the deeds of the past. , "Full yourself together, man," he com- manded sternly. The room was full of indistinct shadows, lighted an it was by a wintery candle, and the gloom became overwhelmingly oppressive. " How long will it take the police to force as entraece?" demanded Blandford. Higgins got to his feet from the bed and grinned. He was again the truculent ruffian. " 'Ow ! thet's arright, tek 'em all their toirne, we'll git—leg bail, Cully. The cops ain't trapped Joe Higgins. Catch a weasel asleep and stave 'is eye brows, do thet and yew'll foind me a nanoin'." "We Must our friend on the bed, and ourselves, before we start." The eatables were quickly divided and as quickly devoured, while they listened to the police endeavouring to force an entrance. Blandford wee cool and collected, the Un- known remained mute, and the men eat their food to the last crumb. Blandford's athletic training stood by him now, and Higgins, rogue, receiver, everything that was vile, silently acknowledged his master. "Where's the corkscrew?" asked Bland, ford, shortly. "We must have a long drink iece." Ain't got 'on," said Higgins, knocking the neck off the bottle, from which he was about to drink, but Alec quietly took it froni the fellow's massive hands, and partially filled three glasses. ' No more," he paid authoritatively, in &amp;n- -ewer to Higgins' hint of another supply, "keep the remainder—" There was a crash below, Higgins started and ran to the stairhead. " Blime strite, the cops'll be in—they've bust a ninge," and he slunk into the centre of the room. "Now, finiggins, no funk. You are our guide, we must escape from this den. My friend and I have no desire to be introduced to those gentlemen hammering at your door. Come, don't go to sleep, wake up." "Kelp me, yen' the rite sort, I was hulked; but under a gineral like yew, Joe Higgins is himself. Strip them crab shells,'' pointing to Blandford's wretched boots, for he was moved by admiration at the calmness of his hitherto despised lodger. "Why remove them?" asked Alec, stir-</p>
        <p>"They ereks. Give a 'and, an' elp to git this live corps on his toes." The Unknown, though he find not entered into conversation, perfectly understood what was taking place, and required no urging. He caught hold of the extended hands, and was pulled from the bed tri the floor, standing erect between the two men. " Douse the gum," ordered Higgins. "The what?', asked Blandford. A malicious expression passed across the rogue's wicked face, and he put the flaring candle beneath the bed. " Crep down the stairs as if they was rottin' eggs. Don't sneeze, or con, tck care where yer treads," enjoined Higgins, warn•</p>
        <p>-- . ingly. "Where are you taking us?" asked Bland- ford, drawing away. " Ye' re junked:" chuckled Niggles. "No," whispered Blandford, "but I'll know, before I go a step further." "'Ark to that," said giggles, referring to the crackling of wood below. " Yer'll 'eve , the derbies on yer wrists afore yer can say Jeck Robinson, if yer nails yerself to the Mediu'. I'm square, I is; jest come along slick." "There's a strong smell of burning," re- marked the Unknown, speaking softly. "Rummy, ain't it; very rummy," said Niggles. showing his fangs. "Yew gents stretch yer lags, to save scortellin' yer tails." Blandford, convinced of the futility of fur- ther hesitation, crept down the stairs, sup- porting the stranger, while their disreputable guide went on in front. Down, down in the gloom they went, past the partly-demolished door, add descending more' steps, they heard the clang of heavy bars, and slipped down into cavity of inky blackness. A dark and musty smell rose to their nostrils. "Good heavens," both Blandford and the Unknown exclaimed involuntarily. The trio had plunged suddenly ankle deep into muddy water. 'Don't be skeared, an' don't per clack too ' loud. Now for a light an' a drink," came the husky voice of Niggles, through the darkness, the words echoing again and again among the crannies of the slimy brick arch in which they found themselves. By the' indistinct rays of the candle they saw that they were in some ' underground passage or sewer in the course , of repairs. "There's foive minutes for grub, gents," grinned Niggiii., fastening a lantern to the wall. 1 When the light shone forth there wan a rush of pattering feet, and many squeals and : squeaks, and Blandlord shuddered at the 'numerous tiny sparks he saw outside the in. fluence of the lantern's rats. Rats, rats, the vault was teeming with the vermin. Bland- ford's hand gripped hold of Niggin's coat- collar, he suspected that ho and the Unknown had been drawn into a trap where they would be left to perish. "Wants to knar where per are'" said Niggins, 'winking. "Under the road in an old sewer. If that there .glim warn't ' burnin' them there rets would ind pick yer bones in a jiffy. Thinks lam goin' to play th' double shuffle? No fear, not me, we guts into the streets together. Tek a swig—it will prevent the cold Deena' per innards." The other two recognised the wisdom of the advice, and drank. The spirit roused and warmed their blood from head to foot. "Now peg," and he gave to each man a 'portion of food, and talked while he munched. "We should be three tip-top Tobys, right bang at the top of the pun' fession. We'll be Co. : Yew do the plunder an' I'll git rid of the swag." Ile leered insinuatingly, pressed clam to Blandford, slipping into his tail coat pocket a crimson leather case of no great size. "No thanks," said Blandford, dryly. "The parting of friends in always painful, but when we are once more on terra firma we will shake hands and part." " Part !" roared Niggins, furiously. The rats, whose numbers had increased, scam- pered away in venous directions, scared by the noise. But they returned a moment later, more bold than before, circling nearer and nearer. "Fart !" repeated the ruffian, subduing his harsh voice to a rumble. " S'help me, no mother could look arter 'er precious kid snore than I'll look arter yew, Mr Masher. Part! Tee mine, body and soul." The ruffian thrust his fare into Bland- ford's with such a fierce scowl that the Un- tw kn n pushed in between the two. There w a murderous gleam in the blood-shot e es at the interference. Blandford was on t e point of letting fly with his left, but a r t at that moment sprang on Niggins' mutter and others commenced to attack the -- Inknown , who attempted to beat them off. The teeth of a rat were closing on Niggin's ear, when he crushed the animal between his powerful fingers. Drawing a revolver from a hip pocket he discharged the weapon point blank into the swarming vermin. Thee began a horrible rending, squealing and squeaking, the wounded being devoured by their fellows. Niggins consulted a handsome gold re- peater by the light of the hanging lantern. " Right 0, trot along, cull ies, nip dog's on dooty, and we can git into the street and no ..</p>
        <p>PearT</p>
        <p>cops. After dragging their benumbed feet a few yards further through the muddy slough, they reached a ladder which Biggins mounted, followed by Blandford and his silent companion! The party found themselves at the mouth of an excavation in the road. The slaty clouds were tinged with red, its of the ris- ing sun, and cries of "Fire, fire, fire," rose on the damp night air, swelling louder and louder with every call. Then came the shouts of the swelling crowd, and the rumble of engines and the thud of the pumps. Blandford looked at Biggins, who said in ,newer to the look: "The old crib's ablaze, bat the diiainde is safe," and he was gone.</p>
        <p>CHAPTbn, THU WY lITIEIOUI4 NICILACI. "Don't Alec, you send a sympathetic shud- der ehooting along every nerve in my body. Ratting in a sport, so called, but for three bipeds to be boxed in a drain. surrounded by hundreds of underground scavengers of our city licking their jaws in anticipation of a cannibal feast is too realistic to be pleasant." The speaker was Archer Chinnery, and his listener and host, Alec Blandford, once more attired in the garb of a gentleman. The two friends were lounging in deep arm chairs, in a easily furnished room, one of a set of chamber., situated in Jermyn-street, Hay- market. In the old days, Medford and Captain Chinnery had been the best of chums, at the 'Varsity they had been inseparables; there- fore it is got strange that after their separa- tion they should be exchanging confidences. To Chienery's credit, he it said, helmet never cold shouldered or shunned the back-slider, and indeed he had been greatly hurt that Alec, in his extremity, had been too proud to aek for assistance, which would have been gladly rendered. These inseparables had come together again, and were lingering over a mid-day breakfast. Appetite satisfied, they were 'enjoying the luxury of a morning whiff, and Blandford had been relating his escape from the den run by Niggine, and the incidents which had happened it the rat in- rented sewer. fie had brought his narrative tip to the disappearance of the ruffianly re- ceiver. "Shall I finish? If not boring you, I should like to :elate the whole of the story, for somehow I feel that the time has come when I ought to ask advioe from someone." " Fire away, and be sure that any service I can render is at your disposal." "Old chap, that's a bygone conclusion," said Blandford. "Well the horrors ended when we found ourselves in the rend, but our difficulties remained. How could we walk the streets in our :socks, and escape notice. was a question that neither the Unknown or myself could answer. We stood shivering and "drying our extremities at a blazing coke brasier net before a wooden shelter from which the night watchman was absent. Judg- ing by the observation let fall by Niggins in the sewer I concluded that he wan a con- federate, and we waited patiently for the man's return to duty. Our detention was not for long. he appeared just as our feet were dry, and I bribed him to go and pile- chaseJor us boots, socks, and raps, and thus we made a fairly respectable appearance, and to obtain lodging's was the first proceeding. The next day we replenished our wardrobes and migrated to an hotel close to the Em- bankment, then we settled here. From that date till now, the Unknown and I have shared everything. The inconvenience of poverty has gone, we have plenty if not a plethora of wealth." "And he has not been able during the turns that you have been close companions to recollect his own name?" asked Archer re- flectively,. " No. In he insane?' "Not a hit of it. As sane as yen ono,' swered Blandford. "Too interest see. Please let me have the rest of the story." " The blow on the head has partially de- prived him of memery ; the past in to him a blank, and his name he has completely for. gotten." "hare you committed a medical man?" asked Chinnery, selecting another cigar from a box at his elbow. " Yes, a specialist, who could do nothing, but he held out a hope that chance would be- friend him in the end." "You must introduce me to him," said Chinnery. "With pleasure. He is one of your own sort." Blandford stood in front of the fire warm- ing himself and reflecting. ' You have something more to tvll," said Chinnery. "How do you see that," said the other, smiling. " Because I remember your old habit of pulling down your cuffs when cogitating. Are you mystified?" " Mystified? Yes, that is the correct word. old pal. I am mystified, a great deal ,re no than you can possibly imagine. A complication has arisen. I want to tell you about it." Chinnery carefully lighted his cigar, and looked expectantly at his friend, who went into his bedroom and returned, a dilapidated frock coat in his hand. The garment caused Archer to exclaim aloud: "Great Scott," he cried, " is there in that ragged coat?" " This coat, which I religiously preserve, was the only covering I poeseseed on the night of my adventures. This threadbare coat, I shall bequeath to my heirs, if ever I have any, and tell them ite history. Now listen," said Blandford, etepping to Chin- nerfe side and speaking low: " Four days ago I searched the pockets. °bower° the tail is minus the pocket. Note that?" "I do," said Chinnery, a wry smile on his clean shaven lips. "In my search I felt a hard substance in the extreme corner of the lining, and found this," eaid Blandford, gravely. He pro- duced a faded crimson leather case. "How did it come there?" asked Chinnery, surprised. "I can only guess." "And your guess is--" "That the case was placed where I dis- covered it by Niggins for a purpose of his own." " To implicate you in a theft." "Exactly my own idea. I recall his words, which at the time I took but little heed of." Blandford patted the coat, and Chinnery asked: "What were the words?" "Slelp me, no mother could look arter 'cc precious kid more than I'll look arter yew, Mr. Masher." "Meaning Alec, that he would not losie sight of you or the ease. By the bye, I sup- wee it's not empty," said Chinnery, now brimming over with curiosity. "Look," and Blandford opened the case, disclosing a diamond necklace of unusual brilliancy. " I have a extraordinary feeling that those gems will in some way now hidden dominate my future life." There was a spirit of prophesy in the words which struck Chinnery forcibly, and he took the case to the window the better to ex- amine the gems by the strong rays of the noonday sun. De remained strangely silent and preoccupied, and at last said, below his breath: "By jove, I cannot be mistaken." " You recognise the necklace," demanded Blandford, eagerly. " Unquestionably," replied the other, im- pressively. "I cannot be mistaken." " You could restore them to the owner,' said Blandford in a tone of intense excite- ment. " I could," replied Chinnery with decision "I am a connoisseur of gems, a collector, as you are aware, and am well acrainted with the value of all precious stones. "Where did you see them last?" "Round the neck of a most beautiful woman, perhaps the handsomest woman in the whole of London. It is only she who possesses such lt, unique set of stones. They are simply perfect. See their depth and how blue white they are, see how they send out every colour of the rainbow." And he al. towed the string of glittering baubles to glide through his fingers till they appeared to be a line of fiery life. " And the woman's name?" asked Bland. ford, shuddering unaccountably. " Miss Miriam Trafford." "I do not remember ever hearing the name, and yet it seems strangely familiar to me," said Blandford slowly. "Is the lady you mention in society?" "Of a kind, a very limited kind. Men mostly. She does not move near the sacred circle." " Tell me of her," said Blandford, resting hia chin on the back of his chair. "Miriam Trafford's father is dead," began Chinnery. "There were whispers of a crime and that she was the perpetrator of it, but nothing could be proved. Trafford Khan i made a huge fortune n Persia, and built him- self a palace in Mayfair. A real Persian palace, built and decorated entirely by Per- sian workmen." . Blandford rose from his seat and paced restlessly about the ream while Lid friend- e,-eitloned traria,</p>
        <p>"what mystery</p>
        <p>For Coughs of Children</p>
        <p>Mothers will appreciate the great advantage of ha, fig a cough remedy for their children which is approved by the medical profession, and which can be given to children, not only with perfect safety, but with certainty of benefit. The little ones are peculiarly susceptible to the soothing and tonic influence of Anglers Emulsion, and it must be a very bad cough indeed that will not be quickly relieved by its use. Angier's Emulsion is strongly recommended by the medical profession, not only for colds, coughs, whooping cough, bronchitis mid all lung affections, but also for scrofula. rickets, malnutrition and wasting diseases generally.</p>
