      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ANGIER',.!)muISION</head>
        <p>ANGIER',.!)muISION</p>
        <p>/ 0,. . . •</p>
        <p>Modes at the Thestre.—New Spring styles used to make their first and earliest appearances on the Riviera in general, soul at Monte Carlo in particular, and for these places the celebrated houses put forth all their energy in producing noveltiee—not always in the bent of taste—in millinery and dressmaking, but nowoi-days ournew styles appear at the theatre, end drat...kers , and their clienteles take their ideas from the exquisite gowns nd millinery that appear oi, the stages of our best houses. In connection with too All.ltritish move- ment, the dresses at one of the lambs theatres have been made "an far a. possible " of fabrics of British manufacture—and the result is certainly a triumph for honso-rnade goeds, a fount of inspiration in the matter of charming simple elegance and a liberal education in the matter of the coming ftulions. Aboot Millinery.—The new straw. are almolutely bewildering In variety and novelty, and so cleverly and cunningly are some of them made, that they might easily Ire mistaken for velvet. Others have knittsd effects, suggested doubtless by the popular knitted caps and suits worn so couch this season at the winter sports resort. Indeed, much of the art.ifizial plumage made of wool and straw has also a knitted effect. The rose will meet certainly be the flower of the Coronation seamen. The march hats are laden with the King Edwerd, Dorothy Perkins, and Hiawatha varieties ; sweet pees are popular, too, in rich purple, deep crimson, and all the delicate pink and lavender shades that distinguish this lovely flower. The toque of white sweet peas, with clipped ostrich feather brim, will meet with many admirern, despite the brilliant hues that will probably be put on the shade. This shape pressed down on the head will be seen throughout the spring, and the huge bat will make its appearance when the rude winds of March and April have departed. A very pretty Hungarian cap is made entirely of riblmn, and has loops arranged in the form of homer plumes, with which alone it is decorated. Very becoming are the theatre caps made of gold or silver net, notes a band of roses. New Footgestr.—Our footgear is of great importance sines the short skirt is to remain wan on. The latest style is the shoe In velvet to match the colour of the dreae. These shoes are ornamented with steel, silver or gold buckle,. (taco? the latest novelties in shoos for evening wear are ttose of brocade to match the gowns. Tnese are so well cut that they are most elegant in appearance. Some evening shoes again have tiny bouquets of rosebud., or other dowers worked in narrow picot ribbons, with a tiny gem in the heart of each. Canadian mocoaslns ore muoh treed for bedroom wear, carried oat in pastel-tinted kid or leather embroidered with head epray, and trimmed .with fur. A Luncheon Beverage.—The medical profession generally deprecates the practice of eating between meals, but it very often happens that some midway nourishment is required and for this purpose llorlick's Malted Milk is to be recommended. It is refreshing and austainieg. Poser...ins all the nutritive value of full-cream milk enriched with tieur extracts of the best eelected cereals, it is a complete food, especially valuable for the young, and for people for whom an easily assimilated diet Is necessary. Moreover, it has the great advantage of being easily mewed, in- couvenient for travellers, sod for use in the house it all times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SECRET OF BEAUTIFUL HAIR.</head>
        <p>THE SECRET OF BEAUTIFUL HAIR.</p>
        <p>NO matter how carded you may be of your heir— how often or how faithfully you may shampoo or brush it—the hair cannot attain the thick glossy brilliance nature intended for all men, women and children so longue the dandruff germ is sapping the strength and vitality of the hair roots. Frequent brushing of the hair and a shampoo once every two or three weeks are both valuable aids to gaining hair beauty, but they cannot destroy the dandruff germ. So far as known the only way to destroy this germ is to rub into the scalp twice a day a mixture prepared by mixing 3ot. Bay Rum with dram Menthol Crystals and, lox. Lavona de Composes. As this preparation not only destroys the dandruff germ but is also a truly marvellous hair-grower, it should be applied only where hair is desired. Any chemist can prepare the above formula or you can procure the ingredients and mix them at home; but you must use it regularly; and be sure to brush jour hair frequently and give it an occasional shampoo. Avoid the frequent use of hot curling-tongs, as they tPnil to make the bah dull and lifeless.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>COST JF EDUCATION IN</head>
        <p>COST JF EDUCATION IN</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Education Committee met at Preston on Monday and adopted the annual estimates, details of whit% ware published on Wednesday. The Chairman, Sir Fleury Ilibbert, proposed the adoption of the report of the higher Education Com- mittee. Ile raid it was estimated that for the forth- coming year the amount to be expended on higher education would have to be increased from £113,550 to L'126,100, and the amount to be met by the ratss would have to be increased from £10,600 to £90,15:50. During the year two new secondary schools would have to be erected at Eccles' and Middleton, and provision for them had to he made. There were decreases on the items of scholarships and exhibitions of £1,500, oath. training of teachers of ,£l,OOO, and on financial assist' aoce to endowed grammar actiools of £1,000; but there was an increase in the eatimatee for capitation greats of 4.:,00. Alderman Warle-Descos referred to estimated receipts of £31,000 from the Customs and Excise. lie maid that owing to the alteration in the Budget last year they only received £23,000, inatead of the estimated £31,000, which they bad received previously, and unions the Chancellor of the Exchequer introdimed certain dropped clauses in last year's Finance Act in the new Budget they would only receive the £2.3,000. They had thought it wise to estimate for rho full amount, but it was an uncertain quantity, and be felt that it was necessary to warn the Committee that unless they received the full amount they would have to wipe out an satin .£6,000 annually by the rates. Sir Henry said it wan extremely probe' 'e that they would receive what he might describe • back pay,' seeing that the Chancellor of the .equer had promised that it should be done. Moving the report of the Elementary Education Sub- committee, Mr. J. J. Cockshott (Southport) mid that the sum to be expended on elementary oduoation during the year won £527.000, with which they hoped to edocate some 136,50Urshildren. They hoped to receive £277,590 by means of Government grants, and £1,956 from local authorities and parents, leaving .C 211,440 to be levied by the rates. This was an increase of £l,OOO, partly due to the fact that teachers' salaries had incressed by £2,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>U)</p>
        <p>r3fr</p>
        <p>"Those ft i7-I.l:,alegect persohs who were permitted to cross the threshold of Trafford'e house were astounded at its vast magnifi- cence. The halls, the corridors, and suites of apartments were dreams of oriental splen- dour." Blandford stopped short and stared aghast at his friend. " Such a place must have been the talk of London," he managed to say. "It was but little known, for the outside resembles an ordinary English mansion. The interior, however, is beyond description." "Then there was no delusion?" muttered Blandford. " What was no delusion?" asked Chinnery. "The description given by the Unknown, when lying on my bed in the wretched garret in Seven Dials." Iris face showed to his friend how the misery or that period was impressed oil his memory. "'tell me about it," said Chinnerv. "No, no, not now," said Illandord, ner- vously. "But can you give me no further information," ho asked. " Not much beyond this, that Trafford Khan brought his workpeople from Persia in a steamer chartered for the pi gpose, and when the decorations of the intetior were completed he deported them back in the same way to their own country. The exterior was finished by Iluntly and Company, the well- known contractors. ' "From whom did you derive your informa- tion?" "From the sweetest of sweet girls, Aida Morant, the most darling girl in the world." "In love?" said Blandford, who wag aware of his friend's failing of falling in and out of love a dozen times in a year. "Well, yes, and no." "Is Miss Morant a relative of Trafford Khan?" A ward, I believe. But like every per- son in that extraordinary household, around this pretty child there bangs a mystery." " A mystery that I'll net myself to solve. My unknown friend is mixed up in the do- ings of these foreign acquaintances of yours. To him I owe an.eternal debt of gratitude; for he van my salvation. From the depths, through him, I have one more floated to the surface, and to repay his generosity I am de- termined to see him righted, whatever his wrongs may be." Observing Chinnery's evi- dent surprise at this outburst, he concluded somewhat lamely, "Archer, I am no longer is backslider, but a man who has profited by a bitter experience; a waster no more, believe</p>
        <p>me"' There was a silence, labial Blandford broke. " \Vete you a frequent guest of the late Khan?" " Not frequent, an occasional guest," Chin- nery answered. "Our acquaintance began in 'Teheran, but I had not been to Trafford House for over two years when I went to the Khan's funeral a short time ago." " Had you any particular reason for that?" "Miriam Trafford had a strange effect on me. I cannot give any excuse for the sensa- tion, for her voice was mellow and musical, voice that once h urcl would for ever linger ir the mind. Her eyes were magnetic, fas- cinating, enchanting, what you will, and it is easy to comprehend how a glorious crea- ture would cajole our sex to their ruin. But her witchery only bewilders me, repels me. and tills me with fear." His voice dwindled into a whisper as the door of the room opened quickly, and Chinnery 'sanded to his feet, exclaiming in amazement, "Why, as I live, it's Michael Lawson!" (To be Continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WOMEN'S CIIIT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CIIIT.</p>
        <p>'This Fell Sergeant, Death."—The death of Lady Suffield will be keenly felt by members of the Royal Family. She was married to Lord Suffield in 1951, and was one ot the oldest and most intimate friends of Oiseen Alexandra. It is nearly eight and forty years since the youthful Danish Princess came to this country, and won all hearts by her grace, charm, beauty and winning sweetnese. Of the eight bridesmaids who attended her Majesty on her wedding on March 10th, 1863, several are atilt living, but many of the friends of her early married life have passed away. The forth- coming enniversary of her wedding day will be the first since the overwhelming loss which fell upon her, and the nation, las'. May, and it was not given to King Edward to celebrate that rare event in Royal as well no in ordinary lives—a golden wedding. liar Majesty's silver wedding in 1888 was overshadowed by the death of the old German Emperor the day before and the departure of the then Prince of Wales, with his elder eon, Albert Victor, to attend the funeral at Berlin. The National Emblem of Wales—Queen Alexandra, when Princess of Wales, wore a leek at the Drawing Room which took plume° St. Devid's Day (March let I 1311882, bat it is held by many Welshmen that the real emblem of Wales is not the leek hut the daffodil " Lily of Daffyd." This apparently has the sanction of the Carnarron Board of Education, in anthorising the children to wear daffodils instead of leeks upon St. David's Day. The two plants are botanically akin, and it is, probably bennea,, the daffodil seldom blossoms so early as St. David's Day, whilst the wild leek grows profusely around the coed of South Wales that the one usurped the place of the other as the national emblem of the Principality. Old-Age Marriages.—There is a tendency in theme times to attach age-limits to various conditions of life. It has various applications from the mechanic who ; s declared to be " too old at forty," to the clergyman, who it is now [suggested is " too old at seventy," but there is in this country, no age-limit for marriage. Shakespeare rays" When the age is in, the salt in out" but whether it is due to that, or to the advent of old- age pension., there seems to have been something like an epidemic of Septuagenarian and octogenarian wedding. lately. The one recorded last week in which the bridegroom was 01, and the bride 80, might have been supposed to be a record for, late marriage, but Sin far from enjoying that distinction. in the parish register of Greenwich, there is mention of the marriage of " John Cooper of this parish, aged 108, to Margaret Thomas, of Charlton, Kent, aged 80 " and if a search were made, it is possible that marriage records would be found in which the joint ages exceeded 188. Suffragists and the Ceesus.—Desnite the fact that e Women's; Suffrage Bill has secured find place for die- amnion in the House of Commons after Rester, the militant suffragist+, announce their intention to per- severe with the census boycott. Women householders, and lodgera, all over the country, will be asked to refuse to fill op the reams paper, on the ground that they are denied their oitizenship. Any ocoupier guilty of ench default is liable to a fine of .65, but apart from that, the cannon is a matter that concerns the welfare of the people, inasmuch as it is the basis of numerons enquiries concerning social reform, and especially the conditions of employment of women and girls. There is consequently no [sense in this appear to them to act against their own intereets. There are, according to Mr. Churchill, five million male adults who have no vote, and it would be just as illogical for them to adopt this suggeeted defiance of the law. Mise Colvin First Play—Mine Marie Corelli has written a short play in which she is understood to severely criticise the "Votes for memos" moremeet. The piece is called "The Sedan Chair," and ie to be produced on the London stage next month. The authoress done not introduce politics, but her views on militant cuff 'seism have long been known to the public and are likely to find expression in the piece, M sparkling witticisms and striking epigrams. Although her career is no nearly related to the dramatists art, it is the first time that Miss Marie Curelli has written for ' the stage and it is her intention to personally super- intend the rehearsale of her sketch.</p>
      </div>
