        <p>LYSIM URBAN COUNCIL. THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Lymin District Connell was held in the Council Rooms, Lymm, on Thursday evening. Nlr.o o.3mith providerl.and there wore al. preseont,Nlesars. W.G.Grnires,F.Lookyer,T. 11. Warburton S. Daviev, the Rev. I. 11. Thurston. Dr. de Jong, (medical ~thcer Mr. Donaldson (gas manager, and Mr. Mort (surveyor).</p>
        <p>-o'HOICAL 01,/, 61I'M REPORT. A LOW DIRATO a %LE.</p>
        <p>Thu Medical Officer of Health (Dr. E. M. De Joao) prevented his annual report in which he stated that the house accommodation throughout the district was sufficient and tit for habitation. The open space around the houses mu ample, end the surroundings of the dwellings were elem. As regarded births, 107 were registered in 1910-61 melee and 46 females. l)1 these, five (three mans and two females) were illegitimate. One of theee died during the year. In 191,9 there were 12:, births, end the avenge for the previom ten years was 117. They had a birth-rate of 21-4 per thousand living, am against 23-1 I, 1909, and 23.9 for the previous ten man MP.. The ' births for 1910 were considerably below the average in 1909. In 1910 there were :a deaths, four of which were i reaitlents dying in public institutions outside the district. This gives a net death-rate of 11.6 per thousand living Se against 13-1 for 1909. That was the lowest recorded tenth-rate for ten years, even lower than that for 1909, itself the lowest previously recorded. The average death-rate in the district forth, last ten year was 131. There were four inquests held during the year. The modification of the law with rmpect to compulsory vaccination wee ,till having an effect upon the voccina- tion in this district. The number of •accinations was lessening year Ity year. Tee water supply was in the hands of a private company, and was generally laid on throughout the diet-tot where posaihle. Analysis gives the result of a good potable water. With the usual tables, Dr. De Jong concludes, is the report of your Sasitary Inspector, Mr. David Mort, whom I should like to thank for his willing help and co-operation in all matters ii, which his services have been re ,uired. I should here also like to express my thanks to the nurses who hod charge of the came of diphtheria in the hospital during the epidemic for their untiring work and Mill. Some of those cases were very ill indeed and all of them recovered. I Mould also like to thank Nurse Nash, our district norm, whose promptnms in finding out and calling attention to auspicious cs..xes dit I much to check the spread of the epidemic indeed, tor many years now, her quiet end unpretentious work in the district as a teacher of personal and 'domestic hygiene, of ripht infant feeding, am-,l her early recogoi- gip, of jerectioue cases have heen of incalculable value from a sanitary point of view. The Rev. G. It. Thurstoe moved the adoption of the report, and in doing so he thanked the doctor for the manner in which he took care of the health of the district. They were very much indebted to him. . Hem, hear.) lie moved that the report he adopted. Mr. Daviesmcondaland the motion was unanimously •greed to. _</p>
        <p>CORONATION MIERRNTORs. 4; I rrs.Ry GEOROIN AND MART,.</p>
        <p>The Rev. U. B. 'I hurston said he would like to know whether it would be possible if the girls and boys with the names of Mary and George could pay their contributions to the King and Queen's gift at the Council &amp;lice. There ought to be some means, he said, by which they might be able to contribute. It had been done in several other places, he believed. It was a nice thought and he was •ure that all those boya and girls who were fortunate in possessing those two names would like to make their contribution. Mr. Groves : The money that we use will have to come out of the Coronation festivities fund. The Local Government Board have issued an order that they will sanction a certain amount on the rate for this purpose. Mr, Thurston : I move that the time contributionn be made between next Monday and Saturday at the Council oilers during office hours. The Chairman So short it time as that ? I should give them longer. Mr. Thor•ton : Well, you are the anbacriptions have to he sent in coon. Say before March 23th then. The Chairman I most say that it is a very short Lim!.</p>
        <p>-31, Thurston pointed out that it did not prevent the children reeling it some other way. He would have opposed the resolution, he added, deciding that a fund should be got up in Lymm for the Colliery disaater. if he had been at the Council Meeting.lt was no use send- ing shout .C 5 when, if they had op. mid a fund earlier, they could have sent £5O. !Hear, heriLl Eventually on the proposal of the Rev.O.B.Thurston,it W. decided to open • fund which should canto on March 25th, and that all contributions could be made at the Council Offices during olfice hours. It one also decided that a notice should be inserted in the Warrington papers advertffing the fact.</p>
