      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CONFECTIONER " SCORES."</p>
        <p>------- At the Runcorn Sessions, on filondav,Joeepla Hender- son, Regent-street, was summoned for the tenth time for following his calling of acentectioner on the Lord'. Day--Hafendant, for the lint time, raised the point that he was not only a confectioner, buten estate agent, having eighty houies to look after, lie pointed out that there were at present .0•PrIll appeal Caset pending, in which persons had two occupationa, rho question tieing whether they followed their " ordinary calling " on Stiodaya.—The magistrates adjourned the case sine die.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN EDUCATIONISTS' BREEZE. .DROP THE PEDAGOGUE."</p>
        <p>A lively diecuseion took place st the Runcorn Library Committeee meeting over the question of the estab- lishment of school libreries. The committee some time ago peened a resolution in favour of school libraries, hut the Urban Council rejected the proposal. Mr. F. J. Moulton DO suggested that a letter which had appeared in the Press over the signature of the 'chairman, Mr. F W. Hutton, woe misleading, end Mr. Hutton must have known it.</p>
        <p>The ( hairman : Pardon me. Will you kindly recollect that you ore not addressing your schoolboys'? Mr. Moulton : I know you are not a schoolboy. If you ware, I should be inolined to gite you a whipping. Mr. Annett This is for too personal. The Chairman M, Moulton, you addressed me In • tone that called for toy remark. Mr. Moulton You think so? I don't. If you make a misleading statement, I hare reasonable grounds for complaint. The Chairman : Certainly ; but remembor you are addreosing grown-op people. Later Mr. Pope asked Mr. Moulton to remember ho Ass not in school, nod" drop the pedagogue." In reply to this Mr. Moulton later reminded Mr. Pope sod the chairman that they were not in the works with their workmen.</p>
        <p>Mr. Annett oneof the members, protested &amp;gains t the personalities, end left the room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>BURIAL GRIEVANCE Al HAYDOCK. SEQUEL TO PARISH DIVISION.</p>
        <p>At the reneging of the Haydock Dietrict Council. Councillor Lloyd voiced a grievance which has sprung tip in regard to burial freer for interments in the hay. nook Parish Churchyard and moved the appointment of a Bub-committee to consider the question of providing a cemetery for the district. Ile stated that the reason for his bringing the noitter forward was that the Rev. F. Ireland, vicar of St. Janrea's,beil been charging double burial fees to Ilaydock reeidents who happened to live outside a certain irnaginery line drawn across the centre of the township. They knew that the Church of England authoritiee had divided the district into two parishes, and it seamed that the people who li•wl in the new parish of St. tlark's were to be compelled to pay double the usual fees for the privilege of burying their deed in graves which they had previously purchased in the old church- yard. The council probably had no voice in liking the mount to be charged for burials, hut they could provide a cemetery and make provision for the people who wiabed to bury in the district where many of them had spent the whole of their live., without allowing anyone to compel them to pay an extra half-guines for the privilege. The population of St Mark's nes about 4,6451 ; practically belt of the whole distric•a Fie did not know what proportion wes connected with St. Mark's Church, but there were at least three other churches in the district, in addition to the congrega- ' tion of St. Story'', Catholic Cbirch, Black brook. He understood that the churchyard had been given as a burial ground for th• people of Ilaydock, and he thought the present arrangements were against the wishes of the donor. fie believed that regal opinion were that the Vicar of Sc. Jamerie had no right to treat the inhabitants of the new parish of St. Mark'e all non. parishioners and that they could not be compelled to pay double fees. Nlr. J. Robinson (churchwarden of St. Mark's expressed the hope that an amicable arrangement might be come to with Mr. Ireland, and env/wed that the matter he referred to the Highways Committee for consideretion snd report. Mn. Lloyd agreed, and this coupue was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NAVAL OFFICERS FATAL HEROISM.</head>
        <p>NAVAL OFFICERS FATAL HEROISM.</p>
        <p>—••—•— •• Lt.-Commander 'tarold F. Alderten. of torpedo- boat 115, was drowned at Sheerness on Wednesday, in trying to rescue one of his crew, who hail fallen into the rough sea near the entreat, to Stangate Creek. The lieutenant lowered himself with a in, but slipped, and Was carried away by the ware,, The Waraatl was rescued into eaLattsted state.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. 118 LENS CATHOLIC CHARITY BALL</p>
        <p>The seventy-fourth annual Catholic charity ball wee hold on Wednuday night at the St. Helens Town Hell in aid of the Catholic @chords of the tovrn and dietrict. The hall wu most beautifully deonratel, and the whole of the rooms and corridors rendered we comfortable as possible. A distinguished company mumbled, the event being under the patronage of .11 the local gentry,' the patronesaes being the Mayorese (NH. Beecham), ' the Countess of Derby, Lady Gerard and lady Mary Gerard ; chairman of committee, Nfr. G. Stringfellow ; treasurer, NI r.W, Woodcock : hon. secretsr7, Mr..loseph Sephtoo ; end Meiners. A. Williams, K. M Lachlan and C. J. Middlehtiret M.C.'r. Mr. W. Dennettli orchestra provided the musio arid the function was most aocceseful. ALLEGED GAMBLING HOUSE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helemon Slooday,Chief Constable Ellerington said that there were on the list fifteen charges of gaming, which it would he necemary to have postponed, , because one of the polio. witnesses was not able to attend Court. Ho Wen very ill, and it might be • con- enterable time before he was better. The CU. Were against the keeper of a chip potato shop named Peter Welch, nod his wife, Elialwith, of I, Dyson-street, Pesieley-cross, who were charged with seine their house and permitting it to be used for the purpom of gaining; and several young men Were charged with resorting to Welsh's shop to gamble. Mr. E. W. Swift, who appeared foe the defence, said that ',under the circiim- PAM., be could not oppose the Alj.surninent. The beach adjourned the cases sine die. FUNERAL OF A ST. HELENS WORTHY.</p>
        <p>The funeral of Mr. Nathan Ellison, of Brook House. Sutton, who years ago was e prowl., lit figure in Liver- pool, took place at Farnworth Churchyard on Monday Mr. Ellison for many year, wan contractor to the Liver- pool Corporation, providing all the carting and horses for the Sanitary Depertment, and carrying out all the haulage work of the city. Hr was especially skilled in the management and breeding of horses, and it is no doubt due in • great measure to hint that Liverpool attaineJ, and ha. kept, the reputation of employing the moet magnificent specimens of horeetineh of any city in the country. Mr. Ellison lived to the ripe age of ninety-ten, and died on Thursday at Brook Ifoase where he had resided for shout thirty-live years. The mountern at the funeral were Mr. Harold Benson 'grandeon), West Derby ; Mr. John Garton r grandson, and Mr. H. Sainsbury, of Faxakerley ; Mr. John Lowe. Essex Colonel J. W. hlll'ear, J.P., and Mr. .7. B. Leach J P., Huyton ; Mr. Thomas Denning, London ; Mr. W. I). Laird and Mr. N. Laird, Crooner ; and Mr. W. Haaelden.</p>
        <p>TREASURER CHARGED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ST. HELENS BURIAL SOCIETY SENSATION.</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS BURIAL SOCIETY SENSATION.</p>
        <p>As foreahadowed at the Brat bearing some curious revelations regarding the management of a St- Helens friendly society were made when Thomas Eilen,lioensee of th• Bear's Paw Hotel, Westfield-street, was charged on remand on Triesil•y with stealing, as bailee, .6320 ISa. 4d., the money of the British 1411een Burial Satiety, of which he wan treasurer. Mr. Lindon Biley, who prosecuted on behalf of the : truetees, stated that the society Yves unregigerod, and prisoner had been treasurer since Ma. On two occa- sions prisoner was authoris•.l to invest soma of £lOO with the St. Helens Corporation, but failed to do no. I On December 31, 1909, there appeared to be £lO4 I6s. 2d., in Parrs Bank to the credit of the society, but really there was only 16a. 2d. In 1903 prisoner mortgaged to the society come property he bad and they lent him £3OO, elthough he only paid .6290. Sin years later, when he was in a howun belonging to Messrs. Oreenall, Whitley and Co., be gave the firm a second mortgage on the property, informing them that th• Bust mortgage was for £lOO. When atispichn was aroused, he told the Burial So.iiety that the deeds of the property bad been burned, but when inquiries began to he ntsde by a solicitor it was discovered that the title desd• were in the passeasion of Men.. tireenall, e9hitley and and that they had come into their posalAsion as s result of prisoner's false pretence,.. . . .</p>
        <p>r.The-WOOks throughout had been ann.lly submitted and audited, and Mr. Riley suggested that it wan proof of the confidence which the society had in the prisoner that ale manipulatiocs in the accounts had not been di.coyered.</p>
        <p>onor pleaded guilty to two chargos and not guilty tn th ft other", sod was committed for trial at Manchester Assi,os.</p>
        <p>THE SIM' EN. E. Thomas Eden, a St. Helens man, was sentenced to three years imprisonment at the Nlancbester Assizes on Wednesday for +series of frauds on the British Quoen Friendly Burial Society, of which society he was treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL TREATMENT OF PIMPLES AND BLACKHEADS.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL TREATMENT OF PIMPLES AND BLACKHEADS.</p>
        <p>A speedy and economical treatment for disfiguring pimples is the following Gently smear the face with Cuticura ointment, but do not rub. Waeh off the ointment in five minutes with Cuticura soap and hot water and bathe freely for some ininutee. Repeat morning and evening. At other tlmes use hot water and Cuticura soap for bathing the face as often as agreeable. Citticitra soap and ointment are equally eucceeefid for itching, burning, rcaly and crested humours of the skin and scalp, with loss of hair, from , infancy to age' usually affording instant relief when I all else fails. All shout the economical care of com- plexion, hands and hair is given in the latest Cuticura hook. pent post-free, by Potter Drug A, Chem. Corp. 27, Charterhouse Sq.. London. E C.</p>
