      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WARRINGTON ESPERANTO SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ESPERANTO SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The shove society held their second social on Monday last at the society's headquarters in Bewsey• street. Amongst those present were Mr A Griffin (president St. Ilelens Society), Mr Ft West f Awn, tary of the Esperanto Federation of I,lol=llllre and Choslii re , Mr Graham (secretary Liverpool Society), and Mr Jackson f secretary Chester Society). There were other Esperantists present from Widnes, Liver- pool. Chesmr, and St. Helens. besides the officials and members of the Warrington Society and many of their friends.—A whist drive first held, the primes belag awarded as follows :—Ladies : I, Miss Magreth ; '2,Miss Dimmitile. Gentlemen : I, Mr West :2, Mr Lavery. The Esperanto hymn " La Espero " was sung by all tht Espemntists. . . .</p>
        <p>The 'present Presiden• of the Warrington Society (Rev. It. It. Erimareei) in welcoming the guests from other towns mid that their erceence wee both plea mint end helpful. Their fluent speeches in Esperanto would encourage our local Ever/midst, to emulate their pro- ficiency. he sociability and kindly greeting of Eeperantists bore witness to the spirit which vitalised Esperanto namely Eeperantiern, a factor that aims at making the whole world kin. (Applause,. The Vice-President (Mr. Cathro) epoke of the facili- ties afforded to local people. There wile a club in full working order, a weekly class every Tuesday with head- quarters in Bewsey.street, a moet competent instructor besides which the ariecial system of tuition enabled any one to start at any time. %lies Smith, a local Esperantist, on behalf of the Warrington ladies, welcomed the guests and showed how the world language, while simple even for persons whose educarion had been scant,set offered&amp; delightful field for study to anyone who possessed any linguistic or philological tendencies. Mr. Griffin, St. Helens, congratulated Warrington upon the great progress they had marle,and said that he Ind visions of Warrington becoming in the very near future the centre of the Esperanto movement in lancashire.</p>
        <p>Mr. 1.3. West spoke wernilvof the success achieved and hoped that other Lsocavhire and Cheviiire towns would vie with Warrington in establishing • club and , joi.n. the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Cathro, E. G. Burgess (hon. secretary Warrington Esperanto Society),R. to. and J.Enonett scted ILI M</p>
        <p>lAn excellent • programme wee enjoye.l by the corn. pony end was as follows :—Character song by boys and girls trained by MI. A. Kilburn , "The miller and the maid ". in Esperanto) harvest scene, chottnee dances, by boys and girl. (trained by Miss 1.. Caldwell), , Esperanto 'erase by Monter W. Duffey f aged 71, and IMentes H. Teri. loge 8); songs, " Two eyo of grey " end" Violeta " by Mille Griffin (St. Helens) ; " When I the heart ii young," by Miss A. Bradshaw ; " Down the vale," Mina 'A. &amp;kers!. " Beautiful garden of , roses," Miss J. Corney " and "Who's don a-calling," by . Min F. Flood. All the I.oov, were flung in Seperanto. The programme also Included dvisces which were engaged in between the various songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Independent Order of Reehabhes.</head>
        <p>Independent Order of Reehabhes.</p>
        <p>_ he members of Fiiions Pride Tent, along with the members of the recently formed Latchford branch of the same Tent, met for their annual social on Monday evening last at the Wesleyan school, Bold-street. About sixty members sat down to tea after which Bro. Sydney Phillips presided over an exceedingly inter- esting programme arranged by Bin W. M. Carruthers consisting of songs and recitation, Bro. Ernest Hannan very able acted as accompanist. Daring the evening 13ro. J. .R. Barker (Wigan), D.A.S.J.T. pre- sented book prizes to the following members: Bros. I Norman Fernley, Sydney Phillips, Herbert Dyson and Sister Esther Permington.Other prizes were also given for regular attendance. The programme concluded with gramaphons selections by Bro. David Smith, an old member of the Tent at present on a viait to this country from Canada.</p>
        <p>At the inquest at Liverpool on John SW•130e11. • tanner, vrho slipped and fell into a pit of boiling liquid at the Garstr n tannery. doctor declared the scalds to be the worst be hod ever seen.</p>
