      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PROTECTING THE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>PROTECTING THE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The Work of the NSPCC In</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>The National Society for the Prewentiru of Cruelty to Children is doing an excellent, and unbrtunately. much iiceded work in Warrington and district. as the amnia' report for the year ended March 31st, 1910 shorn, We give the ill wring extracts : —The Com- mittee of the Warrington and District Branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children have to report for the above period the cases investigated have been as follows :—Total number of complaints, 23'.1 ; children affected OA ; offenders involved, 347. The following is the classifi- cation of complaints --Neglect awl !starvation, 225 ; ill-treatment and assault. eight ; abandonment, two: causing to beg, three ; immoral surroundings. one ; 239. rfhe action in them being : —Warned, 225; prosecuted (and convicted: 11 ; otherwise dealt with three. The penalties inflicted in the prosecuted cases were two years two months, and £3 Os. Ct. in fines. Children known to be insured, 335 ; supervision visits, 1,550. During the year nine children have died where the cases were under investigation. The sources from which complaints came during the year trill be interesting, as showing the confid- ence reposed in the society by the general publi,. Reported by the General Public ... 149 Police 22 „ School Officials . „ Other Officials... ... 42 DiscOvered by the Society's Inspector 26</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Total</head>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>Since the last tn.eting new offices have been obtained at 1:!, Froghall-lane, Warrington, where a very cotntncvlions house has been taken at a very reasonable rental.</p>
        <p>Inspector. Wainstvright, 'rho has taken the place of Inspector Cox, ha, proved himself an efficient and capable officer, and the Committee are very pleased with his work.</p>
        <p>Mra. W. W. Dimmelow has kindly consented to at se jOint Hon. Secretary with Mr. Jesse. and io devot- ing herself very enthusiastically to details of organisa- tion. The Committee feel confident that her services will have most beneficial result to the Branch.</p>
        <p>Thanks to the splendidly loyal help of the ladies of the several committees, who year after year collect funds in order that the society's work may go on unfettered, the year expenses have been enecessfully met, and there is a small balance available for the national work. True, the expenses have been excep- tionally low, but there has been% small increase from the dietrict cominirteee of the Brooch, and thir it is hoped will be a feature of each succeeding balance sheet. It must always be remembered that the Branch has had substantial help from the National Fund, and anything we can do to help it will lie most welcome. Thanks are most heartily given to all the lady collectors for their kind help, and to the Secretary and members of the League of Pity. The Branch is also sensible of the debt it owes to those who so willingly undertook and tarried through the arrangements for the several social functions that were hell during the year for the society's benefit. For the appreciative I contribution of the Board of Guardians, the Branch would express sincere thanks. The co,peration of the Press, Police, and the many public officials in the Branch area is greatly valued. Their help in the past ' year has been of much as,istance. The officials of the Branch are —Presidetit, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart ; hon. treasurer, Mrs. F. Aylmer Frost, Grappetiliall Hall ; general boo, secretary, Mrs. W. W. Dinamelow, Caronido, Latchford ; cases hon. secretary, Mr. W. D. Jeans. jun., 17, Bold-strcet. Children's League of Pity, hon. secretary, Mies Broadbent, The Hollies, Lstchfurd, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell and the Hector.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the British Wornen's Temperance Association a meeting was held an the Thewlie-street School. on Thursday evening. Mr. T. Dale presided and was supported on the platform by the Rev. A. M. Mitchell and Mrs. Simpson. There was a good attendance. The C11111(31, remarked that the fact that of 10 Northern towns Warrington was second in the lint for drunkenness was a sad reflection upon the citixens of Warrinwon. They were told in the recent Licensing report that there were lee licensed houses in the borough and that gave us no average of one bilis° to every 331 inhabitants. Ile would say that there were more than half of the peeple who never used a public house, so they would have to deduct , half of the 331 sod say that there was one public ! house to every 11;7 inhabitants. Regarding the had state of drunkenness in the town the Chairman thought that those who were reeponsible for it would have called together all the members of" The Trade S' and considered whether they could not abate this terrible disaster—for it was nothing less. But they brew that they would not. (Applause.) The Rev. A. M. SI uon u., at the outset, alluded to the work that Miss Simpson was doing on the Board of Guardiana and rematked that if there were more 11.W.T.A. women along with her they would soon have a revolution and the little difficulties which they encouutered there would soon be swept awsy. The rev, gentleman mentioned the article which he wrote some ”are ago regarding Warrington and which was descriled as " libel on the town." But in calmer moments during the latter half of last year—they came to the state of penitence and acknowledged that what he said was right and that the town was or had been similar to his demeription of it. Warrington was under the obligation of redeeming its character. hut he was afraid it wee going to take its time over it. He did not think it meant to hurry up over it Whatever might be going on in the may of improving the environment and chocking infantile mortality, there did not seem to be any abatement as regarded drimkennens. Warrington had not risen to the better things it promised to do and,therefore, it was keeping up its reputatation for insobriety. It was not a were nice tbing for Warrington to have the reputation of being second among ten Northern towns in drunken- ness. Were they taking tou much of Warrington water in Buttonwood? (Laughter.) He did not find it necessary to dilute Warrington water with any alcohol to make it consumable. (Laughter) He saw that the kind and gentle Bert ,r had been saying some- thing about the conditio. of Warrington through drunkenness and that he hal advocated the closio,g of more public-housee and drinking only at meals. With regard to the former he wan in entire agreement, except he would like them all closed; but with regard to the latter he did not agree with him. becaitne drinking at meals was bad for the digestion. But what the Sector had said was encouraging and if they could only persuade the Rector to collie out as a !thoroughgoing temperance reformer they would all be twilling to carry him shoulder high through the town. (Laughter awl applause. But he did wish that the Rector would put on a le Fier face in the matter of temperance reform than he did and he hoped that through what he (the Rector) had sail he would not lose anything in the way of subscriptions -(laughter)— because he knew that certain people who lived in the country—might not like it. (Laughter) He thought the Rector must have greatly advanced in his views by advocating the closing of more public-houses and he was glad he had the courage to make the sag. gestiun. By all means let them have some of the public-hr us. cicaed , but he would have them all swept away—and he would begin at the Church gates. (Applause.) Other placed were reducing their public-houses and he wanted to know why Warrington [should be behind haud.There was no better field for the temperance reformer than the brewers' stronghold awl he sail "Pot your foot down." (Applause./ However, he believed that the grip of the driuk traffic was being loosened. (A pplausei. SIIINON in proposing a vote of thanks to the Rev. A. M. Mitcheli arid the Chairman said that during the last few months there had been an increase in the inemberehip of the association of 300 members. (Applause). During the evening Miss Delaney and Miss Wilkie.il contributed some excellent song..</p>
