        <p>HOLMAN KIN(:DON:</p>
        <p>The Coming Slavery. quite' agree Arial the appropriateness of the phrase with' 'Winch you headed Mr. Chesterton's recent.lectarer•pn "The Coming Slavery." " More kicks than,:castots "very succinctly sums up the potion of diel.iiorkingelass, under recent Socialistic legislatioiq gee very little gain to wcrkingmen by ant' of AO realest Acts which have been passed for their eapeclid tenefit. Take the Workman's Corn. p.m./loft lor Injury Act : Alter a man has ohaaingd. eompeneation under it he finds it very difficiodko.find further employment, and the fear of 'I, compensation bas rendered employers very careful about employing aged or posaibly deficient workmen. Take.the Oki Age Pension Act . What good is the Qld Age Pension to a Mall thrown on the w.,rld at 60 jlears of age, and for,oth what good is it to a person salmis in receipt of in. lid, from the parish, and who • .is . being otherwise helped by friends? As soon as *hat person reaches the age limit entitling him to the ' penstoo the friends cease tocontribute, and that person Ilain worse cage than if there were no pennion. What advantage is a Labour Exchange to the working- nuts ? It may benefit the favoured few, hut how many will there be who, owing to some Air, they may have made, will have a difficulty in finding anew employer when a'l their past mistakea may be recorded in a Lthogr Exchange '? My advice to workingmen is to be wise in time snd take care of their bard-earned wages ; be frugal and sober and baud yourseltes together, and instead of quarrelling so much with your employers and among yourselves try to meet all in a spirit of somprotnise. Form industrial end other self-help societies among yourselves and eschew the political agitator and demogogte from whatever point of the political compass he may come, for lie only exploits you for his own' immediate purpoaes.---1 am, Ac. PHIL SQL:01).</p>
