      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Rector and the Doctrines of Rome</head>
        <p>The Rector and the Doctrines of Rome</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of February ISth, the Rector (Rev. Canon (Villis) is reported to have said at the annual couvemazione in connection with the Warring- ton Church S.ciety :—" The whole of the East and the whole of the West ,believe in prayer for the departed. . . . Those who have lost those very near and dear to them value this doctrine of the communion of saints and in that doctrine is implied prayer through Jesus Christ for the departed, for rest and peace in Him." The Rector also said " I think as • rule, speaking broadly, we may acknow- ledge that when the East and West agree they are Ir,etetrserelf .he middleadimilong:i of a thiitwhfeunriotrs. Winkle fmnd o election crowd, he asked Mr. Pickwick" What shall we do?"' Shout with the crowd " raid Mr. l'ickwick. " But if there are two crowds?" "Then shout with the larger "said Mr. Pickwick. Evidently that is what the Rector is doing, but after all it is not a very forcyfol argument. Prayers for the dead ere closely connected with the doctrine of Purgatory, but the Church of England declares Purgatory to Ise " a fond thing vainly invented and founded upon no warranty of Scripture, lot ralher repugnant to the Word of Clod." Prayers for the dead are unscrip- torsi. and Wall specicus arguments in favour of ths practice we can affirm that no patriarch, prophet. apostle or saint mentioned in the Scriptures ever offered spa prayer or the dead. The Bible does not coutain one single sentence in favour of prayer for the dead. The Rector stays that the doctrine of the Communion of Saints " implies prayers for the dead." It implies nothing of the sort, but only the fellowship which the whole family of Christ have in Him. Prayers for the dead are rejected by the Prayer Book, they are condemned by the Homilies, they are con- demned by the Reformers, but perhaps this latter would not have much weight with the Rector he should, however, be loyal to the doctrine of his own Church as set forth in the Thirty-nine Articles. Bishop Jewell said ." Praying for the dead is super- stitious and without warrant of tlod'a Word." Arch- bishop Usher said " l'raver is abused when we pray Inc such as tied bath made no promise of, as when men pray for eon's departed." The Church teaches that there are but two places after this life—hearen and hell. In heaven the soul surely needs no prayer.; in bell it is without redemption. In Revelations air. 13 St. John says, " Blessed are the dead which die in the Lord from henceforth ; yea, saith the spirit, that they may rest from their lalxmrs " If the holy dead lire in this state of blessed. ness of what me is it to pray for their souls ? In conclusion, let me, as a Protestant Churchman, warn your readers that prayers for the dead are connected with some of the worst errors of Rome, and it was the teaching of doctrines subversive of the" truth as it is in Jesus " that led on, for conscience sake to sever our connection with the Established Church, and to worship in just the manner to which old-fathioned Churchmen have been accustomed, but a aer-vice of which they ha,' been deprived by Ritualiats and bacerdotalists.—l am, air, yours obediently, WILLIAM 1. JAYE.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church, Reformed Church of England, Bold-went. Warrington. Feb. 21, 1911.</p>
        <p>Sir, —Would you allow me through the medium of your paper,just to remind the Rector that the Church of England has !settled the tpleation of the Sacraments more than 300 years ego. She gays in Article 35: There are two Sacraments ordained of Christ our Lord in the Gospel, that is to say. Baptism and the Supper of the Led. Those live commonly called Sacraments, that is to say, Confirmation, , Penance, Orders,Matrimonv and Extreme Unction, are not to be counted for Sacramental of the Gospel, being such as have grown pertly of the corrupt following of the Apostles, partly ate elates of life allowed in the Scriptures ; but yet have not like nature of Sacraments with !Upbeat am! the Lord's Supper, for that they have not any visible sign or ceremony ordained of God." Will the Rector stand by this judgment u If no, why dose he seek to add those five :which our Church &amp;es not allow) to wake them seven? As a teacher in a Church of England Sunday School I protest against any each teaching. May I also ask is the Church Society becoming a means of propagating Romish teaching in our National Church for, from your report of their meeting I do not find any of its members making any protest against the above ?—Yours SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHER.</p>
      </div>
