        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>C. Vose Wins the Northern Championship. ConiiatUlations to C. Voae, our brilliant loin] runner, upon bin great achievement in winning hi. Northern Cross-country Championship, on Saturday ! 'I he races which were decided at Hayclock Bark, were the first to take place under the new schema by which the cam- petitors are selected in provincial trial races. As far • as the sctual racing was concerned the event was quite , a 'names, but the public nttendanoe wee rather leas than the average of former meetings. The weather was favourable despite the half-sole which blew against the runners is they traversed the nt night. Seven of the North• Eastern counties clubs were unable to meet the expense of their long journey. The juniors' pack mustered shout 110 strong,composed of thirteen tearer and twelve individuals. Breed I Dur- ham (Jarside Slai t wait eytnd Bryant !Preston', three of the individual runners, soon found their we 7 to the head of the held, and fought desperately over every inch of the seven miles' course, and only a few yards separated them et the winning post. Team honours seemed always good for Sscrsd Heart !St. Helens!, who won bye comfortable mnrgin. In the senior race about 100 runners represented twelve teams, and one iodi•idual entry. Scott (Broughton), Witllsce (Bolton), Glower and Cooke Holbrook (Warrington). sod Proctor (Armley and Wortley) were favolirites for first place, but although they took part in a capital struggle over the ten miles' course, the sctual winner proved to he C. Vose, who was entered as one of Wnrrington's reserves. Ile trick the lead before the half-distance wee smelled, wild thenceforward never looked in real danger of defeat. Details JCNIOIt RA, 0. Individunl placing,</p>
