      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Rev. W. Seed and Christian Science.</head>
        <p>Rev. W. Seed and Christian Science.</p>
        <p>Sir—lf lits was correctly reported in your last usage, the flee. W. Lead asked his congregstion to accept his estimate of Christian Science. as he had • tried to read firo. Eddy's book.' Eghipped midi this experience the reverend gentleman appears to have found no difficulty in asserting that Christian Science mu; neither Christian not Science. Unfortunately it eeeins the rule rather that the exception with those who do not on their first introduction to the subject immediately lay hold of some measure of the truth of liristian Science that they should be led into posing as authorities on the subject, and into en indulgence in that least admirable of all exercises of the human</p>
        <p>mind—passing judgment without knowledge. It does not seem to occur to them, all the eloquence of the Bible as to the preciousness and rarity of understand- ing and wisdom notwithstanding, that what sppears foolishness unto thent may be hut the expression of their own want of understanding. And Christian Science is sot a subject to be taken in at a glance. • Its very name ought to protect anyone from imagining such a thing possible, for it would he hard to tind two words of more profound or comprehensive significance than the two great words which constitute it. And when one learns that all true science must necessarily he Christian if it is to be knowledge of the truth, then the irreSytable and eternal fact is recognised that absolide Christian Science is all there is to know. And so no one can possibly criticise Christian Science. The most that can be attempted is to criticise Mrs. Eddy's exposition of it, and the most dint is actually attemred is the criticism o' noes own mistaken conception of that exposition. "Christian Science '—to quote from an article in the issue Of the 7th Jan. of the Christian Science Monitor, the daily newspaper of the Christian Science Church. —" reveals (lid as divine mind; the one great intelli- gence in which man and the universe bane their origin and support. The term Christian Science indicates how this divine Mind applies itself to the needs of men and the universe; for it defines the Christianity lived and taught by Christ Jesus as Science and shows how this Science must in law and order bring Christian living into full expression in human life."— ours truly, • WILLIAM J. BONNIN. • 14, St, Peter's Square, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Salaries, Incomes and Wages</head>
        <p>Salaries, Incomes and Wages</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 was not surprised to find severe eastigatione being administered to our 'roan Councillors upon opening nip last Saturday's " Examiner." For the crime was one that needed the ifrantic punishment of writing a letter to the Nees containing one's opinion of our administrative worthies. For six part I do not Hume them for the crime of looking well after one of their own class and being nigganlly when considering the needs of others. The one thing I regret is that the workingelass do not see to it that their interests are represented directly upon our Town Conned by workingmen. I agree with your corm- pondents no far as they go, lint the peculiar thing is that they, with others, are doing whet one of your roirespontlents accuse the Council of, to wit, swallovr ing the cainel whilst straining at the gnat. For it does seem strange to one who studies econmnies somewhat to find people energetically effusing over the raising of a n,eful public servant's salary. whilst never raising a word of protest against some of the anomalies we find in the way of incomes. For instance, it is estimatml that there are 00,000 persons scheduled as income tax payers who state that they have no occupation—that is. they get an income without working. And the "Daily Mail" Year Book asserts that "about ifith/,000,000 woo taken by aeout 1,250,000 well-to-do men, women and children in 1907." And also in 1907 six men died leaving behind them a total sum of £9,736,009. I wonder if your correspondents think these people's services to the cotninunity justify wealth being poured upon them to such au extent, or if this wealth means that their services are as many times as valuable ao their wealth represents over our Tcwn Clerk's salary. I haves list before me with the names of a few of these people and the number of directorships they held individually. Earl of Chesterfield is a director in IS different concerns. Lord Gifford is a director in 13, Lord St. Davide is a director in 12, and so on through a list of about 311 names holding, per individual, anything from 5 to 15 company directorships which logically brings one to the con- elusion that them, men are either phenomena in the way of intellect or else directorships must be extremely soft jobs. For myself I believe it to he utterly impossible for one person to he so ecquainted with the details of 18 different businesses as to perform anything other than services of a noniinal character. That there are ' such incomes, as unearned ones we have the assurance ' of Mr. Asquith, and that it is not always people of • ability who acquire wealth, we have the assurance of Mr. G. K. Cheaterton. History teems with the lives of want and limitability that some of the geniuses of ' the world have used. I hope most of your readers seriously read the life of Crompton which appeared in your paper some time ago. The Net of the whole matter is that ability in general is not adequately</p>
        <p>remunerated in mope; as our present etnndar'd remuneration says it ought to be. Ability io a commercial commodity bought and exploited like other commoditiei by the owners of capital. The</p>
        <p>ratepayers may have some cause to r complain. hut I would ask some of them to be persistently con- . . . . .</p>
        <p>sistent and weigh this question with tri broad outlook and not become no narrow as to always view Corpora- tion officials' salazies with a microscopic intenaity and overlook evils if far greater maandude.—Youra, LABOR 01INIA VINCET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Women and the McCann Case.</head>
        <p>Women and the McCann Case.</p>
        <p>Sir.—. great deal has teen ape ken and written on the McCann case. Unionista are making party capital out of it, instancing the case as a foretaste of horrors to come if Ireland gets Home Role-----r Rome Rule. Women see in it, riot so much the foretaste of undesirable things to come in Ire- land, as an instance of undesirable things that already exist in the United Kingdom.' Briefly, the case stands thus t—The Pope some little time back ismed the Ne Temere decree, accord- ing to which all mixed marriages, in which one of the contracting parties is a Roman Catholic, are to be considered illegal unless solemnized in a Roman Catholic Church. Mr. and Mrs. McCann are respectively Roman Catholic and Presbyterian. and were married in t. Presbyterian Church. Their marriage seems to have • been happy enough till a priest perseaded the husband that living a life , of sin. Mrs. McCann refused to be married a second time, and the consequence was that her ' babies, one only two months old, were taken away from her. Shortly after, her husband vanished too. This is Mr. Birrell's view of the limitation .--" Mrs. McCann is entitled to all her rewedies sad rights as a married woman." Then Mr. Birrell explains the value of these rights and remedies (not without a relish of the irony of it) :—" But she (Mrs. McCann, has been I deprived of her children by her husband, and by the Linen of this country a husband is entitled to the I custody of his children." " The police naay I be invoked to find a pocket handkerchief • or an umbrella. but to my that they are to be invoked as private detectives to find out the where- abouts of children who were living with their father —their legal guardian—ie a proposition to which I cannot agree." . . .</p>
        <p>The caw. has provoked much diveuseion. Unionists have evinced a quite extraordinavy sympathy for Mrs; McCann, although an equally hard cave, in which the law lately deprived a widow of her children out ct respect to the will of a dead husband, who wanrt Roman Catholic, seemed ithogether to escape ill attention. ttlt,6lr to</p>
        <p>As a further object lesson in the caprice of cl Mr. Davila and Mr. Dillon, far from being tic the spectacle of suffering womanhood, have great commiseration for Mr. McCann, who iv; by circumstances to run away with Mre. . • ..</p>
        <p>babies. They have conjured lip a picture of , misery for which Mn. McCann W. entirely This is silo party game, delightful perhaps who play it. Women, who sometimes serve, in it, and who may have their blackeued for party purposes, aa having their children and belongir for religious purposes, can hardly be revel in it. Is it wonderful that they w: legislation—with the only tcol that can vote ? That they consider the chivalry that !</p>
        <p>ed' brYy lahown forced Cann).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>LI ERPL 'THE PLAINTIFF.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>multitude of sins. The saying " woman stands to lose more than she will gain " by getting the vote," is what Sidney Smith would hove called one of the delights of Noodledom.—Yours Ac., SPECTATOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Walking Day.</head>
        <p>Walking Day.</p>
        <p>Sir.—! see in your Net week's issue that at a meet- :ng convened by the Mayor, it was decided to alter the date of Walking Day to Thursday, June :,•:?nd, (Coronation Day). Now, Walking Day is essentially a Sunday school Festival, and such alteration of the day ought not to he made without giving Sunday school officials an opportunity of expressing their</p>
        <p>• A. meeting called for 4 o'clock in the afternoon does not give them this opportunity, and I would suggest that the Sunday school officials ought to demand another meeting, to be called in the evening, to enable them to be preaent, in order that an arrangement satisfactory to them may be arrived at.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>W. BROWN, (Superintendent Wycliffe Sunday School.) 2 tth February 1111.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Church and Trade.</head>
        <p>Church and Trade.</p>
        <p>Sir,--In your valued issse of Saturday last you state, referring to the licensed trade, that "If it received leas bolstering from both Church and State the way would be easier ; and lam sure I need not appeal to your well-known fairness to allow me a small portion'of your space for a few comments. In the first place it can hardly be said that the church bolsters up the trade. 'I rue she does not indulge in the same unmeasured shun of men engaged in a legitimate business that is, too frequently, the chapel's contribution to temperance reform; hut in her efforts towards genuine improvement she is no !esti earnest because she rather saddles the right horse with the blame and does not elevate the drunkard to the position of a martyr, whilst treating the publican, usually an honest and straight dealing man. as a criminal or worse.</p>
        <p>Turning to the question of the State, what do we find? A monopoly han been created in lieu of freedom of trade, but it was created at the choice of the State and not for the advantage of the trade. The State obtains a large percentage of her revenue from the trade and, in addition, levies large same annually, from the trade itself, to enable her again for her own (113,1,,, to reduce the membeve of thcee engaged in it, still further. . .</p>
        <p>Again, the State's servants have the right of entry how, when, and where they will and the publican may be dragged into the Police Court to answer for the smallest offence, frequently the fault of a third party. He opens when the State says open, and closes when it says cloee, whilst his whale life and character are subject to enquiry before he in permitted to engage in 'business at all. Then. as a climax ton!!, after having invested his money in a perf,tly legitimate trade, it is demanded, generally by political oppanents. that he lie driven forth because • small section of the public is unable, or unwilling, to take its refreshment in a moderate and respectable manner ! . .</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Sir. may I urge that the true remedy is the reform of the public-house, i.n the lines of Lord Latuington's Bill, and the making of the drunkard understand that he is the offender and that he tlil be treated as such.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A FORTIoRI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>February 2211 d, 1911._ _ Lancashire Choir for Empire Festival.</head>
        <p>February 2211 d, 1911._ _ Lancashire Choir for Empire Festival.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I beg a little of your valualile space tonal! 1 attention to a matter which is of public and national j interest. I refer to the Festival of Empire which is Ito take places! the Crystal Palace during the summer. I need not enter into particulars of this, which promisee to be one of the finest and molt interesting exhibitions ever held in this country, and as its object is to draw more closely together the various sections of the British Empire, and to bring the Mother ountry more closely into touch with her colonies. it 'can hardly fail to excite patriotic interest. Now to clne to the point, I wish your kind assistance in making it generally known that in con- nection with this Festival a scheme is being carried into effect, to raise in Lancashire a choir of 4.001) voices to give a concert on the large orchestra at the Crystal Palace on dune 17th. This scheme was Isid before a meeting of officials of the various societies and choirs on Thursday the 11th inst., by Mr. Astley and Mr. Swindell, the chairman and secretary of the Lancashire Festival Choir Committee, under the presidency of the Mayor. At this meeting a reedit- tutu was anionic...3ly passed in favour of joining in the scheme, and at a second meeting held on Thursday the 23rd. inst., further action was taken, and Mr. Crossley was provisionally appointed, and accepted, the position of conductor anti trainer, subject to a sufficiently large Warrington choir being enrolled. A further meeting is to held at Cairo-street school on Monday. March tith, and I should be glad to receive the names and addresees of anyone wi.,hing to join the choir, which is open to all in the town and neighbourh oil having the requisite qualifications. and who are prepared to attend the requisite number of practices, on or before Saturday, March 4th. See advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>exenrsion, and will also hale some chance of seeing something of the preparations for the Coronation,which hike, place the following Thursday. JOHN P. REYNOLDS. (lion. Sec., Pro tem.) Marllield,Grappenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Town and the Rates. Ex-Auditor's Suggestions.</head>
        <p>The Town and the Rates. Ex-Auditor's Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Sir—From what can be gathered there is every pros- pect that thin ratepayere of Warrington are shout to be faced with an increase of the rates in this and sonicl of the following years. It is not, I fear, for many a ' pleasant prospect to face. No one, however, who is conversant with the town can for one moment deny that the cause of the increase, viz. new schools, new bridge., the widening of our main streets, housing mid improved sanitary arrangements, are long overdite and for my own part I trust there will be no halting on the part of the Council to carry cut what is required. The town and its residents will be all the better and the brighter if a vigoroue and progressive policy is actively pursued. Of course improvements cannot be carried out without money. If we call for a tune we must lie prepared to pay the piper. fy object in writing at the moment, which I think is opportune, as the Council are considering "ways and means "is to suggest in all seriousness that they should consider whether it is not wise to call upon some of its Reserve Fund. I underatand they have the power Ito do so. I wish to draw attention ti two some I which stand to the credit of the two following account N. VIZ. : -- Electricity Department, £11,1195 7s. &amp;I.; Tram_ 1 way Deportment, £8.437 Te. 11.1. These amounts stood .o thecredit of the Reserve Fund ou the nlst March, 15111, and pmbably they will further increase. It must he borne in mind that these Reserves have been created after making all allowances for depre- ciation and repayment of Sinking Fund loans. The lelectricity department Ina not cost the ratepayers a single farthing and along with the tramways is a credit to the town.</p>
        <p>What I venture to suggest is that £3,000 should he taken from these two Reserve Funds i.e.. 11,5410 from each. I think at this period in the history of our town the Council would be justified in doing no. The preient generation has borne probably more than its just share of the burden of the past. IVe seek to make insprovernents and should do so, not only for ourselves lint for those who come after is, and the latter will benefit by what we do novr.</p>
        <p>I also take the opportunity of again pointing cut that the Council should also consider the question of merging the Gas Office Department into the Borough Treasurer's Department. It is bound to take place— the sooner the better, for a great saving each year can be affected by carrying this out—to coy nothing of the gain in respect of the public convenience. Having been an Elective Auditor for the past four years has given can a close insight into the town's finances and also its management. I take the view that our undertakings are well managed, and that our town as a whole is well served by its officials and staff.</p>
        <p>May I add that in order to save the town the troulde and expense of a small election I have withdrawn my name as a candidate this year for the position of Elective Auditor. The public are tired of elections.</p>
        <p>I sincerely trust that the Council will take-the.su-g-- creations I have offered in the spirit they are made. for I feel as I know others do. that the burdens of the ratepayers should be made as light as possible co-incident with a progressive policy.—l am. &amp;c. E. ENOLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EMPIRE HALL SKATING RINK.</head>
        <p>EMPIRE HALL SKATING RINK.</p>
        <p>The Empire Skating Rink continue, to be well patronised every evening with enthusiastic roller- skaters, and it ia not surprising. for the Empire Rink owns one of the linen floors in the Kingdom, aml skaters lind it a pleasure to glide over its smooth I surface. The enterpr.sing management are to be congratulated on the excellent mariner in which they cater for their patrons. Every week sees a toll pro- gramme of interesting events. This week has been ino exception to the rule, for each evening the usual , crowd of expert and otherwise) tinkers have thronged I the floor, and competitions have been arranged for their benefit. On Wednesday evening the ever- popular weekly hockey match was played, Frodsham supplying the opposition, but although the visitors put op. stout resistance they were eventually over-played Try the faster and cleverer Empire team, and • the hornesters ran ...it winners by The competi- tion night, which takes place each week, in proving a very popular feature at the rink. Two arousing competitions had been arranged for this week. one Ewing a gentlemen's backward race. After an atnuning and close contest Mr. S. Davies proved to be the winner. Another competition which was productive cf a good deal of amusement was the ladies' candle race. bliss G. Rotherham wan success- ful in carrying off the price.</p>
      </div>
